, and puts the number away somewhere. \\"Go on, then,\\" Sera says.","choices":[{"label":"Where did you learn the trade?","conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_the_trade"}}],"next":"camp_sera_the_trade"},{"label":"What is this camp worth?","conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_prices"}}],"next":"camp_sera_prices"},{"label":"Aldric says you lie about small things.","conditions":[{"companion":"sera","approval_at_least":15},{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_small_lies"}}],"next":"camp_sera_small_lies"},{"label":"The book in the loft. What is actually written in it?","conditions":[{"companion":"sera","approval_at_least":40},{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_the_hand"}}],"next":"camp_sera_the_hand"},{"label":"The dockets with the reeve''s stamp on them.","conditions":[{"flag":"grain_stamp_seen"},{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_dockets"}}],"next":"camp_sera_dockets"},{"label":"The leash. Now that it''s decided.","conditions":[{"flag":"sera_leash_fate"},{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_fate_told"}}],"next":"camp_sera_fate"},{"label":"The tablet''s been blank a while now.","conditions":[{"companion":"sera","approval_at_least":30},{"not":{"companion":"sera","romance_stage":2}},{"not":{"companion":"sera","romance_locked":true}}],"next":"sera_close"},{"label":"Read me the ninth line.","conditions":[{"companion":"sera","romance_stage":2}],"next":"romance_after"},{"label":"Goodnight, Sera.","end":true},{"label":"What will you do when this is over?","conditions":[{"quest":"sera_leash","status":"completed"},{"companion":"sera","approval_at_least":15},{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_after"}}],"next":"camp_sera_after"},{"label":"There was a letter for you at the Flagon.","conditions":[{"flag":"camp_sera_after"},{"level_at_least":4},{"companion":"sera","approval_at_least":15},{"not":{"flag":"sera_post_fate"}}],"next":"camp_sera_the_letter"},{"label":"Tell me about your father. Not the entries. The man.","conditions":[{"flag":"camp_sera_the_trade"},{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_father"}}],"next":"camp_sera_father"},{"label":"Take the evening off. There is nothing for you to do.","conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_nothing_to_do"}}],"next":"camp_sera_nothing_to_do"},{"label":"I have used you.","conditions":[{"flag":"camp_sera_prices"},{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_used"}}],"next":"camp_sera_used"},{"label":"Tell me one true thing, and I will do nothing whatever with it.","conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_one_true_thing"}}],"next":"camp_sera_one_true_thing"},{"label":"Nobody spells your name right.","conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_name"}}],"next":"camp_sera_name"},{"label":"If this goes wrong, what happens to you?","conditions":[{"flag":"camp_sera_the_hand"},{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_worst_case"}}],"next":"camp_sera_worst_case"}]},{"text":"\\"If it is a job, say the job,\\" Sera says. She is at the edge of the firelight with the tablet, and she does not put it down, and she counts the exits behind you while you speak, which she does not think you can see her doing.","choices":[{"label":"Where did you learn the trade?","conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_the_trade"}}],"next":"camp_sera_the_trade"},{"label":"What is this camp worth?","conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_prices"}}],"next":"camp_sera_prices"},{"label":"Aldric says you lie about small things.","conditions":[{"companion":"sera","approval_at_least":15},{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_small_lies"}}],"next":"camp_sera_small_lies"},{"label":"The book in the loft. What is actually written in it?","conditions":[{"companion":"sera","approval_at_least":40},{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_the_hand"}}],"next":"camp_sera_the_hand"},{"label":"The dockets with the reeve''s stamp on them.","conditions":[{"flag":"grain_stamp_seen"},{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_dockets"}}],"next":"camp_sera_dockets"},{"label":"The leash. Now that it''s decided.","conditions":[{"flag":"sera_leash_fate"},{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_fate_told"}}],"next":"camp_sera_fate"},{"label":"The tablet''s been blank a while now.","conditions":[{"companion":"sera","approval_at_least":30},{"not":{"companion":"sera","romance_stage":2}},{"not":{"companion":"sera","romance_locked":true}}],"next":"sera_close"},{"label":"Read me the ninth line.","conditions":[{"companion":"sera","romance_stage":2}],"next":"romance_after"},{"label":"Goodnight, Sera.","end":true},{"label":"What will you do when this is over?","conditions":[{"quest":"sera_leash","status":"completed"},{"companion":"sera","approval_at_least":15},{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_after"}}],"next":"camp_sera_after"},{"label":"There was a letter for you at the Flagon.","conditions":[{"flag":"camp_sera_after"},{"level_at_least":4},{"companion":"sera","approval_at_least":15},{"not":{"flag":"sera_post_fate"}}],"next":"camp_sera_the_letter"},{"label":"Tell me about your father. Not the entries. The man.","conditions":[{"flag":"camp_sera_the_trade"},{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_father"}}],"next":"camp_sera_father"},{"label":"Take the evening off. There is nothing for you to do.","conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_nothing_to_do"}}],"next":"camp_sera_nothing_to_do"},{"label":"I have used you.","conditions":[{"flag":"camp_sera_prices"},{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_used"}}],"next":"camp_sera_used"},{"label":"Tell me one true thing, and I will do nothing whatever with it.","conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_one_true_thing"}}],"next":"camp_sera_one_true_thing"},{"label":"Nobody spells your name right.","conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_name"}}],"next":"camp_sera_name"},{"label":"If this goes wrong, what happens to you?","conditions":[{"flag":"camp_sera_the_hand"},{"not":{"flag":"camp_sera_worst_case"}}],"next":"camp_sera_worst_case"}]}],"camp_sera_the_trade":[{"text":"\\"My father kept the mill''s books twenty-two years and I kept them with him from nine years old.\\" Sera does not look up from the tablet. \\"You learn it as a rhythm before you learn it as arithmetic. Cart in, weight, moisture, deduction, price, name. Six things, forty times a day. If you can do six things forty times a day without your hand getting bored, you will never be hungry in a market town.\\" She turns the stylus over. \\"That is the trade. Everybody thinks a clerk is clever. A clerk is somebody who does not get bored.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_sera_the_trade"}],"choices":[{"label":"You were nine.","next":"camp"},{"label":"You are the cleverest person at this fire and you keep saying you are not.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":3}}],"next":"camp_sera_trade_clever"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_trade_clever":[{"text":"\\"Don''t,\\" Sera says, pleasantly. \\"Clever gets weighed. Dull gets left alone. I have been left alone for two years by a man who has had four clever people arrested, and I would like that run to continue.\\" She writes something on the tablet. It is not a word.","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_prices":[{"text":"\\"This camp.\\" Sera looks up for about two seconds. \\"Your boots, sixteen silver, resoled once and badly. The half-orc''s axe would fetch eleven gold from a smith and four from a fence, and she would take the four to avoid the conversation. The cleric owns nothing, which is either very restful or very expensive depending on who is feeding him. The warden''s cloak I cannot price, because nobody sells them, and the last one I saw was on a body the watch brought down off the cut.\\" Back to the tablet. \\"You asked. People always ask, and they never like the boots.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_sera_prices"}],"choices":[{"label":"And you?","next":"camp_sera_prices_herself"},{"label":"Do you do that in every room you walk into?","next":"camp"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_prices_herself":[{"text":"\\"Forty gold and a horse, if you sold me back to Marrow before the assize,\\" Sera says, with no change in her voice whatever. \\"Sixty if you waited until he needed the books done. I have kept that figure current for two years. It is the only number I have never once had to look up.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_small_lies":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"camp_sera_small_lies"}],"text":"\\"Bread,\\" Sera says. \\"No apple. We have done this one and you did not enjoy it as much as you expected to.\\"","choices":[{"label":"No.","next":"camp"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]},{"text":"\\"He is right,\\" Sera says. \\"He is right, and he said it to my face, which I liked, and I am not going to stop.\\" She sets the tablet down. \\"Try this. Ask me what I had for breakfast.\\" You ask. \\"Bread and an apple,\\" she says at once, and then: \\"That was a lie. It was bread. Nothing turns on it, nobody is harmed, and you now hold one piece of information about me that is wrong. If you ever come to sell what you know about me, it is worth a little less.\\" She picks the tablet back up. \\"Do that four times a day for eleven years and nobody anywhere has a clean picture of you. It is the cheapest armour there is and it fits everyone.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_sera_small_lies"}],"choices":[{"label":"Keep doing it. Just not about the things that matter.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":4}}],"next":"camp_sera_lies_allowed"},{"label":"Then I cannot trust a word out of you.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":-3}}],"next":"camp_sera_lies_distrust"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_lies_allowed":[{"text":"Sera looks at you slightly too long, which is what she does instead of being caught. \\"That is an arrangement I have with exactly nobody,\\" she says. \\"All right. Not the things that matter. Ask me about breakfast at your own risk.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_lies_distrust":[{"text":"\\"No,\\" Sera agrees. \\"That is the correct conclusion, and I would rather you reached it here than in a loft.\\" She goes back to the tablet, and does not sit near you again that night, and is perfectly civil about all of it.","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_the_hand":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"camp_sera_the_hand"}],"text":"\\"Eleven entries in my father''s hand, copied out in mine,\\" Sera says. \\"You have had it. I am not going to get better at saying it by practising.\\"","choices":[{"label":"You do not have to.","next":"camp"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]},{"text":"Sera is quiet long enough that you think she is going to charge you for it. \\"It is not a confession. It is a copy.\\" She turns the stylus over and over. \\"Everything false in the mill''s books, written out fair — you have had that much from me. What I have not said is where the false entries start. They start the spring before my father died. Eleven of them are his. His hand, his ink, his initials. He was not clever and he was not brave and Marrow had him exactly the way he has had me, and it took two years off the end of him.\\" Her voice does not move at all. \\"So when Marrow told me to make the fair copy, I copied his eleven out in my own hand as well. Dated them. Initialled them. Mine. There is no page anywhere in Rivermark with my father''s hand on a false entry, because I sat up four nights and made sure of it.\\" A pause. \\"That is what is in the loft. He thinks he holds a rope on me. What he actually holds is the only thing I have ever done on purpose.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_sera_the_hand"}],"interjections":[{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric does not say anything at all. He puts wood on the fire and stays up, and is still there when she goes to sleep."},{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse: \\"You wrote yourself into it to keep a dead man out of it.\\" Sera: \\"He was not dead yet. That was the reason for doing it quickly.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Then we burn it, and nobody ever reads either hand.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":5}}],"next":"camp_sera_hand_burn"},{"label":"You have hanged yourself for eleven entries.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":-3}}],"next":"camp_sera_hand_cold"},{"label":"It is a headstone with your name on it, not a rope.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_hand_burn":[{"text":"\\"Yes,\\" Sera says. \\"That is what I want, and I have not managed to say it in a whole sentence until this minute, so thank you, and I would like to stop talking now.\\" She does. She stays at the fire, though, which she does not usually do.","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_hand_cold":[{"text":"\\"Eleven entries, and a man who taught me the six things,\\" Sera says. \\"You are quite right that the arithmetic is bad. I have been over it a great many more times than you have.\\" She closes the tablet. \\"Goodnight.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_dockets":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"camp_sera_dockets"}],"text":"\\"Nobody counts rubbish,\\" Sera says. \\"That is my entire contribution to this and I have already made it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"It was a good one.","next":"camp"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]},{"text":"\\"Eighteen sacks, third one this month, and the stamp is a reeve''s and not a factor''s.\\" Sera is pleased, in the way she gets when numbers do something. \\"He is not stealing grain. He buys it forward at a price, contracts it out at a better one, and then does not deliver it — and a sack that never arrives still has to go somewhere, so it goes into a cellar or a ditch to rot. The docket that proves it existed goes in the waste-basket. The waste-basket is the only honest book in Rivermark.\\" She taps the tablet. \\"Three dockets a month for two years, and nobody has ever counted them, because nobody counts rubbish.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_sera_dockets"}],"choices":[{"label":"Then we count them.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":3}}],"next":"camp_sera_dockets_count"},{"label":"Rotted grain is not a hanging matter.","next":"camp"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_dockets_count":[{"text":"\\"I have,\\" Sera says. \\"Obviously I have. Seventy-one, and I can tell you which weeks the road was shut by which ones are missing.\\" She almost smiles, and stops it. \\"You are the first person who has said we about it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"she_goes_downriver":[{"text":"\\"Then I will make it,\\" Sera says. \\"You asked me what I was for and then you let me answer it, and nobody has ever done the second half of that.\\" She picks up the satchel. \\"I have left you the dockets. Seventy-one of them, counted, with the weeks the road was shut marked in the margin, and a note on the front telling you which four matter. Do not lose it. I am not doing it again for free.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Go and be bored somewhere warm.","end":true}]}],"after_she_stayed":[{"text":"\\"I could,\\" Sera agrees. \\"Forty gold, a bond, and nobody in the building with a hold on me. That is not a consolation prize, it is the best offer I have had in my life and I have turned it down for a party that sleeps in fields.\\" She squares the tablet on her knee. \\"I am not going to pretend that was sentiment. You asked, and nobody has asked. It turns out that is worth about forty gold a year to me, and I have been extremely surprised by the figure.\\"","choices":[{"label":"It''s worth more than that.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":5}}],"end":true},{"label":"Nothing.","end":true}]}],"the_first_account":[{"text":"She reads it out. Eight lines: where the party has been, in order, with dates; what it was paid, to the silver; and four names, one of which is Aldric''s and one of which is the innkeeper''s. It is scrupulously accurate and there is nothing whatever in it that could not be got from the taproom in an afternoon. \\"That is the trick,\\" Sera says, folding it. \\"An account that is entirely true and entirely useless is much harder to write than a lie, and they will pay me for it for about a year before anybody senior enough reads two of them side by side.\\"","choices":[{"label":"And when they do?","next":"when_they_do"},{"label":"Read me the next one too.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":4}}],"end":true}]}],"when_they_do":[{"text":"\\"Then they will stop paying me and write to somebody else in this valley instead,\\" Sera says, \\"and I will find out who, because a man who has just started being paid buys a coat.\\" She puts the account in her satchel. \\"That is why I took it. Not the eight gold. I wanted to be on the inside of somebody''s post.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Buy a coat yourself and see who notices.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":3}}],"end":true},{"label":"Nothing.","end":true}]}],"camp_sera_after":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"camp_sera_after"}],"text":"\\"I have not thought of an answer since you asked,\\" Sera says. \\"I have thought of four more things to do this week, which I notice is not the same.\\"","choices":[{"label":"It is not the same.","next":"camp_sera_necessary"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]},{"on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_sera_after"}],"text":"Sera does not answer for long enough that the fire moves. \\"That is a very expensive question and you have asked it for nothing,\\" she says at last. \\"Forty gold and a horse. That was the figure — what I was worth, sold back, before the assize. I kept it current for two years and it was the only number I never had to look up.\\" She turns the stylus over. \\"Nobody is buying. I worked that out about a fortnight after the loft and I have not had a use for the figure since, and I would like to tell you that felt like being let out of something.\\"","choices":[{"label":"And it did not.","next":"camp_sera_necessary"},{"label":"You are free. That is what free is.","next":"camp_sera_free"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_free":[{"text":"\\"Yes,\\" Sera says, pleasantly. \\"That is the correct word and I have been using it at myself for a fortnight.\\" She sets the tablet down, which she does not do. \\"Would you like to know what I have actually done with the fortnight? I have priced this camp twice. I have counted the dockets again. I did the innkeeper''s tally for him on Thursday and did not tell him, because he would have had to say thank you and then we would both have had a job to do about it.\\" She looks at the fire. \\"That is not what a free person does. That is what somebody does who has worked out that a person who is not necessary can be left behind, and who has never in her life been kept for any other reason, and who is not going to find out this month whether that is true here.\\"","choices":[{"label":"You are not being kept for the arithmetic.","next":"camp_sera_not_the_arithmetic"},{"label":"Then find out. Ask.","next":"camp_sera_asking"},{"label":"Let it be, tonight.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_necessary":[{"text":"\\"It is not,\\" Sera agrees. \\"I have priced this camp twice this fortnight. I counted the dockets again, which did not need counting. I did the innkeeper''s tally for him on Thursday and did not tell him.\\" The stylus goes still. \\"I know exactly what that is. A person who is not necessary can be left behind, and I have never once been kept for any other reason, and I would rather be the most useful thing at a fire than find out whether that is still the arrangement.\\"","choices":[{"label":"You are not being kept for the arithmetic.","next":"camp_sera_not_the_arithmetic"},{"label":"Then find out. Ask.","next":"camp_sera_asking"},{"label":"Let it be, tonight.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_not_the_arithmetic":[{"text":"\\"You would say that,\\" Sera says. \\"You would say it whether it was true or not, because it is the kind thing and you are, on the whole, kind.\\" She picks the tablet back up. \\"I am not being difficult. I am telling you that I have no way of testing it, and that I am a person who tests everything, and that this is the one thing I have ever had to take on somebody''s word.\\" A pause. \\"I am taking it. I want that noted. I am simply going to go on counting things as well.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Noted.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":5}}],"next":"camp"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_asking":[{"text":"\\"Ask,\\" Sera repeats, as though the word were in a foreign hand. \\"Yes. That is the obvious thing and I have known it was the obvious thing for a fortnight.\\" She turns the stylus over twice. \\"I will tell you what happens when I ask. I get an accurate answer from somebody who has worked out what I want to hear, because that is what I would do, and then I am worse off than before because now I cannot trust the one thing I did have.\\" She almost smiles. \\"So: no. I am going to be useful, and you are going to keep me, and if that is for the arithmetic then at least the arithmetic is sound.\\"","choices":[{"label":"One day somebody is going to make you an offer you cannot cost.","next":"camp_sera_the_offer_ahead"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_the_offer_ahead":[{"text":"\\"Then I shall be extremely rude to them,\\" Sera says, and picks the tablet up, and writes a line on it that is not a word. \\"Goodnight.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Goodnight, Sera.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_the_letter":[{"conditions":[{"quest":"sera_what_she_is_for"},{"not":{"flag":"sera_post_fate"}}],"text":"\\"The letter is where it was and it says what it said,\\" Sera says. \\"I am not going to read it to you a fourth time at a fire.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Then let''s decide it.","next":"the_offer_choice"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]},{"on_enter":[{"start_quest":"sera_what_she_is_for"}],"text":"\\"There was a letter for me at the Flagon,\\" Sera says, in the voice she uses for the north field tally. \\"Not for the party. For me, by name, at an inn I have slept at eleven times.\\" She has it out already. \\"Somebody has bought the mill''s paper out of the wreck. In among it were seventy-one delivery dockets out of a waste-basket, counted, in a fair hand, with the weeks the road was shut marked in the margin.\\" The stylus goes still. \\"They do not want the dockets. They have written to ask for the hand.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Read it to me.","next":"the_offer_read"},{"label":"Who signed it?","next":"the_offer_seal"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]}],"the_offer_read":[{"text":"\\"Clerk of accounts, at the landing at Greyhythe. Forty gold a year, a room over the counting-floor, a bond of twenty against my honesty which is returned at the year''s end, and one line at the bottom that I have read about forty times.\\" She reads it flat, as she reads everything. \\"Neither the house nor any officer of it shall require of the clerk any account, entry or copy other than the true one.\\" She folds it along one crease and stops. \\"That is not a kindness. That is a clause. Somebody has been sued over that and put it in the form, and it is still the best sentence anybody has ever written to me.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Who signed it?","next":"the_offer_seal"},{"label":"Then we should decide it.","next":"the_offer_choice"},{"label":"Not tonight.","end":true}]}],"the_offer_seal":[{"text":"\\"Nobody signed it. It is a house hand and a house form.\\" Sera turns it over. \\"There is a seal. Black wax, and it is not a merchant''s mark and it is not a guild''s — a plumb and a square, cut small, like a mason''s, which is a strange thing for a counting-house to put on its post.\\" She looks at it for a moment longer than she needs to and then puts it down. \\"I have seen it before. I could not tell you where, which is not a sentence I have ever had to say.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Think about where.","next":"the_offer_seal_where"},{"label":"Then we should decide it.","next":"the_offer_choice"},{"label":"Not tonight.","end":true}]}],"the_offer_seal_where":[{"text":"She does think about it, properly, for a good while, and comes back with nothing, which visibly annoys her more than the letter does. \\"It will be a docket,\\" she says. \\"It is always a docket. I have read about nine thousand of them and one of them had that on it and I did not look twice because nobody looks twice at wax.\\" She squares the letter to the edge of the tablet. \\"Ask me again in a month. I will have it by then and it will be a Tuesday and I will be furious.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Then we should decide the letter.","next":"the_offer_choice"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]}],"the_offer_choice":[{"text":"\\"I will put the three of them out and you will say one,\\" Sera says. \\"I have done this with contracts for eleven years and I am going to do it with this because it is the only way I know how.\\" She sets three fingers on the table. \\"One. I go. Forty a year, my own name on the door of the room, and a clause that says nobody may ask me for a false entry.\\" Two. \\"I stay, and you pay me what the post pays, because I am not going to be the person at this fire who works for board and then resents it in four years — sixty gold, in writing, and you will find that cheap.\\" Three. \\"I write back and take a retainer and stay. They get eight lines on the first of the month about where we have been. You would get to read every one before it goes and I would not send one you had not read.\\" She sits back. \\"Those are the three. There is no fourth and I have looked.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Take the post, Sera. Go.","effects":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"sera_what_she_is_for","stage":"she_took_the_post"}}],"next":"she_takes_it"},{"label":"Sixty gold, in writing. Stay.","conditions":[{"gold_at_least":60}],"effects":[{"take_gold":60},{"set_flag":"sera_paid_properly"},{"quest_stage":{"quest":"sera_what_she_is_for","stage":"she_stayed"}}],"next":"she_stays_paid"},{"label":"Stay. I have no sixty gold and I am asking anyway.","effects":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"sera_what_she_is_for","stage":"she_stayed"}}],"next":"she_stays_asked"},{"label":"Take the retainer. Stay, and read me every line.","effects":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"sera_what_she_is_for","stage":"she_took_the_retainer"}}],"next":"she_takes_the_retainer"},{"label":"I need to think about it.","end":true}]}],"she_takes_it":[{"text":"Sera does not move for a moment. Then she says, \\"Right,\\" and picks the letter up, and folds it the rest of the way, and puts it inside her coat, and none of it takes as long as it should. \\"You could have said the second one,\\" she says. \\"I costed it. It was cheaper for you than the third one and you did not say it.\\" She stands. \\"You said the one that is best for me. I am going to be some time getting over that and I am not going to do it in front of you.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Go on, then.","end":true}]}],"she_stays_paid":[{"text":"She writes the terms herself, on the back of the letter, in the fair hand, and makes you sign both copies. Sixty gold a year, paid quarterly, and a line she adds at the end without discussion: that either party may end it at a quarter''s notice and nobody is to make a scene about it. \\"There,\\" Sera says, and blots it. \\"Now I am the most expensive thing in this party and everybody will treat me accordingly, and I have wanted that since I was nineteen.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Worth every gold of it.","end":true}]}],"she_stays_asked":[{"text":"\\"You have not got sixty gold,\\" Sera says. \\"I know exactly what you have got, to the silver, and it is not sixty.\\" She looks at the letter, and then at the fire, and then at nothing in particular for rather a long time. \\"And you asked anyway, out loud, in front of a fire, having nothing to put behind it.\\" She picks the letter up and puts it in the coals herself and watches the whole of it go, the way she watched the loft book. \\"That is the first offer I have ever taken that I could not cost. Do not ask me how I feel about it. I have no idea and I am going to find out slowly.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Slowly is fine.","end":true}]}],"she_takes_the_retainer":[{"text":"\\"Then I write back,\\" Sera says, and does it that evening, and shows you the draft before it is sealed because that is the arrangement now and she says the arrangement starts immediately or not at all. \\"They will pay eight gold a month for eight lines a month,\\" she says, \\"which is the best rate I have ever been quoted for anything, and I have costed it, and I cannot make it come out as anything except somebody paying to have a person in this party.\\" She seals it. \\"So they shall have one. And she will be mine.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Read me every line.","end":true}]}],"camp_sera_father":[{"text":"\\"That is not a costed question,\\" Sera says, which is what she says when she has decided to answer one. The stylus goes over and over.\\n\\n\\"He sang the weights. Twenty-two years, six days a week, in a yard with forty carts a day through it, and he sang the deduction column, because it scans. Damp two, dirt one, short weight three. It is a very stupid little tune and it fitted the work exactly.\\" She writes something that is not a word. \\"He was not clever. He was not brave. He could do six things forty times a day without his hand getting bored, and he taught me to do it before I could reach the desk.\\"\\n\\nA pause.\\n\\n\\"I have not been able to do a deduction column in silence since. I do it in my head with the tune under it, and I have never once told anybody that.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_sera_father"}],"choices":[{"label":"Sing it.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":7}}],"next":"camp_sera_father_sing"},{"label":"He taught you the six things.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":4}}],"next":"camp_sera_father_six"},{"label":"He also signed eleven false entries.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":-3}}],"next":"camp_sera_father_eleven"}]}],"camp_sera_father_sing":[{"text":"\\"No,\\" Sera says.\\n\\nAnd then, four minutes later, when the conversation has been allowed to go somewhere else and the fire has been built up and nobody is looking at her, she does. Once, badly, at about a quarter of the volume a person sings at.\\n\\nDamp two, dirt one, short weight three.\\n\\n\\"That is the whole of it,\\" she says. \\"It is not a good tune. Do not ask me again.\\" She picks the tablet back up, and she is not writing anything on it.","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_father_six":[{"text":"\\"He did,\\" Sera says. \\"He taught me the six things, and I have made a great deal more of them than he ever did, and I have been perfectly clear with myself about which of us was the better man at it.\\" The stylus turns over. \\"Thank you for saying taught. People say used.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_father_eleven":[{"text":"\\"He did,\\" Sera says, with no change in her voice at all. \\"Eleven. In the spring before he died, in his own hand, with a man standing over him.\\" She closes the tablet. \\"I have that fact. I have had it two years, and I wrote it out fair in my own hand precisely so that nobody else would ever have to have it.\\"\\n\\nA pause.\\n\\n\\"You have had it four minutes and you have spent it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_nothing_to_do":[{"text":"\\"Certainly,\\" Sera says, and puts the tablet down, and folds her hands.\\n\\nFour minutes later she has re-stacked the firewood by length. Six minutes after that she has the strap off the pack that has been fraying for a week and is doing it herself, badly, because it is not her trade.\\n\\nShe catches you watching.\\n\\n\\"Yes,\\" she says. \\"I am aware. I got to about ninety seconds and then I began pricing the wood.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_sera_nothing_to_do"}],"choices":[{"label":"Then sit here and be no use whatever to anybody, for one hour.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":6}}],"next":"camp_sera_nothing_hour"},{"label":"Suit yourself.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":-2}}],"next":"camp_sera_nothing_shrug"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_nothing_hour":[{"text":"She does it. She sits with her hands empty and looks at the fire and does not touch the tablet, and it is one of the more uncomfortable hours anybody at that camp has spent.\\n\\nAt the end of it she says: \\"Fifty-eight minutes. I counted, because of course I counted.\\" She picks the tablet up. \\"I should like to do it again tomorrow. I would like that noted, and I would rather not discuss it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_nothing_shrug":[{"text":"\\"I shall,\\" Sera says, pleasantly, and finishes the strap, and does the innkeeper''s tally in her head for the practice, and is the last person at the fire by an hour and a half.","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_used":[{"text":"Sera does not look up, and does not pretend to have to think about it.\\n\\n\\"Three times,\\" she says. \\"The loft, obviously — you wanted a hand that could copy and I was the hand. The gate on the Ford road, where you put me in front of a serjeant because a clerk in a clean coat gets a different answer than an armed party does. And the fourth day out of Rivermark, when you did not care to count the coin yourself.\\"\\n\\nShe sets the stylus down.\\n\\n\\"They are dated. I have been waiting a considerable while to find out whether you were going to say it, or whether I was going to have to.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_sera_used"}],"choices":[{"label":"All three. And I would do the gate again.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":6}}],"next":"camp_sera_used_again"},{"label":"That is not what those were.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":-3}}],"next":"camp_sera_used_denied"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_used_again":[{"text":"\\"You would,\\" Sera says. \\"So would I. It worked, nobody was hurt, and I was extremely good at it.\\"\\n\\nShe picks the stylus back up and turns it over twice.\\n\\n\\"Here is the difference, and it is the only one I have ever cared about. Marrow used me and told me it was a kindness. You have used me and told me it was using me.\\" A pause. \\"I have no idea what to do with that. I shall go on counting things while I work it out.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_used_denied":[{"text":"\\"It is exactly what those were,\\" Sera says, still perfectly civil. \\"I do not mind them. I minded the loft, and I have said so, and it is finished.\\"\\n\\nShe closes the tablet.\\n\\n\\"What I mind is being told that a thing I have written down, dated, in a fair hand, did not happen. That is the one thing in the world I cannot argue with anybody about, because if my count is wrong then I have nothing at all.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_one_true_thing":[{"text":"\\"That is not how information works,\\" Sera says.\\n\\n\\"I know.\\"\\n\\n\\"...You do not. Go on, then.\\" She looks at the fire for a while, and when she speaks it is not in the tally voice. \\"I am afraid of the dark. Not of anything in it — of the dark, which is what a child is afraid of, and I was not, and then I spent two hours in a loft with the hatch shut, and now I am.\\"\\n\\nThe stylus does not move.\\n\\n\\"There is no arithmetic in that anywhere. It is worth nothing to anybody, it cannot be sold, and I have just handed it to you.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_sera_one_true_thing"}],"choices":[{"label":"Then here is one back, and it is worth nothing either.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":6}}],"next":"camp_sera_true_back"},{"label":"That is worth something.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":-3}}],"next":"camp_sera_true_priced"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_true_back":[{"text":"You tell her one. It is small and it is stupid and it is true, and there is nothing whatever in it that anybody could use.\\n\\nSera listens the entire way through without once looking at the tablet.\\n\\n\\"Right,\\" she says at the end of it. \\"Now we each have one, and neither is worth anything, which is the point of the exercise — and I have understood it at roughly the speed of a slow child.\\" She very nearly smiles. \\"Do not tell me another tonight. I shall want to write it down, and then we are back where we started.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_true_priced":[{"text":"\\"It is,\\" Sera agrees, and something behind her face shuts very tidily. \\"About four silver, to a man who wanted me somewhere with a lamp. Thank you — that is a useful correction and I shall not need it twice.\\"\\n\\nShe picks the tablet back up and is perfectly pleasant for the rest of the evening.","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_name":[{"text":"The stylus stops.\\n\\n\\"Vance. With an e.\\" She says it the way other people say a password. \\"It is Vans on every docket in Rivermark, in three different hands, going back before I was born, because the first clerk who wrote it down did not ask and every one since has copied the man in front of him.\\" She turns the tablet over. \\"My father''s was Vans on his indenture, on his burial fee, and on the paper the mill kept his wages on for twenty-two years.\\"\\n\\n\\"I corrected it once. I was eleven. The man laughed and said it did not signify.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_sera_name"}],"choices":[{"label":"It signifies. I will spell it right on everything I ever write.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":5}}],"next":"camp_sera_name_kept"},{"label":"It is one letter.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":-2}}],"next":"camp_sera_name_letter"},{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_name_kept":[{"text":"\\"Everything you ever write,\\" Sera repeats, and audits the sentence for about two seconds, the way she audits everything. \\"That is a very great deal of paper for one letter.\\"\\n\\nShe writes something on the tablet. It is, for once, a word.\\n\\n\\"Yes. All right. Thank you.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_name_letter":[{"text":"\\"It is one letter,\\" Sera agrees. \\"It is also the only part of a person that appears on every piece of paper they are ever on, and I have spent my whole working life being the one who gets it right for other people.\\" She goes back to the tablet. \\"It does not signify. You are quite correct. It has simply never once been spelled properly in four generations.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_worst_case":[{"text":"\\"I have costed it,\\" Sera says at once, which is how you know she has costed it more than once. \\"The assize sits at Greyhythe twice a year. Eleven false entries in my own hand, dated and initialled, is not a fine. It is the pillory and then seven years at the works, and I am told nobody does seven.\\"\\n\\nShe writes nothing on the tablet with a great deal of attention.\\n\\n\\"The rest of you would be witnesses. That is not a complaint either; it is what the paper says. Everybody at this fire can stand up and be believed. I am the only one who is already written down.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_sera_worst_case"}],"choices":[{"label":"Then it does not go wrong.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":-2}}],"next":"camp_sera_worst_promise"},{"label":"Then I stand up in the same room and say I told you to.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":6}}],"next":"camp_sera_worst_stand"},{"label":"Say the number of years again.","effects":[{"approval":{"sera":3}}],"next":"camp_sera_worst_years"}]}],"camp_sera_worst_promise":[{"text":"\\"You cannot price that,\\" Sera says, \\"and neither can I, which is exactly why it is not a comfort. It is a very kind sentence with nothing behind it.\\"\\n\\nShe closes the tablet.\\n\\n\\"I would rather have the true one, and the true one is that it might. Goodnight.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_worst_stand":[{"text":"Sera stops writing.\\n\\n\\"That is perjury,\\" she says. \\"You understand that it is perjury. Same court, same assize. You would go to the works with me, and it would not even help, because a party leader who says he told his clerk to falsify a mill''s books is a man confessing to the larger half of it.\\"\\n\\n\\"Yes.\\"\\n\\n\\"...Yes.\\" She puts the stylus down flat, which she does not do. \\"Nobody has ever offered me anything that stupid. I am going to decline it, and I am going to go on declining it, and I should like it left standing where I can see it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"camp_sera_worst_years":[{"text":"\\"Seven,\\" Sera says. \\"At the works. I said it once already and you have made me say it twice, and I notice it was easier the second time, which is the sort of thing I would rather not have found out about myself in front of a fire.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"camp"}]}],"sera_return_need":[{"text":"\\"Yes,\\" Sera says. \\"That is generally the shape of it.\\" She does not stop writing. \\"I have been needed by three separate men in this town. All three were entirely sincere about it and I have the entries.\\"\\n\\nThe stylus goes over.\\n\\n\\"Try again, and this time do not open with what I am for.\\"","choices":[{"label":"You are the best clerk in the county.","next":"sera_return_flattery"},{"label":"I have nothing for you to do.","next":"sera_return_nothing"},{"label":"Leave her to it.","end":true}]}],"sera_return_flattery":[{"text":"\\"I am the best clerk in the county,\\" Sera agrees. \\"I was the best clerk in the county in a loft with a hatch over me, and it did not signify then either.\\"\\n\\nShe finishes a column and rules a line under it.\\n\\n\\"Being good at a thing is why people keep me. It has never once been why anybody came back for me. Again.\\"","choices":[{"label":"We need you.","next":"sera_return_need"},{"label":"I have nothing for you to do.","next":"sera_return_nothing"},{"label":"Leave her to it.","end":true}]}],"sera_return_nothing":[{"text":"The stylus stops.\\n\\n\\"Say that again,\\" Sera says.\\n\\nSo you do. That the party is going up the Ford road with a warden and a cleric and no books in it anywhere, and there is not one thing on the way that wants a clerk.\\n\\nShe does not write anything for what is, in her, a very long time.\\n\\n\\"Then why are you standing in a fish market,\\" she says.","choices":[{"label":"Answer her.","next":"sera_return_why"}]}],"sera_return_why":[{"text":"It is not rhetorical. The stylus is down, and she is waiting, and she will keep waiting, and she will price whatever comes out of your mouth to the copper.","choices":[{"label":"Because a fire with you at it is better, and I cannot show you the arithmetic.","next":"sera_return_why_true"},{"label":"Because you are one of us.","next":"sera_return_why_group"},{"label":"Leave her to it.","end":true}]}],"sera_return_why_group":[{"text":"\\"One of us,\\" Sera repeats, and turns it over, and finds the seam in it inside about a second and a half. \\"That is a set with a boundary, and I have spent my whole life on the far side of one. Who decides who is inside it, and on what day?\\"\\n\\nShe picks the stylus back up. \\"Again.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Because a fire with you at it is better, and I cannot show you the arithmetic.","next":"sera_return_why_true"},{"label":"Leave her to it.","end":true}]}],"sera_return_why_true":[{"text":"Sera says nothing for a while.\\n\\n\\"You cannot show me the arithmetic,\\" she says.\\n\\n\\"No.\\"\\n\\n\\"I have spent two years making certain that everybody who kept me could show me the arithmetic, because a person who can be costed cannot be dropped without somebody noticing the hole.\\" She looks at the tablet and does not pick it up. \\"You have just told me there is no hole. That is the most frightening thing anybody has ever said to me.\\"\\n\\nThen, because she is who she is, she picks the tablet up after all, and writes a figure, and turns it round.\\n\\n\\"One thing first,\\" Sera says.","choices":[{"label":"Read the figure.","next":"sera_return_wages"}]}],"sera_return_wages":[{"text":"It says sixty.\\n\\n\\"Two months and four days, at whatever a clerk is worth to an armed party — which nobody has ever set, so I have set it.\\" The stylus taps the figure once. \\"Pay it. Not because it buys anything; you have just been at some trouble to tell me it does not. Because it is owed, and I should like to find out whether you will settle a debt with somebody you are not currently getting anything out of.\\"\\n\\nShe waits.\\n\\n\\"Marrow always paid. That is the part nobody understands. To the copper, on the day, for two years.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Sixty. Here.","conditions":[{"gold_at_least":60}],"effects":[{"take_gold":60},{"set_flag":"sera_paid_properly"},{"recruit":"sera"},{"approval":{"sera":15}},{"set_flag":"sera_returned"}],"next":"sera_return_paid"},{"label":"You would rather have the money than the sentence.","next":"sera_return_barb"},{"label":"I do not have sixty on me.","next":"sera_return_wait"}]}],"sera_return_barb":[{"text":"\\"I would rather have both,\\" Sera says. \\"I am aware that is greedy. I have been extremely poor and extremely frightened at the same time for two years, and I have concluded that the sentence goes down a great deal better with sixty gold under it.\\"\\n\\nShe taps the figure again.","choices":[{"label":"Look at the figure again.","next":"sera_return_wages"},{"label":"Leave her to it.","end":true}]}],"sera_return_wait":[{"text":"\\"Then go and get it,\\" Sera says, and goes back to somebody else''s tally. \\"I am here on Thursdays, and most other days. Nobody is bidding for me.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back out into the square.","end":true}]}],"sera_return_paid":[{"text":"She counts it. Of course she counts it.\\n\\n\\"Sixty,\\" Sera says, and it goes away somewhere about her person, and the tablet follows it. \\"That is the first wage in two years without a condition on the back of it.\\"\\n\\nShe stands, and puts together a bundle, which takes four minutes because she owns almost nothing.\\n\\nAt the edge of the square she stops.\\n\\n\\"If you ever do have a job for me, give it to me. I am not made of glass and I am extremely good.\\" She settles the strap. \\"I only wanted to know that it was not the whole of the account.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Out of the square, then.","end":true}]}],"romance_one":[{"on_enter":[{"romance":{"companion":"sera","stage":1}}],"text":"\\"You,\\" Sera says. \\"Obviously you. I have been extremely clear about it in my own head and catastrophically unclear everywhere else, and I would like that noted as a failure of my own systems and not of yours.\\"\\n\\nShe squares the tablet to the edge of the table, which is what her hands do when the rest of her is elsewhere.\\n\\n\\"I want to be careful about this. Not cautious. Careful. There is a difference and I have only recently learned it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Be careful with me, then.","next":"romance_two"},{"label":"Noted. Nothing more than that.","end":true}]}],"romance_two":[{"on_enter":[{"romance":{"companion":"sera","stage":2}}],"text":"\\"Right,\\" says Sera, and takes a breath she has clearly budgeted for.\\n\\n\\"Here is what I want, and I want it exactly: nothing in writing. No arrangement. No terms. I have been owned on paper by a man who was very courteous about it and I am not doing a courteous version of it with you.\\"\\n\\nShe pushes the tablet away — actually away, off the edge of her own reach, which you have not seen her do.\\n\\n\\"So there is no column for this. It is the only thing I have that is not written down anywhere, and I would like it to stay that way, and I would like it very much.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Nothing in writing.","end":true}]}],"romance_after":[{"text":"\\"''Stayed,''\\" Sera says. \\"That is the ninth line. One word. I have written it every day for eleven days and I have not once been able to improve on it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Don''t improve on it.","end":true}],"pool":"afterglow","conditions":[{"companion":"sera","romance_stage":2}]},{"pool":"afterglow","text":"\\"I have stopped budgeting my breaths around you,\\" Sera says, as if closing an account. \\"I ran the figures. The margin was costing more than the risk.\\" She sets the stylus down — properly down, capped — and moves the lamp so the light falls on both of you.","choices":[{"label":"Spend freely.","end":true}],"conditions":[{"companion":"sera","romance_stage":2}]},{"pool":"afterglow","text":"The tablet stays blank tonight. She is not stuck; she is simply not writing, which for Sera is a whole holiday, and she has let the watch-fire side go to whoever wants it. \\"One column,\\" she says, when she catches you looking. \\"It balances.\\"","choices":[{"label":"It balances.","end":true}],"conditions":[{"companion":"sera","romance_stage":2}]},{"text":"Sera does not look up from the tablet, but the lamp has already been moved so the light falls on both of you.","choices":[{"label":"Close enough.","end":true}]}],"sera_close":[{"conditions":[{"companion":"sera","status":"active"},{"companion":"sera","approval_at_least":30},{"not":{"companion":"sera","romance_stage":2}},{"not":{"companion":"sera","romance_locked":true}}],"text":"Sera has the tablet out and has written nothing on it for some time, which for her is roughly shouting.\\n\\n\\"I have been costing something,\\" she says, \\"and it will not cost. I have tried it four ways. It does not have a number and I do not know what to do with a thing that does not have a number.\\"","once":true,"choices":[{"label":"What are you costing?","next":"romance_one"},{"label":"Some things don''t.","next":"romance_one"},{"label":"Leave her to it.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"companion":"sera","status":"active"},{"companion":"sera","approval_at_least":30},{"not":{"companion":"sera","romance_stage":2}},{"not":{"companion":"sera","romance_locked":true}}],"text":"\\"I have started keeping a second column again,\\" Sera says, and then stops, because she has heard it.\\n\\nShe puts the stylus down very precisely.\\n\\n\\"Not like that. Not the neat one and the true one. One column. It is just that there is a thing in it that I have not told you and I have decided that is the same as the other kind.\\"","pool":"close","choices":[{"label":"Then tell me.","next":"romance_one"},{"label":"You don''t owe me the whole ledger.","next":"romance_one"},{"label":"Say nothing.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"companion":"sera","status":"active"},{"companion":"sera","approval_at_least":30},{"not":{"companion":"sera","romance_stage":2}},{"not":{"companion":"sera","romance_locked":true}}],"text":"She has taken the watch-fire side away from you again, which is the third night running, and she does not do things three nights running by accident.\\n\\n\\"I know,\\" she says, before you say it. \\"I am aware. I have been aware for some time and I have been extremely busy about it.\\"","pool":"close","choices":[{"label":"Be aware out loud.","next":"romance_one"},{"label":"Be busy, then.","end":true}]},{"text":"Sera taps the stylus twice and files whatever it was under a heading you cannot see. \\"Not tonight,\\" she says. \\"The figures aren''t ready.\\"","choices":[{"label":"When they''re ready.","end":true}]}],"camp_sera_fate":[{"text":"\\"I keep reaching for the weight of it,\\" Sera says. \\"Eleven years of a courteous man''s paper, and the hand still goes to the pocket where the copy lived.\\" She spreads the empty hand flat on her knee and looks at it with professional interest. \\"The reach is getting shorter. I have been measuring.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_sera_fate_told"}],"choices":[{"label":"Measure on.","end":true}],"conditions":[{"flag":"sera_leash_fate","equals":"cut"}]},{"text":"\\"I held it because a leash you hold is a leash you can see,\\" Sera says, in the voice she costs things in. \\"I stand by the arithmetic.\\" The stylus stops. \\"I would like it noted that standing by a thing and liking it are separate entries. They have always been separate entries.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_sera_fate_told"}],"choices":[{"label":"Noted.","end":true}]}]}}',0,0,0,NULL),
('serjeant_ballow','Serjeant Ballow','Town Watch','Twenty-two years on the watch and the last four of them on the west gate, which is where the watch puts a man it has stopped promoting.','assets/images/npcs/captain.png','serjeant_ballow',1,'{"start":"greeting","nodes":{"greeting":[{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_almoners_list","stage":"read_the_list"}],"text":"The man on the west arch is somewhere past fifty, with the collar of a serjeant and the posting of a probationer, and he watches you cross the whole distance from the High Street before he speaks.\\n\\n\\"Ballow,\\" he says. \\"Nobody comes down here to talk to me, so you want something, and it is not the road, because the road goes to farms.\\" A pause. \\"Which means it is the farms.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Thirty-six families off the west road in eight months.","next":"he_has_counted"},{"label":"How did you know?","next":"how_he_knew"}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"watch_knows"}],"text":"Ballow is standing a good deal straighter than the west gate requires. \\"Twenty-two years,\\" he says, \\"and I have never once wondered who paid me. That is not a thing a man is supposed to have to wonder.\\" He looks along the road. \\"I have kept the money. All of us kept the money. I want you to know I know how that sounds.\\"","choices":[{"label":"It sounds like a man with a family.","next":"a_man_with_a_family"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"reed_took_it"}],"text":"\\"Paid on the day,\\" says Ballow, \\"same as the last fourteen. Twenty-six of us and the west side walked twice a night.\\"\\n\\nHe looks along the road, and it takes him a moment to get to the rest of it.\\n\\n\\"The captain has stopped saying the roll is met,\\" he says. \\"She used to say it. Every quarter day, in the guardroom — the roll is met, gentlemen. She has not said it since about Lent and I do not think she knows she has stopped.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Would you rather she had sent it back?","next":"would_you_rather"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"watch_thin"}],"text":"\\"Four gone,\\" says Ballow. \\"Two to the granary, one to the stone yard, one to his brother at Greyhythe. West side gets no night patrol at all now.\\" He shifts his weight. \\"And the captain sent the money back, in front of the council, with her name on it. So that is what the four of them cost and I have not decided what I think and I have had a fortnight.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Was she right?","next":"was_she_right"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"west_holdings_recorded"}],"text":"\\"It is in the day book,\\" says Ballow, and he says it like a man reporting a thing done rather than a thing achieved. \\"Reed had me read the thirty-six names back to her out loud while she wrote. Took a quarter of an hour.\\" He looks along the road west. \\"I knew eleven of them to speak to.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Any of them come back?","next":"none_come_back"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"text":"A serjeant of the watch stands under the west arch with nothing whatever to do, and does it properly: back straight, eyes on the road, hands behind him.\\n\\n\\"West gate,\\" he says. \\"Ford road out, farms and the burnt mill beyond. Nobody stops anybody here. If you are going out, go; if you are coming in, welcome to Rivermark, and mind the fourth cobble.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Quiet posting.","next":"quiet_posting"},{"label":"Thanks.","end":true}]}],"quiet_posting":[{"text":"\\"It is the posting the watch gives a man when it has stopped expecting anything of him,\\" says Ballow, without any particular feeling. \\"Twenty-two years. Four of them here.\\" He looks along the road. \\"I do not mind it as much as I am supposed to. You see a great deal from a gate nobody thinks is worth watching.\\"","choices":[{"label":"What have you seen?","next":"what_he_saw"},{"label":"Thanks.","end":true}]}],"what_he_saw":[{"text":"\\"Families,\\" says Ballow. \\"Coming in. All year.\\"\\n\\nHe says it in the flat voice of a man who has been waiting a long while for somebody to ask and has decided in advance not to make anything of it.\\n\\n\\"In daylight. With their tools. Which is what has been sitting wrong with me since about the fifth one.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Why does that sit wrong?","next":"he_has_counted"},{"label":"Thanks.","end":true}]}],"how_he_knew":[{"text":"\\"Because I have been standing here for four years watching them come in,\\" says Ballow, \\"and because sooner or later somebody was going to.\\" He almost smiles and thinks better of it. \\"I have told two people. One was my wife. The other was Wyke''s predecessor, who died.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Tell me, then.","next":"he_has_counted"}]}],"he_has_counted":[{"text":"\\"Thirty-eight,\\" says Ballow. \\"Not thirty-six. Two of them went straight through and down to the river stair and took a boat, so they never got on the almoner''s book.\\"\\n\\nHe has clearly been keeping the number in his head for months.\\n\\n\\"They come in daylight, in families, with their tools on a cart. That is not a farm that has failed — that is a farm that has been *sold*, and a man who has sold walks in the front the way I am standing here now.\\" He shifts. \\"Every one of them had sold. Not one of them would say to whom. I asked the first eleven, and after the eleventh I stopped, on account of how they looked at me when I did.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_almoners_list","stage":"the_west_gate"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse: \\"Not frightened of you. Ashamed in front of you. Those look nearly the same and they are not.\\""},{"companion":"aldric","conditions":[{"not":{"companion":"ilse","status":"active"}}],"text":"Aldric: \\"They would not name the buyer. Men who have been cheated name the buyer at once, and loudly.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"How did they look at you?","next":"how_they_looked"},{"label":"Did anyone ever come the other way? Buyers, surveyors, agents?","next":"the_other_way"},{"label":"Thank you, serjeant.","end":true}]}],"how_they_looked":[{"text":"Ballow takes his time over it.\\n\\n\\"Like men who had done a sensible thing,\\" he says at last, \\"and did not want to have to explain to a watchman why a sensible thing had put them on the parish inside a twelvemonth.\\"\\n\\nHe puts his hands back behind him.\\n\\n\\"That is the part I could not put down. Nobody was angry. If somebody had cheated them there would have been one angry man in thirty-eight, and there was not one.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Did anyone ever come the other way?","next":"the_other_way"},{"label":"Thank you, serjeant.","end":true}]}],"the_other_way":[{"text":"\\"Out?\\" Ballow considers the road. \\"Carts, drovers, the priory''s people twice a month, and a surveying party in the spring with rods and a chain, which I logged, because you log a surveying party.\\"\\n\\n\\"Whose?\\"\\n\\n\\"Town''s, I assumed. Roads get measured.\\" He frowns slightly, for the first time. \\"I did assume that. Nobody told me it.\\"\\n\\nHe lets it go, and so does the conversation, because there is nothing there to hold on to.","choices":[{"label":"Thank you, serjeant.","end":true}]}],"none_come_back":[{"text":"\\"Not one,\\" says Ballow. \\"That is the other thing I have kept in my head. Thirty-eight families out of that road, and in four years not one man has gone back out to have a look at what somebody else is doing with his ground.\\"\\n\\nHe watches the empty road for a moment.\\n\\n\\"I would,\\" he says. \\"I would not be able to keep away.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}],"a_man_with_a_family":[{"text":"\\"Three,\\" says Ballow. \\"Two of them grown and one of them still eating.\\" He looks at the road. \\"So yes. I have kept it, and I will keep the next one, and I will go on standing on this gate for whoever it turns out I am standing on it for, and the only thing that has changed is that I know I do not know.\\"\\n\\nA long pause.\\n\\n\\"That is worse than I expected it to be,\\" he says. \\"I want that on the record with somebody.\\"","choices":[{"label":"It''s on the record.","end":true}]}],"would_you_rather":[{"text":"\\"No,\\" says Ballow, immediately, and then stands there with it.\\n\\n\\"No,\\" he says again. \\"I have got three at home and one of them still eating, and a watch of eighteen does not walk this gate at all, and then I am a serjeant of nothing.\\"\\n\\nHe puts his hands behind him.\\n\\n\\"But I should have liked to be asked,\\" he says. \\"That is the whole of my complaint and I am aware it is a small one. Twenty-two years, and the question of who I work for was settled in a room I was not in.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}],"was_she_right":[{"text":"\\"Yes,\\" says Ballow immediately, and then stands there for a while.\\n\\n\\"Yes,\\" he says again. \\"And Mattock''s widow has no lodger''s rent now and the west side is dark from midnight, and both of those are also true, and I have been a watchman twenty-two years and I have never once been able to hold two true things at the same time without one of them going bad.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}]}}',0,0,0,NULL),
('sib_marrell','Sib Marrell','Nightman','Empties Rivermark''s cesspits by dark and knows the underside of it better than the watch knows the top.','assets/images/npcs/sweeper.png','sib_marrell',1,'{"start":"greeting","nodes":{"greeting":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"night_soil_fate","equals":"killed"}],"once":true,"text":"Sib Marrell is not sitting on the step. Sib Marrell is halfway down a grating on the High Street with a rope round his middle and his boy on the other end of it, shouting up about the state of the brickwork, which is the sound of Rivermark working properly.","choices":[{"label":"Leave him to it.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"night_soil_fate","equals":"people"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"Neighbours,\\" says Sib Marrell, and shakes his head at the water. \\"Twenty years I have been down there and I have known there were folk in the tunnels for nineteen of them, and the one week I get frightened it turns out to have been folk.\\"\\n\\nHe spits into the river without much force.\\n\\n\\"I have started leaving the Granary Row grating on the latch,\\" he says. \\"That is not for telling anybody.\\"","choices":[{"label":"It''s not for telling anybody.","next":"not_for_telling"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"night_soil_fate","equals":"sealed"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"Nine of them bricked and barred and I can work over the top of them in my shirt-sleeves,\\" says Sib Marrell. He does not sound pleased about it. \\"Eleven gold and two days. Twenty years I have been asking that council for a new ladder and they found eleven gold in a fortnight for brick.\\"\\n\\nHe looks along the quay.\\n\\n\\"Wait till it rains,\\" he says. \\"That is not a threat, that is a drain. A drain that will not take water is a wall, and the water still comes.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Where will it go?","next":"where_it_goes"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"night_soil","stage":"what_is_down_there"},{"flag":"conduit_crawler_beaten"}],"text":"Sib Marrell looks at the state of you, and at what is on your boots, and gets up off the step for the first time in a week.\\n\\n\\"Well,\\" he says. \\"Go on then.\\"","choices":[{"label":"It''s dead. It was forty feet of something that shouldn''t be forty feet of anything.","next":"the_crawler_is_dead"},{"label":"It wasn''t only that. There are people down there.","next":"the_people"}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"night_soil","stage":"what_is_down_there"}],"text":"\\"You have been down,\\" says Sib Marrell, reading it off your boots without any pleasure at all. \\"Right. And?\\"","choices":[{"label":"There are people living down there. That''s what''s been moving the gratings.","next":"the_people"},{"label":"There''s something in the conduit and it isn''t people.","next":"not_yet_dead"},{"label":"Brick the gratings. Then nothing comes up them.","next":"the_bricking"}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"night_soil","stage":"into_the_drains"}],"text":"\\"I said I would not take you and I meant it,\\" says Sib. \\"There is a hatch in every cellar between the market and the quay and half of them have not been opened in my life. Find one and go in it and it is nothing to do with me.\\"\\n\\nHe scratches his jaw.\\n\\n\\"The Flagon''s,\\" he says, after a while. \\"Hearthstone''s. Her north wall is not a wall. I have known that for eleven years and I have never once said it to her.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Why tell me now?","next":"why_now"},{"label":"I''ll find it.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"night_soil","status":"active"}],"text":"There is a man sitting on the bottom step of the river stair where the wind takes the smell off him, which is where nightmen sit, and he has been there most of a week, which is not where nightmen sit at all.\\n\\n\\"Sib Marrell,\\" he says, without being asked. \\"And before you start — the pits are in arrears, yes, I know what week it is, and no.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Why not?","next":"why_not"},{"label":"The council sent me.","next":"the_council_sent_me"}]},{"text":"A man sits on the bottom step of the river stair, downwind of everything, doing nothing at all with great determination.\\n\\n\\"Nightman,\\" he says. \\"I would not come closer if I were you and I would not shake hands with me at any price.\\"","choices":[{"label":"You''re not working.","next":"why_not"},{"label":"Fair enough.","end":true}]}],"the_council_sent_me":[{"text":"\\"Council,\\" says Sib Marrell, with feeling. \\"The council has put a notice on a board. The council has not put its own arm down a grating on Granary Row at two in the morning, and if it would care to, I shall hold the lamp.\\"","choices":[{"label":"What''s down there?","next":"why_not"}]}],"why_not":[{"text":"Sib Marrell takes his time, and when he starts he does not look at you, which for a man who has been waiting a week to say it is about right.\\n\\n\\"Twenty years,\\" he says. \\"Man and boy and my father before me. I have been down every grating in this town and I have met everything that lives in them — rats, and rats, and the eels, and once a dog that had been down there long enough to have opinions.\\"\\n\\nHe rubs his hands on his knees.\\n\\n\\"The gratings are being *moved*,\\" he says. \\"That is the whole of it. Not broken. Not shifted by the water. Lifted, and put back, and put back *square*. Three of them, on my round, over about a fortnight.\\"\\n\\n\\"And?\\"\\n\\n\\"And there is nothing in this town with hands that ought to be under it,\\" says Sib Marrell.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"night_soil","stage":"find_the_nightman"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"kessa","text":"Kessa: \\"Put back square.\\" She looks at the nearest grating. \\"That''s the bit, isn''t it. Not that it moved. That somebody tidied.\\""},{"companion":"ilse","conditions":[{"not":{"companion":"kessa","status":"active"}}],"text":"Ilse: \\"Nothing hides a track it does not know it is leaving. Whatever moved those knows it is being looked for.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Take me down.","next":"he_will_not_take_you"},{"label":"Have you told the watch?","next":"not_the_watch"}]}],"he_will_not_take_you":[{"text":"\\"No,\\" says Sib Marrell. \\"And I will not send my boy, and I have said that to his face and he has not spoken to me since Tuesday.\\"\\n\\nHe stands up, which takes a while.\\n\\n\\"Find your own hatch,\\" he says. \\"There is one in every cellar on this side of the town and I am not going to be the man who put you in one.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"night_soil","stage":"into_the_drains"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Which one would you use?","next":"which_hatch"},{"label":"I''ll find one.","end":true}]}],"which_hatch":[{"text":"He thinks about it for long enough that you can watch him decide to be helpful against his own better judgement.\\n\\n\\"The Flagon''s,\\" he says. \\"Hearthstone''s cellar. Her north wall is not a wall, and it has not been a wall for eleven years, and I have never once told her because what would she do with it.\\"\\n\\nHe sits back down on the step.\\n\\n\\"Behind the grain sacks,\\" he says. \\"Where the sump is. Get a lamp you can put in your teeth.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Why tell me at all?","next":"why_now"},{"label":"Thank you.","end":true}]}],"why_now":[{"text":"\\"Because in nine more days the pits go over,\\" says Sib Marrell, \\"and then it is not my week off, it is a fever in the Row, and I have seen one of those and you have not.\\"\\n\\nHe looks at the water.\\n\\n\\"And because I am fifty-three and frightened,\\" he says, \\"and I have been sitting on this step for six days working out how to say that to somebody, and it turns out you just say it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"I''ll go and look.","end":true}]}],"not_the_watch":[{"text":"\\"No,\\" says Sib, and there is something careful in it. \\"And I would take it kindly if you did not either, not yet.\\"\\n\\nHe turns his hat over.\\n\\n\\"Watch goes down a grating, watch finds whatever is down a grating, and then there are men with staves in the tunnels for a fortnight.\\" He stops. \\"That is all I am saying. Twenty years is a long time to be underneath a town and not learn who else is.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Take me down.","next":"he_will_not_take_you"},{"label":"Who else is?","next":"he_will_not_say"}]}],"he_will_not_say":[{"text":"\\"Go and see,\\" says Sib Marrell. \\"I am not going to put words in your mouth about people who have never done me any harm, and I am not going to pretend to you that I have not known.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Take me down.","next":"he_will_not_take_you"}]}],"not_yet_dead":[{"text":"\\"Then it is still down there,\\" says Sib Marrell, \\"and so am I not.\\" He sits back down on the step. \\"Come back when it is one or the other. I am not being hard. I have got a boy and a bad chest and no other trade.\\"","choices":[{"label":"I''ll go back down.","end":true}]}],"the_crawler_is_dead":[{"text":"You tell him what it was and where it was and how long it was, and Sib Marrell listens to the whole thing with his hat in his hands.\\n\\n\\"On the crown of the vault,\\" he repeats. \\"Above the lamp.\\"\\n\\n\\"Above the lamp.\\"\\n\\n\\"Twenty years,\\" says Sib Marrell, \\"and I have never once looked up.\\"\\n\\nThe nightmen are back down the gratings inside the week. He does not thank you — he shakes your hand, which given his trade is a considerably bigger thing, and which he clearly knows.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"night_soil","stage":"killed_it"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Look up more often.","end":true}]}],"the_people":[{"text":"\\"I know,\\" says Sib Marrell.\\n\\nHe says it straight away, and then has to sit with having said it.\\n\\n\\"I have known since I was thirty-one. There are folk in the flood tunnels and there have been folk in the flood tunnels since my father''s time, and they have never once taken anything off me nor stopped me working.\\" He turns the hat over. \\"And this month they started coming up my gratings, and I got frightened, and I did not once think to ask why they had started.\\"\\n\\n\\"The gates shut at dusk,\\" you tell him. \\"They always have. The drains are the only door they have got.\\"\\n\\nSib Marrell puts the hat on.\\n\\n\\"Right,\\" he says. \\"Then I have wasted a week of the town''s pits being frightened of my neighbours, and I shall go and say so to the council in those words, and they will not like it, and that is the council''s affair.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"night_soil","stage":"it_was_people"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric: \\"He said ''my neighbours'' without deciding to. That is the whole thing settled, whatever the council does.\\""},{"companion":"ilse","conditions":[{"not":{"companion":"aldric","status":"active"}}],"text":"Ilse: \\"Nobody will do anything about Undertown now. That is the best outcome available and I want it written down that I know how thin it is.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Say it in those words.","end":true}]}],"the_bricking":[{"text":"It costs the council eleven gold and takes two days.\\n\\nNine gratings on the High Street and Granary Row, bricked and barred. The nightmen work over the top of them in perfect safety and Sib Marrell gets his round back and does not look happy about any of it.\\n\\n\\"A drain that will not take water is a wall,\\" he says, on the second day, watching the mason. \\"And the water still comes. I have said that to three men in a good coat and every one of them has told me it is not the season.\\"\\n\\nThe first hard rain of the autumn comes up through the cellars of everything between the market and the quay. Undertown loses the whole of its eastern side in a night.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"night_soil","stage":"bricked_the_gratings"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Leave it there.","end":true}]}],"not_for_telling":[{"text":"\\"Good,\\" says Sib Marrell. \\"Because there is a girl of about nine who used to come up that one and now does not have to knock at Hearthstone''s back door for what she is after, and I would rather that arrangement went on being nobody''s business.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Nobody''s business.","end":true}]}],"where_it_goes":[{"text":"\\"Where it always goes,\\" says Sib Marrell. \\"Down, till it cannot, and then up somebody''s cellar steps.\\" He looks along the quay at the neat new brickwork. \\"Mine is on the high side. That is the only reason I am talking to you about it and not shouting.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}]}}',0,0,0,NULL),
('sister_nairn','Sister Nairn','Keeper of the Deep Mere','Was mate on a grain hoy for nineteen years before she was anything else, and it shows in how she stands. For six years the offerings thrown off the temple rail have been washing back ashore, and she has been quietly collecting them and locking them in the back room, because throwing them in again feels like an insult to something.','assets/images/npcs/cleric.png','cleric',8,'{"start":"greeting","nodes":{"greeting":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"mere_fate","equals":"answered"}],"expression":2,"text":"The back room is open and empty and has been swept, and the door is propped with a stone so that it cannot shut.\\n\\n\\"Six years of it went over that rail in one afternoon,\\" says Sister Nairn, \\"with the whole district on the shingle and every single one named aloud by the person who threw it. Four hours. Nobody left.\\"\\n\\nShe looks at the water.\\n\\n\\"Nothing has come back. That is not forgiveness. I want to be very clear with you about that, because everybody in Old Waer has decided it is, and I have not got the standing to argue with a district that has started sleeping again.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Then what is it?\\"","next":"not_forgiveness"},{"label":"Leave her to it.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"mere_fate","equals":"quiet"}],"expression":5,"text":"The back room is locked. Sister Nairn checks the lock after she has locked it, and then checks it again, which she did not use to do twice.\\n\\n\\"It is quiet,\\" she says. \\"You agreed and I am grateful and I have been grateful every day since in a way I do not much care for.\\"\\n\\nShe puts the key back inside her collar.\\n\\n\\"There were eleven more on the shingle on Tuesday. I have had to unlock it after all. That is the only thing I am going to say about it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Say nothing.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"drowned_room_beaten"}],"expression":4,"text":"Sister Nairn has been standing at the rail for some time and has not thrown anything over it.\\n\\n\\"You have been in the room,\\" she says. \\"You need not describe it. I was mate on a grain hoy for nineteen years and I have had one recurring dream since I was twenty-six and you have just been standing in it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"They were laid out. Recently.\\"","next":"laid_out"},{"label":"\\"What do you want to do?\\"","next":"the_choice"},{"label":"Not yet.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"offerings_seen"}],"expression":3,"text":"\\"You have seen them,\\" says Sister Nairn, \\"so we are past the part where I decide whether to tell you, and I find I am enormously relieved and also would quite like you to leave.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"How many are there?\\"","next":"how_many"},{"label":"\\"What do we do?\\"","next":"the_choice"},{"label":"Later.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"met_nairn"}],"text":"\\"Back,\\" says Sister Nairn, and goes on doing what she is doing, which is mopping a line of wet that runs from the rail inward across the nave floor and will be wet again in an hour.","choices":[{"label":"\\"The room at the back.\\"","next":"the_room"},{"label":"\\"Why is the floor wet in a line?\\"","next":"the_wet_line"},{"label":"Another time.","end":true}]},{"text":"There is no altar. The nave runs out to a rail over forty fathoms of open water and that is the whole of the arrangement, and it is considerably more frightening than a statue would be.\\n\\nThe woman mopping the floor has a sailor''s forearms and a sailor''s stance and does not look round.\\n\\n\\"Sister Nairn,\\" she says. \\"Keeper. Offerings go over the rail, not onto anything. There is no collection, there is nothing to buy and there is nothing I can promise you.\\"\\n\\nShe wrings the mop out.\\n\\n\\"If you have come to ask what is down there, the answer the Temple gives is the Mere, and the answer I give is that I have been asking for nineteen years and the Temple''s answer is better than mine.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"met_nairn"}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"What is in the room at the back?\\"","next":"the_room"},{"label":"\\"Why is the floor wet in a line?\\"","next":"the_wet_line"},{"label":"\\"Ellsa Waer said to ask you how many.\\"","next":"how_many"},{"label":"Leave her to it.","end":true}]}],"the_wet_line":[{"text":"Sister Nairn stops mopping.\\n\\n\\"Because things come out of the water,\\" she says, \\"and they come out at the rail, and somebody carries them in.\\"\\n\\nShe goes back to it.\\n\\n\\"That is the true answer and it takes about four seconds and everybody who hears it decides I am being funny. Go on. Ask the other question.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"What is in the room at the back?\\"","next":"the_room"},{"label":"\\"How many?\\"","next":"how_many"}]}],"the_room":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"offerings_seen"}],"expression":3,"text":"\\"It is open,\\" says Nairn. \\"It has been open since you were last here. I have not gone in.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"How many are there?\\"","next":"how_many"},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]},{"text":"Nairn puts the mop in the bucket and stands with both hands on the handle of it, which is what she does instead of folding her arms.\\n\\n\\"Offerings,\\" she says. \\"That have come back.\\"\\n\\nShe lets it sit.\\n\\n\\"For six years. Thrown off that rail into forty fathoms with the tide going out, and found on the shingle at the foot of Old Waer three and four days later. Not weathered. Not fouled. A copper luck-piece that went in on a Tuesday comes out on a Friday warmer than the hand that finds it.\\"\\n\\n\\"I have been putting them in that room because throwing them back feels like an insult to something, and because the alternative is telling four hundred Waerfolk that the water they have been giving to for four hundred years has started giving back.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"nairn_told_you"}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"Open it.\\"","next":"she_unlocks_it"},{"label":"\\"Ellsa Waer told me to ask you how many.\\"","next":"how_many"},{"label":"Leave it locked.","end":true}]}],"how_many":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"offerings_seen"}],"expression":4,"text":"\\"More than went in,\\" says Nairn. \\"That is the count and it is why I stopped counting.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"What do we do?\\"","next":"the_choice"},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"nairn_told_you"}],"expression":4,"text":"Nairn takes the key out from inside her collar and holds it without going anywhere.\\n\\n\\"She would ask that,\\" she says. \\"Eighty-one years old and she goes straight to the one question I have arranged my whole life around not answering.\\"\\n\\nShe unlocks the door.\\n\\n\\"Come and see, then, and count them yourself, and tell me I am wrong. I would take that. I would take being told I am wrong by a stranger over another six years of this.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Go in.","next":"she_unlocks_it"}]},{"text":"Nairn goes very still.\\n\\n\\"That is not a question about offerings,\\" she says. \\"That is a question about arithmetic, and there is exactly one person on this shore who would think to ask it.\\"\\n\\nShe wrings the mop out very carefully.\\n\\n\\"You had better come and see the room.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"nairn_told_you"}],"choices":[{"label":"Go in.","next":"she_unlocks_it"}]}],"she_unlocks_it":[{"expression":4,"text":"It is a small dry room with shelves that were built for candles and hymnals, and it is full.\\n\\nCopper luck-pieces. A wedding ring. Fish-hooks bound in thread. A child''s shoe. A carved wooden gull with the paint still on it. Eleven silver Waerfolk marriage-tokens, which are given once and thrown once and never made twice. None of it corroded. None of it fouled. A rope-end on the second shelf still smells of the tar it was dipped in.\\n\\n\\"Six years,\\" says Sister Nairn behind you.\\n\\n\\"And here is what I have never said aloud. I have kept the Temple''s book since I came ashore. Every offering given from that rail is written in it, because the giving is a sacrament and the sacraments are written.\\"\\n\\nShe does not come into the room.\\n\\n\\"There are things on those shelves that are not in the book.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"offerings_seen"},{"start_quest":"what_the_mere_gives_back"},{"quest_stage":{"quest":"what_the_mere_gives_back","stage":"the_locked_room"}},{"xp":150}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"Not in the book — meaning nobody threw them.\\"","next":"not_in_the_book"},{"label":"\\"Show me one.\\"","next":"not_in_the_book"}]}],"not_in_the_book":[{"expression":5,"text":"She reaches past you and takes a thing off the second shelf without looking at it, which means she knows exactly where it is.\\n\\nA net-mender''s bone needle, worn to a shine at the grip, with three notches cut in the shank.\\n\\n\\"That is a Waer needle,\\" she says. \\"Those notches are a family mark and they belong to a house that has not had anybody in it for eleven years. Nobody threw this off my rail. Nobody has thrown anything off my rail on behalf of that house since the last of them died.\\"\\n\\nShe puts it back.\\n\\n\\"It came out of the water in April. And there are nine more like it, and one of them is a thing I threw off that rail myself, at my mother''s burial, in the year I came ashore.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Then it is not giving them back. It is answering.\\"","next":"the_answer"},{"label":"\\"Where do they come ashore?\\"","next":"where_ashore"}]}],"the_answer":[{"expression":5,"text":"Sister Nairn looks at you for a long moment and then sits down on the step of her own back room, which a keeper does not do.\\n\\n\\"Yes,\\" she says. \\"Yes, that is it, that is the sentence, and I have not been able to make it in six years.\\"\\n\\n\\"It is not returning them. Returning would be all of them or none. It is choosing. Somebody down there is selecting, and sending, and about a tenth of what comes ashore was never thrown at all — it is being offered *to* us. In the same currency. In our own tokens.\\"\\n\\nShe puts her head in her hands for a moment, professionally, the way you do a thing you have practised.\\n\\n\\"And for six years I have been locking the correspondence in a cupboard.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Where do they come ashore?\\"","next":"where_ashore"},{"label":"\\"What do you want to do?\\"","next":"the_choice"}]}],"where_ashore":[{"text":"\\"The shingle under Old Waer,\\" says Nairn, \\"which is where anything in this harbour comes ashore, and which is the useless answer.\\"\\n\\nShe gets up.\\n\\n\\"The useful one is that they come up on the ebb, and only on the ebb, and never anywhere else on this shore. Not the North Horn. Not the Boom. Not the point.\\"\\n\\n\\"And there is one place in this harbour that fills and empties with the ebb and has done since before the piers were built, and the Waerfolk have a name for one room in it and use the name about once a generation.\\"\\n\\nShe meets your eye.\\n\\n\\"Under the old quay. The room that does not drain.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"what_the_mere_gives_back","stage":"the_drowned_room"}}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"I will go down.\\"","end":true},{"label":"\\"What is in it?\\"","next":"the_choice"}]}],"laid_out":[{"expression":5,"text":"\\"Laid out,\\" Nairn repeats.\\n\\nShe puts both hands on the rail.\\n\\n\\"Not sunk. Not dropped. Arranged, on a floor, under water that does not move, by something that has hands or something that has patience, and I cannot decide which of those two I would rather.\\"\\n\\n\\"Nineteen years I was at sea. I have hauled things up on a line that I did not look at twice. And every one of those was an accident of the water.\\"\\n\\nShe turns round.\\n\\n\\"This is not the water. The water is what it is being done through.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"what_the_mere_gives_back","stage":"the_drowned_room"}}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"So what do we do?\\"","next":"the_choice"}]}],"the_choice":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"drowned_room_beaten"}],"expression":4,"text":"\\"Two things,\\" says Sister Nairn, \\"and I have been standing at this rail for four days failing to choose between them, and I am going to make you do it, and I am aware that is a cowardice.\\"\\n\\nShe holds up one hand.\\n\\n\\"We answer. Every one of them goes back over this rail in one afternoon, in front of the whole district, and every single one is named aloud by the person who threw it. Not a service. A reply. Six years of correspondence answered in the same currency, publicly, so that whatever is down there knows it was heard and so that four hundred Waerfolk know their city heard it too.\\"\\n\\nThe other hand.\\n\\n\\"Or the room stays locked. The Temple stays quiet. The district goes on picking things off the shingle at dawn and not saying anything, which is what Old Waer is best at, and nobody has to find out what happens when you answer.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Answer it.\\"","next":"the_answering"},{"label":"\\"Keep it quiet.\\"","next":"the_quiet_choice"},{"label":"\\"Not yet.\\"","end":true}]},{"text":"\\"I do not know,\\" says Sister Nairn, \\"and I have decided to stop pretending that I am about to.\\"\\n\\n\\"But there is a room under the old quay that does not drain, and things come ashore on the ebb, and I have been keeping a locked cupboard rather than walking down twenty-two steps for six years.\\"\\n\\nShe hands you the key on its cord.\\n\\n\\"Go and look at the bottom of it. Then come back and we will both be looking at the same thing, which is more than I have had since I came ashore.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"what_the_mere_gives_back","stage":"the_drowned_room"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Take the key.","end":true}]}],"the_answering":[{"expression":2,"text":"Sister Nairn does not thank you. She goes and opens the back room and props the door with a stone so that it cannot shut, and then she goes out onto the shingle and starts telling people.\\n\\nIt takes four hours. Nobody leaves. Every piece goes over the rail into forty fathoms and every one is named aloud by whoever gave it — and the ten that nobody threw are named by Nairn herself, house by house, including the empty one.\\n\\nNothing comes back.\\n\\n\\"That is not forgiveness,\\" she says afterwards, hoarse, on the rail. \\"I want that written down somewhere. It is a letter answered. We do not know what we have agreed to.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"what_the_mere_gives_back","stage":"answered"}}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"No. We do not.\\"","end":true}]}],"the_quiet_choice":[{"expression":3,"text":"Sister Nairn nods, and locks the back room, and checks the lock, and then checks it again.\\n\\n\\"Thank you,\\" she says, and means it, and it is awful.\\n\\n\\"I have been alone with it for six years and what I wanted was not help. I have had four offers of help. What I wanted was one other person to look at the whole of it and agree that the right thing is to do nothing, so that it stops being my decision.\\"\\n\\nShe puts the key back inside her collar.\\n\\n\\"You have given me that. I am going to be grateful to you for it for a very long time and I do not think either of us should be pleased.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"what_the_mere_gives_back","stage":"quiet"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Go.","end":true}]}],"not_forgiveness":[{"expression":3,"text":"\\"A reply,\\" says Sister Nairn. \\"That is all. Somebody wrote to this city for six years in the only language it had, and this city finally wrote back, and now it is a correspondence rather than a haunting.\\"\\n\\nShe watches the water.\\n\\n\\"And a correspondence goes on. That is what nobody on that shingle has thought about yet. They are sleeping because the shelves are empty.\\"\\n\\nShe glances at the propped door.\\n\\n\\"I left the room open. There will be more. There will be more and now we have told it that we are listening, and I would like you to be in the district when the next one comes ashore.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"I will be.\\"","end":true}]}]}}',0,1,0,NULL),
('sister_ovid','Ovid','Keeper of the Presses','Keeps the cartulary — every deed, boundary and account the house has held since it was founded. Dusts the presses weekly and lets the rest of the priory look after itself.','assets/images/npcs/wizard.png','wizard',1,'{"start":"hail","nodes":{"hail":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"priory_record_cut"}],"text":"The fourth press is open on the desk at the decade and there is a leather marker in it and a gutter with a clean cut in it where a leaf used to be.\\n\\nOvid has not said anything about it to anybody. She looks up when you come in and looks down again.\\n\\n\\"It was a good knife,\\" she says.","choices":[{"label":"It was.","next":"it_was"},{"label":"Say nothing.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"priory_record_read"}],"text":"\\"You have read it,\\" Ovid says. \\"You will want to read it again. Everybody does, once, about three days later, and then not after that.\\"\\n\\nShe turns the press round on the desk without being asked.","choices":[{"label":"Read it again.","next":"eleven_lines"},{"label":"Not today.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_ford_in_the_record","stage":"eleven_lines"}],"text":"The press is open at the decade and the volume is on the desk under the best window in the house, squared to the edge.\\n\\n\\"The fourth winter,\\" Ovid says. \\"Second week. It is eleven lines and it is in the infirmarer''s hand, and I am going to go and do something at the other end of the room.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Read it.","next":"eleven_lines"}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_ford_in_the_record","stage":"the_fourth_press"}],"text":"Four desks under the best window in the house, three of them stacked and one of them kept clear, and along the north wall the cartulary presses — every deed, grant, boundary and account this order has held since it was founded, bound by decade, in order, dusted.\\n\\nThe woman at the clear desk is old, unhurried, and does not stop writing when you come in.\\n\\n\\"Ovid,\\" she says. \\"I keep the presses. Say the decade and I will get it down, and do not touch the presses yourself, because you will put them back wrong and then it will be wrong for two hundred years.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"met_ovid"}],"choices":[{"label":"The fourth winter back. The ford.","next":"the_fourth_press"},{"label":"Does anyone ask for these?","next":"does_anyone_ask"},{"label":"Nothing. Sorry to disturb you.","end":true}]},{"text":"The only warm room in the priory, and it is warm because the presses must not damp.\\n\\nAn old woman at the one clear desk finishes the line she is on before she looks up.\\n\\n\\"Ovid,\\" she says. \\"Presses. Say the decade.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"met_ovid"},{"start_quest":"the_ford_in_the_record"}],"interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera has read the spines of the whole north wall before anybody has finished saying hello. \\"A hundred and forty years,\\" she says. \\"Complete. I have never seen a complete one.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"The fourth winter back. The ford.","next":"the_fourth_press"},{"label":"What''s in them?","next":"does_anyone_ask"},{"label":"Nothing.","end":true}]}],"it_was":[{"text":"\\"I have not told the prior,\\" Ovid says. \\"I will, because I write everything down and that includes this, and I have not yet worked out the sentence.\\"\\n\\nShe closes the press.\\n\\n\\"A hundred and forty years and this house has never had a gap in it. Now it has one, and the gap is the only interesting thing that ever happened to us.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Say nothing and go.","end":true}]}],"does_anyone_ask":[{"text":"\\"No,\\" Ovid says. \\"Twice in eleven years. A boundary case in the second year, which we won, and a man in the spring who wanted to buy the lot.\\"\\n\\nShe puts the pen down.\\n\\n\\"He was very courteous and he did not want to read any of it. He wanted to buy it. There is a difference and I have thought about very little else since.\\"","interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera: \\"He did not ask what was in it.\\" Ovid: \\"He did not ask what was in it.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"The fourth winter back. The ford.","next":"the_fourth_press"},{"label":"Thank you.","end":true}]}],"the_fourth_press":[{"text":"She gets it down herself, because nobody else is allowed to, and it takes her a while and she does not accept help.\\n\\nThe fourth press. The decade. The volume goes on the clear desk squared to the edge, and she opens it at the winter without looking at the index, which tells you she has been at this page before.\\n\\n\\"Second week,\\" Ovid says. \\"Eleven lines, in the infirmarer''s hand. Nobody has asked for it in four years.\\"\\n\\nShe goes and does something at the other end of the room.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_ford_in_the_record","stage":"eleven_lines"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Read it.","next":"eleven_lines"}]}],"eleven_lines":[{"text":"Eleven lines. Dated. In the infirmarer''s hand, which is a good hand that shakes twice.\\n\\nFour huts on the west bank of the crossing. Nineteen sick, of whom eleven were already dying and eight were not. The fever had begun to take carters, and a river is a thing a fever will not cross, and the house went down to the ford in the second week.\\n\\nThe order to fire the huts was given. It is in the record as an order, given by the house, in the house''s own words, to stop the fever crossing the river. It is not hedged and it is not excused.\\n\\nOne brother would not carry it out. The entry names the hut he would not fire, and names the person who was in it.\\n\\nBettony Frayle.\\n\\nNowhere in the eleven lines does the word *murder* appear.","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"priory_record_read"}],"interjections":[{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric is not in the writing room. He did not come through the cloister arch and he has not come through it since you arrived at this house."},{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera reads it three times. \\"Both of them are true,\\" she says at last. \\"His and this. That is the worst possible arrangement and it is also the most common one.\\""},{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse: \\"Eight of them were not dying.\\" She reads it again. \\"It says so. Their own book says so and they kept it.\\""},{"companion":"kessa","text":"Kessa: \\"They wrote it down.\\" She sounds genuinely thrown. \\"They gave that order and then they wrote it in a book and dusted the book for four years.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Make a fair copy. Exact.","next":"copied"},{"label":"Put it back. It stays in the house.","next":"read_and_left"},{"label":"Cut the leaf out.","next":"took_the_leaf"}]}],"copied":[{"text":"Ovid finds you a good pen and better paper and does not ask what it is for, which is a form of help.\\n\\nThe fair copy is eleven lines and it is exact. You check it against the original twice. Ovid checks it a third time without being asked, and corrects one word, and says the word out loud so that you know which one it was.\\n\\nThe press still holds the original. Now there are two.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_ford_in_the_record","stage":"copied_the_record"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera: \\"Two copies in two buildings. That is the difference between a fact and a rumour and it costs a sheet of paper.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Fold it and put it away.","end":true}]}],"read_and_left":[{"text":"Ovid closes the volume, marks the page with a strip of leather the way she marks everything, and carries it back to the press herself.\\n\\nIt is in the house. It has been in the house for four years and it will be in the house in forty. It says what it says whether or not anybody outside this room has been told, and that is either a comfort or the exact opposite of one depending on the hour of the day you think about it.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_ford_in_the_record","stage":"read_and_left_it"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Leave the room.","end":true}]}],"took_the_leaf":[{"text":"One leaf. Cut at the gutter, clean, in about four seconds, while Ovid is at the far end of the room with her back to you doing something she does not need to do.\\n\\nThe priory''s record of the fourth winter now has a gap in it where the ford was.\\n\\nNobody can produce their version any more. Neither can anybody else, ever — not the prior, not the man who wanted to buy the press, not Bettony Frayle''s brother, not in eighty years when everyone who was there is dead and the only thing left is what is written down.\\n\\nYou do not think about that part until you are on the track going down.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_ford_in_the_record","stage":"took_the_leaf"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera does not speak until the gate. Then: \\"You have made it unprovable. For him and against him. Both.\\""},{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric is at the gate with the horses and asks what was in the book, and there is now exactly one person alive who can tell him, and it is you."}],"choices":[{"label":"Fold it and go.","end":true}]}]}}',0,1,0,NULL),
('slip_sawyer','A pit-sawyer','Sawyer',NULL,'assets/images/npcs/miner.png','miner',1,'{"start":"greeting","nodes":{"greeting":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"masts_fate","equals":"found"}],"text":"\\"They were in the dock the whole time,\\" the sawyer says, and spits. \\"Under a Drennmark boat. Half this yard knew and the other half worked out not to ask.\\" He looks at the chalked quota on the gable. \\"And we are still short of timber, and that is still a real number, and nobody has moved it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]},{"pool":"idle","conditions":[{"flag":"met_gedda"}],"text":"\\"Doubled,\\" the sawyer says, pointing his chin at the gable. \\"The quota. Doubled. On what timber? You cannot saw a promise.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]},{"pool":"idle","conditions":[{"flag":"met_gedda"}],"text":"\\"Sawyers'' Hall votes Thursday,\\" he says. \\"On whether to down tools. It will not carry. It has not carried in eleven years. We take the vote anyway because the year it does carry, everybody wants to have been in the room.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]},{"pool":"idle","conditions":[{"flag":"met_gedda"}],"text":"He watches the third slip, where nothing is happening and forty men are standing about being paid to stand about. \\"That is what a shortage looks like from inside,\\" he says. \\"Not hunger. Waiting.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]},{"text":"A pit-sawyer sits on the end of a baulk with his forearms on his knees, resting in the deliberate way of a man on somebody else''s time.\\n\\n\\"Mind the ways,\\" he says, nodding at the greased slip. \\"Two men a year go down those and it is always somebody who has been here long enough to stop looking.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}]}}',0,0,1,NULL),
('sten_harrow','Sten Harrow','Farmer','Third of his name on this ground and the first to be written to. He has four letters in the house and a lamp on the porch table for reading them by, because he will not read them where his wife can see.','assets/images/npcs/farmer.png','sten_harrow',1,'{"start":"hail","nodes":{"hail":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"harrow_fate","equals":"held"}],"once":true,"text":"The lamp is on the porch table and it is lit, and he is sitting under it in the evening reading nothing at all, which he tells you is the point.\\n\\n\\"Common to three holdings,\\" Sten Harrow says. \\"Can''t be bought off one man because it isn''t one man''s. Bewick down the road worked out what that meant about four days after he''d signed it and came up here to shake my hand and then swore at me for twenty minutes.\\"","choices":[{"label":"He''ll come round.","next":"he_will_come_round"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"harrow_gone"}],"text":"The yard drains as well as it ever did and the gates still hang true and there is somebody else''s dog on the chain. The ground is being worked already, which was fast.","choices":[{"label":"Walk on.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"harrow_farm","stage":"what_they_are_buying"}],"text":"\\"You''ve been up the river,\\" Harrow says. He has the four letters out on the porch table in a row, in date order, which is how he keeps them. \\"Go on, then. Say the thing you came back to say and say it in front of the lamp, because I''ll want to look at them while you do.\\"","choices":[{"label":"They aren''t buying the farm.","next":"not_the_farm"}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"harrow_farm","stage":"the_letters"}],"text":"The dog gets up, walks to the end of its chain, and waits to see which way you go, and Sten Harrow comes out of the byre wiping his hands and does exactly the same thing.\\n\\n\\"You''ll be the ones Ellery sent up from the market,\\" he says. \\"Right. Well. There''s nothing here that wants swords.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"met_harrow"}],"choices":[{"label":"There''s a lamp on your porch table that never gets lit.","next":"the_lamp"},{"label":"You''ve been getting letters.","next":"the_lamp"},{"label":"Then we''ll go.","end":true}]},{"text":"A long low farmhouse, a squared muck-heap, ditches clear, gates hanging true. The man who did all of that comes out of the byre wiping his hands and stops in the middle of the yard rather than at the gate, which is a decision.\\n\\n\\"Sten Harrow. Third of the name on this ground.\\" A pause. \\"You''ll want the road. It''s east.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"met_harrow"},{"start_quest":"harrow_farm"}],"interjections":[{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse: \\"Everything on this holding has been done twice. That is a man who expects to be here in thirty years.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"There''s a lamp on your porch table that has been trimmed and not lit.","next":"the_lamp"},{"label":"The road, then.","end":true}]}],"he_will_come_round":[{"text":"\\"He will,\\" Harrow says. \\"He''s already stopped swearing about it and started telling people it was his idea, which is how I know it''s holding.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Good night.","end":true}]}],"the_lamp":[{"text":"Sten Harrow looks at the porch table for a while and then goes and sits at it, which is an answer.\\n\\n\\"Four,\\" he says. \\"Nine months. Each one better than the last.\\" He gets them out from under the lamp base, in date order. \\"I read them out here because the house isn''t big enough to read them in and my wife has enough to be going on with.\\"\\n\\nHe pushes them across.\\n\\n\\"Not one of them''s signed with a name. Black wax square and that''s your lot.\\"","on_enter":[{"give_item":"harrow_letters","quantity":1}],"interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera takes them in date order and reads the fourth one first. \\"Three of these are a clerk''s hand and this one is not,\\" she says. \\"Somebody stopped delegating.\\""},{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric: \\"They are very polite.\\" Harrow: \\"Aye. That''s what''s wrong with them.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Read them properly. All four.","check":{"skill":"investigation","dc":13,"on_success":"not_the_farm","on_failure":"cannot_see_it"}},{"label":"What does the fourth one say?","next":"the_fourth_letter"},{"label":"Keep them under the lamp.","end":true}]}],"the_fourth_letter":[{"text":"\\"Says there''s a date,\\" Harrow says. \\"Says after the date the offer regretfully lapses.\\" He almost smiles. \\"That''s a man who''s never farmed. A lapsing offer means nothing at all to me. Ground feeds us whether or not anybody''s offering for it.\\"\\n\\nHe stops smiling.\\n\\n\\"Except I''ve read it eleven times.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Read all four properly.","check":{"skill":"investigation","dc":13,"on_success":"not_the_farm","on_failure":"cannot_see_it"}},{"label":"Leave it for tonight.","end":true}]}],"cannot_see_it":[{"text":"Four offers, nine months, rising. Courteous, exact, and unsigned. Whatever else is in them is under the courtesy and it is not coming out on a porch at dusk with the man who owns them watching your face.","choices":[{"label":"I''ll read them again somewhere else.","next":"the_fourth_letter"},{"label":"Leave it.","end":true}]}],"not_the_farm":[{"text":"It is in every one of the four, in the same subordinate clause, phrased four slightly different ways: *together with all rights of water appurtenant thereto*.\\n\\nThey are not offering for two hundred acres. The two hundred acres are what they have to buy in order to get the thing they want, which is the water right on the mill race — which Sten Harrow holds, and has never once thought about, because the mill it used to turn burned down four years ago.\\n\\n\\"That''s nothing,\\" Harrow says. \\"That''s a dead mill and a ditch.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"harrow_farm","stage":"what_they_are_buying"}},{"set_flag":"mill_race_right_known"}],"interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera: \\"Four offers in nine months for a dead mill and a ditch. It is not nothing. It stopped being nothing in the spring and nobody has told the valley why.\\""},{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse: \\"Water goes downhill from that race to the fen. Whoever holds it holds what happens to everything under it.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Then make it three men''s water, not one man''s.","next":"held"},{"label":"Take the fourth offer. It''s the best you''ll get.","next":"sold"},{"label":"Sign it. There''s a fee in it for me and I''ll not pretend otherwise.","next":"brokered"},{"label":"That''s your business. I''ll leave it with you.","end":true}]}],"held":[{"text":"It takes two days, three neighbours, a clerk who wants six coppers, and one long argument on Bewick''s threshing floor about whether a thing that has never been worth anything can be given away.\\n\\nThe water right on the mill race is now common to three holdings, which means it cannot be bought from one man, because it is no longer one man''s. The fourth letter''s date came and went. Nothing arrived to replace it.\\n\\nHarrow has started sleeping. He mentions this in the way a man mentions the weather, and then has to stop and look at the byre for a minute.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"harrow_farm","stage":"held_the_farm"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric: \\"That is the first thing anyone in this valley has done that cannot be undone with money.\\""},{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera: \\"They will buy Bewick. It will take longer and cost more.\\" A pause. \\"Longer and more is the whole of what anybody wins against paper.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Get some sleep, Harrow.","end":true}]}],"sold":[{"text":"He takes it, because it is a very good price, and because you told him to and he had been waiting nine months for somebody to tell him something.\\n\\nThe Harrows go downriver in a hired cart on the second morning with the dog on the tail-board. Sten shakes your hand at the gate and thanks you, properly, without any edge on it at all, which is worse than if there had been.\\n\\nBy the end of the week the ground is being worked by somebody else.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"harrow_farm","stage":"they_sold"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse watches the cart until it is out of sight. \\"That is the fourth this year,\\" she says. \\"Nobody is counting them, so it is not happening.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Watch them go.","end":true}]}],"brokered":[{"text":"There is a fee for bringing in a signature. It is paid the same day, in coin, by a man on a hired horse who does not haggle, does not stay, and does not write anything down.\\n\\nHarrow signed because you told him it was the best he would get, which was true, and because you told him you had no interest in it, which was not.\\n\\nHe did not ask. That is the part that stays.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"harrow_farm","stage":"took_the_brokers_fee"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric says one sentence and then does not speak again until the following evening."},{"companion":"kessa","text":"Kessa: \\"I''ve done worse for less. I''m still not going to say it was fine.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Take the fee.","end":true}]}]}}',0,1,0,NULL),
('street_almoner','Dagen Wyke','Almoner','Keeps the parish dole and the list of who is on it. Has kept the list nineteen years and has never yet crossed a name off for the right reason.','assets/images/npcs/cleric.png','dagen_wyke',1,'{"start":"greeting","nodes":{"greeting":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"almoner_list_fate","equals":"fed"}],"once":true,"text":"The parish box is behind him on the step and it is full, which it has never been in your sight or, from the way he keeps glancing at it, in his. \\"Eighty-one people eat until the middle of winter,\\" says Dagen Wyke. \\"I have written your name in the front of the book, where the benefactors go, and there are four names in there and two of them are dead.\\"","choices":[{"label":"And after the middle of winter?","next":"after_winter"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"almoner_list_fate","equals":"recorded"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"It is in the watch day book,\\" says Wyke. \\"Thirty-six names, the date, and Reed''s own hand.\\" He is standing rather straighter than he was. \\"It fed nobody. I have thought about that every day. And I have also thought that when they come for the next thirty-six, there will already be a page.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Do you think there will be a next thirty-six?","next":"next_thirty_six"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"almoner_list_fate","equals":"work"}],"once":true,"text":"Wyke has the book open on his knee and is drawing a line through names, one at a time, with a ruler. He does it very carefully. \\"Twenty-two,\\" he says. \\"Off my list, into wages.\\" The ruler moves. \\"I have been an almoner nineteen years and I have wanted nothing in the world so much as to draw these lines, and I have never once felt worse doing anything.\\"","choices":[{"label":"They are eating.","next":"they_are_eating"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_almoners_list","stage":"the_west_gate"}],"text":"\\"You have been to Ballow,\\" says Wyke, reading it off you. \\"Then you know what I know, which is not enough to accuse anybody of anything and is a great deal too much to go home with.\\"","choices":[{"label":"I''ll pay the dole. Seventy-five gold, into the box.","conditions":[{"gold_at_least":75}],"next":"into_the_box"},{"label":"Take the list to Reed. Get it written down.","next":"to_the_day_book"},{"label":"They don''t need alms. They need work.","next":"into_wages"},{"label":"I need to think about it.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_almoners_list","status":"active"}],"text":"\\"West gate,\\" says Wyke, before you can ask. \\"Whoever has stood on that gate has watched every one of them walk in. I have never spoken to the watch in my life about anything and I would not know how to begin.\\"","choices":[{"label":"I''ll go and ask.","end":true},{"label":"Why won''t you go yourself?","next":"why_not_himself"}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"west_holdings_recorded"}],"text":"\\"Alms for the parish,\\" says Wyke, and then, seeing you: \\"You. Yes.\\" He touches the book under his arm. \\"It is written down. I keep telling myself that as though it were a meal.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"text":"\\"Alms for the granary families. Hollis weighs the grain; someone must weigh the cost.\\" A thin man of about sixty with a box on a strap and a book under his arm, and the book is very much fatter than the box.","choices":[{"label":"What''s in the book?","next":"the_book"},{"label":"Here — a copper for the box.","next":"a_copper"},{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}],"a_copper":[{"text":"It goes into the box and he says the words he has said for nineteen years and does not perform them at all, which is somehow more affecting than if he had.","choices":[{"label":"What''s in the book?","next":"the_book"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}],"the_book":[{"text":"\\"Who is being fed,\\" says Dagen Wyke. \\"Every parish keeps one. It is not a secret; it is nailed to the church door on quarter days, because a man on the dole is on it in public or he is a man being given money in the dark, and the second one ruins people.\\"\\n\\nHe hands it over without being asked, which tells you a good deal about him.\\n\\nEighty-one names in a hand that gets smaller down the page to save paper. And then, because the entries carry dates, the thing he has plainly been unable to stop seeing: forty of them added since Candlemas.","interjections":[{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric reads it over your shoulder for a long time. \\"Forty in eight months. In a year with no plague and no fire and a good harvest.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Forty in eight months.","next":"forty_names"},{"label":"Give it back.","end":true}]}],"forty_names":[{"text":"\\"Forty,\\" Wyke agrees. \\"And here is the part I have not said to anybody, because I do not know what saying it would be.\\"\\n\\nHe turns the book round and runs a finger down the last column, which is the address each of them gave when they came on.\\n\\n\\"Thirty-six of the forty gave a holding west of the river. Not a street in this town. A holding.\\" He closes the book. \\"A bad year takes farms in no particular order, sir. It takes the thin soil and the old men and the widows. It does not take thirty-six farms off one road and leave the rest of the parish alone.\\"","interjections":[{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse: \\"Off one road.\\" She says it the way she says a track. \\"That is not weather. Weather does not read a map.\\""},{"companion":"sera","conditions":[{"not":{"companion":"ilse","status":"active"}}],"text":"Sera: \\"They all sold, then. Nobody walks off a holding they still own.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"I''ll find out what happened on that road.","effects":[{"start_quest":"the_almoners_list"}],"next":"he_hands_it_over"},{"label":"Farms fail. That is what farms do.","next":"farms_fail"},{"label":"Give it back.","end":true}]}],"farms_fail":[{"text":"\\"They do,\\" says Wyke. \\"I have buried the people off failed farms for nineteen years and I know exactly what one looks like coming through that gate. It comes at night, with nothing, in ones.\\"\\n\\nHe puts the book back under his arm.\\n\\n\\"These came in daylight. In families. With their tools. A man who has lost his land does not bring his tools, because his tools have been sold. A man who has *sold* his land brings his tools, because he means to work with them somewhere else.\\" He looks down the High Street. \\"Every one of the thirty-six brought their tools.\\"","choices":[{"label":"All right. I''ll find out what happened.","effects":[{"start_quest":"the_almoners_list"}],"next":"he_hands_it_over"},{"label":"Then they will be all right.","end":true}]}],"he_hands_it_over":[{"text":"\\"Take it,\\" says Wyke, and gives you the book, which is nineteen years of his handwriting. \\"Copy what you need and bring it back. I have nothing else to give anybody today and I would rather give you something.\\"\\n\\nHe adjusts the strap of the empty box.\\n\\n\\"Go to the west gate,\\" he says. \\"Ballow has been on it four years. He has watched every one of them walk in and I should very much like to know what a man who watches for a living thought about it.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_almoners_list","stage":"read_the_list"}}],"choices":[{"label":"I''ll go.","end":true}]}],"why_not_himself":[{"text":"\\"Because I have been the parish almoner for nineteen years, and the day I am seen asking the watch questions about who owns what, I am not the almoner any more — I am a man with a position on something.\\" Wyke says it without any self-pity at all. \\"Then the box stops filling. It is not a large box. It does not take much.\\"","choices":[{"label":"I''ll go and ask.","end":true}]}],"into_the_box":[{"text":"Seventy-five gold goes into a box that has never held more than nine at once, and Dagen Wyke has to sit down on the step, and then has to stand up again because of what sitting down on the step looks like.\\n\\n\\"That is bread until the middle of winter,\\" he says. \\"For eighty-one people. I want to be very exact with you, because you have been exact with me: it is bread until the middle of winter, and it is not a single acre back, and in the spring there will be more names and I shall have to come and find you.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_almoners_list","stage":"fed_them"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"kessa","text":"Kessa: \\"Seventy-five gold.\\" She is quiet a moment. \\"That''s the most useful anything I''ve watched anyone do with money.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Then come and find me.","end":true}]}],"to_the_day_book":[{"text":"Reed reads the list standing up, at the well, with the market going on round her, and then takes it inside and copies all thirty-six names into the watch day book with the date and the road and the words *given as last address*.\\n\\nThat is the whole of her power and she says so, without bitterness, while she is writing.\\n\\n\\"A man may sell his land,\\" says Captain Elowen Reed. \\"A man may buy it. Neither of them has to tell me why.\\" She blots the page. \\"But there is a page now, and pages are read later by people who are not me.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_almoners_list","stage":"named_the_pattern"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric: \\"Nineteen years he has carried that book about and never once thought to add up his own column. Do not be hard on him. Nobody adds up their own column.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"It''s a start.","end":true}]}],"into_wages":[{"text":"It takes eleven days and a good deal of standing in yards. The granary takes nine. The stone yard takes six. The quay takes four, and Mott Wenn takes three more on his own say-so and does not tell anybody he is paying two of them out of his own carrying money.\\n\\nTwenty-two names come off Dagen Wyke''s list.\\n\\nHe rules a line through each one, slowly, with a ruler, and does not look pleased.\\n\\n\\"They eat,\\" he says. \\"Say it back to me. I need somebody else to say it.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_almoners_list","stage":"found_them_work"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse: \\"Four acres and a roof, and now a wage. That road only runs one way and everybody on it knows.\\""},{"companion":"sera","conditions":[{"not":{"companion":"ilse","status":"active"}}],"text":"Sera: \\"It is the right answer. It is also the answer somebody was waiting for.\\" She does not elaborate."}],"choices":[{"label":"They eat.","next":"they_are_eating"},{"label":"Say it yourself.","end":true}]}],"they_are_eating":[{"text":"\\"They are eating,\\" Wyke repeats, and rules another line. \\"And every one of them owned four acres and a roof at Candlemas and now owns a wage, and I have never in nineteen years seen that go the other way round.\\"\\n\\nHe caps the ink.\\n\\n\\"You did what could be done,\\" he says. \\"I would like it recorded that I said so, in case I am unpleasant about it later, which I expect I shall be.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}],"after_winter":[{"text":"\\"After the middle of winter, there is no more bread.\\" Wyke says it as a fact, because it is one. \\"I am not asking. You have done a great deal more than anybody has done for that box in my time, and a box is a box: it is a thing you fill again.\\"\\n\\nHe looks down the High Street toward the west gate.\\n\\n\\"What I should like,\\" he says, \\"is for there to be fewer names. I do not know anybody who can do that. I am not certain there is anybody.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}],"next_thirty_six":[{"text":"\\"There is a road,\\" says Wyke, \\"and it has more farms on it, and every one of the thirty-six sold to somebody, and not one of them will say who.\\" He touches the book. \\"So yes. I think there will be a next thirty-six, and I think it will be quiet, and I think it will be entirely lawful, and I think that is what has been keeping me awake rather than any of the rest of it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}]}}',0,1,0,NULL),
('street_cutpurse','Rilk','Loiterer','Stands at the east gate looking at hands. Sleeps somewhere the watch has never found and is very calm about it.','assets/images/npcs/rogue.png','rilk',1,'{"start":"greeting","nodes":{"greeting":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"undertown_closed"}],"text":"Rilk is looking at hands, which is what Rilk does, and yours are not among them. He does not acknowledge you at all, and when you move he moves the same distance, keeping the gate arch between the two of you the whole time.","choices":[{"label":"Leave him.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"undertown_court_fate"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"Went down, then,\\" says Rilk, still watching the gate crowd. \\"Stood in front of Aggie.\\" He does not look round. \\"Nobody up here will ever know you did that, and nobody down there will ever forget it, and that is the exchange rate and it is not a good one and I told you so.\\"","choices":[{"label":"You were right about the walk.","next":"the_walk"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_undertown_court","status":"active"}],"text":"\\"Third grating past the tall doors on Granary Row,\\" says Rilk, without moving his lips much. \\"Or go in the way you have been going in, which everybody down there knows about, and which is the difference between arriving and being brought.\\"","choices":[{"label":"I''ll go down.","end":true},{"label":"Come with me.","next":"he_will_not_come"}]},{"conditions":[{"level_at_least":3},{"any":[{"flag":"undertown_known"},{"flag":"sump_rats_beaten"},{"flag":"weir_gremlins_beaten"},{"flag":"strongroom_cleared"},{"flag":"flood_gate_fate"}]}],"text":"Rilk is not looking at hands today. Rilk is looking at you, and has been since you came up the High Street, which is a change.\\n\\n\\"You have been down,\\" he says. \\"Do not bother. Everybody down there knows exactly who has been down and for how long and with how many lamps, because that is the only thing anybody down there has got to talk about.\\"\\n\\nHe moves off the wall.\\n\\n\\"Aggie Slate has your name,\\" he says. \\"And she has asked me — asked, mind, which she did not have to — whether you would come down and have it explained to your face.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Explained by whom?","next":"the_court_explained"},{"label":"And if I don''t?","next":"if_you_dont"},{"label":"Not interested.","end":true}]},{"text":"\\"Wasn''t me. Whatever it was.\\" He is leaning on the gate arch watching the crowd, and he is not watching faces. \\"Streets are full of thieves. Mind your purse.\\"","choices":[{"label":"You''re watching hands, not faces.","next":"watching_hands"},{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}],"watching_hands":[{"text":"That gets you a proper look for the first time.\\n\\n\\"Faces lie,\\" he says. \\"Hands are doing whatever they are doing.\\" He goes back to the crowd. \\"Rilk. And before you ask, the watch has had me up four times in six years and let me go four times, because I have never once been carrying anything.\\"\\n\\nA cart goes through the arch.\\n\\n\\"That is not luck,\\" says Rilk. \\"That is having somewhere to put things down that nobody up here will look.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Where''s that?","next":"he_will_not_say"},{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}],"he_will_not_say":[{"text":"\\"No,\\" says Rilk, and it is not even unfriendly. \\"You are all right. I have watched you cross this gate about nine times and your hands are perfectly ordinary. But you are from up, and everything I have got is that you are not going to find out where I sleep.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Fair enough.","end":true}]}],"the_court_explained":[{"text":"\\"There is a court down there,\\" says Rilk. \\"That is the word and it is the right one, so do not make the face.\\"\\n\\nHe leans back on the arch.\\n\\n\\"Sixty-odd people, one room, and a woman on a plank who has sat it eleven years because nobody down there can think of anyone else who would be believed.\\" He shrugs. \\"No lockup. No gallows. Nowhere to put anybody. So it happens in the room, in front of everybody, or it happens in a tunnel with a hooked pole, and Aggie Slate has spent eleven years making it the first one.\\"\\n\\nA pause.\\n\\n\\"She would like it to be the first one with you,\\" he says. \\"That is not a threat. She has genuinely gone to some trouble to make it not be a threat, which I would think about, if I were the sort of person anybody went to trouble for.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Take me down.","effects":[{"start_quest":"the_undertown_court"}],"next":"he_walks_you_in"},{"label":"And if I don''t?","next":"if_you_dont"},{"label":"Not interested.","end":true}]}],"if_you_dont":[{"text":"\\"Then nothing happens,\\" says Rilk, \\"which is what everybody expects, and which is why she asked me instead of sending anybody.\\"\\n\\nHe watches a hand near a purse and decides against saying anything about it.\\n\\n\\"You go on walking about under a town where sixty people live, being talked about, and one night you are somewhere narrow and there are four of them and it is not a court, it is a tunnel.\\" He shrugs. \\"I am not saying that to frighten you. I am saying it because it is the exact thing Aggie has spent eleven years trying to stop happening, and she would rather it did not start with you.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Take me down.","effects":[{"start_quest":"the_undertown_court"}],"next":"he_walks_you_in"},{"label":"Not interested.","end":true}]}],"he_walks_you_in":[{"text":"\\"Right,\\" says Rilk, and looks genuinely relieved, which he covers badly.\\n\\n\\"Third grating past the tall doors on Granary Row, or your own way in — but if you come your own way, come to the lamps and stand where you can be seen, and do not go along the wall.\\" He straightens his coat. \\"People who come in along the wall are people who have decided something.\\"\\n\\nHe steps back into the crowd at the arch.\\n\\n\\"I will be there,\\" he says, \\"and I will not say anything, because I am not anybody. That is not modesty. There is a rule about who may speak and I am on the wrong side of it and I have been for four years.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_undertown_court","stage":"go_down_with_rilk"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Why the wrong side of it?","next":"the_wrong_side"},{"label":"I''ll come to the lamps.","end":true}]}],"the_wrong_side":[{"text":"\\"Because I take purses up here and bring the money down there,\\" says Rilk, \\"and Aggie''s second line is that the bench is whoever has least to gain, and by that reckoning I am the last man in that room who ought to open his mouth about a stranger.\\"\\n\\nHe almost smiles.\\n\\n\\"She has never once made me stop,\\" he says. \\"She has only made it so that nobody has to listen to me. There is a difference and it took me about two years to be grateful for it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"I''ll come to the lamps.","end":true}]}],"he_will_not_come":[{"text":"\\"I shall be there,\\" says Rilk. \\"I am not walking through the east gate beside you in daylight, because half my trade is that the watch has stopped noticing me.\\" He goes back to the crowd. \\"Come to the lamps. Do not go along the wall.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Understood.","end":true}]}],"the_walk":[{"text":"\\"Everybody says that afterwards,\\" says Rilk. \\"Nobody believes it beforehand.\\"\\n\\nHe watches the arch.\\n\\n\\"It is not the room,\\" he says. \\"The room is only a room. It is the walk down, when you have got about two hundred feet left and you are still deciding whether you are the sort of person who is going to arrive.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}]}}',0,1,0,NULL),
('street_drover','A drover','Townsfolk',NULL,'assets/images/npcs/barbarian.png','barbarian',1,'{"start":"hail","nodes":{"hail":[{"text":"Wagons won''t run the east road till it''s safe. My oxen agree with the wagons.","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}]}}',0,0,1,NULL),
('street_minstrel','A minstrel','Townsfolk',NULL,'assets/images/npcs/bard.png','bard',1,'{"start":"hail","nodes":{"hail":[{"text":"Requests taken, except the one about the goblin king. Wrote it, regret it.","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}]}}',0,0,1,NULL),
('street_scholar','A scholar','Townsfolk',NULL,'assets/images/npcs/wizard.png','wizard',1,'{"start":"hail","nodes":{"hail":[{"text":"The mill ledger, the granary tallies — numbers are the truest gossip in this town.","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}]}}',0,0,1,NULL),
('street_seer','Nan Vetch','Fortune-teller','Reads the water and the weather for coppers on the High Street. Came up out of the fen thirty years ago and has never gone back to look.','assets/images/npcs/sorcerer.png','sorcerer',1,'{"start":"greeting","nodes":{"greeting":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"seers_coin_fate","equals":"stopped"}],"text":"Nan Vetch has moved her stool six feet along the wall, which in her trade is an act of some seriousness, and there is a girl of about nineteen doing cards outside the Flagon where Nan used to sit.\\n\\n\\"Weather,\\" says Nan. \\"That is all I do now, and there is no living in weather, and I sleep.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Who''s the girl?","next":"the_girl"},{"label":"Read the weather, then.","next":"the_weather"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"seers_coin_fate","equals":"turned"}],"once":true,"text":"Nan Vetch has a new shawl and the same stool. \\"Sit down and tell me what I saw,\\" she says, perfectly pleasantly, and holds out her hand for the copper anyway, because the copper is the part that makes it a reading.","choices":[{"label":"Are you still taking the Tuesday silver?","next":"still_taking_it"},{"label":"Nothing today.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"seers_coin_fate","equals":"traced"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"You went up the stair after him,\\" says Nan Vetch, before you have sat down. \\"And you found a boy at a desk who is paid by the letter and has never met anybody, and you came back down here and you did not know what to do with your hands.\\" She snorts. \\"That is not the sight. That is having watched four people do it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Will you stop?","next":"will_you_stop"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_seers_coin","stage":"traced_the_hand"}],"text":"\\"Well?\\" Nan Vetch does not look up from her cloth. \\"Whose hand was it? I have wondered for four months and I have never once been able to make myself follow him, which I would like you to think about before you say anything clever to me.\\"","choices":[{"label":"A copyist paid by the letter, who has never met anyone.","next":"the_copyist_answer"}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_seers_coin","stage":"follow_the_coin"}],"text":"\\"Tuesdays,\\" says Nan. \\"Between the ninth and the tenth bell, from the counting-house end, a man with a leather satchel and a very ordinary coat. He does not stop. He puts it in my dish going past and he has never once said good morning.\\"","choices":[{"label":"I''ll wait for him.","end":true},{"label":"Take my money instead and say what I want said.","conditions":[{"gold_at_least":30}],"next":"she_takes_your_coin"}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_seers_coin","status":"active"}],"text":"\\"You have come back,\\" says Nan Vetch, \\"which four hundred people have not, and you have come back with a question rather than a copper, which is worse for me and better for you.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Who pays you to say the river is coming?","next":"she_admits_it"},{"label":"Nothing today.","end":true}]},{"text":"\\"Your fortune? Cross the river, mind the ford. That one is free; the rest cost.\\"\\n\\nAn old woman on a stool against the wall with a cloth over her knees, doing very good business, mostly with people in farm boots.","choices":[{"label":"A copper, then. Read me.","next":"the_reading"},{"label":"Why is your queue all farmers?","next":"all_farmers"},{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}],"the_reading":[{"text":"She takes the copper, turns your hand over, does not look at it, and looks at your boots instead — which is, you understand a moment later, where the actual information is.\\n\\n\\"Road,\\" she says. \\"Bad ground east and worse ground west, and you will go both ways twice before the year is out.\\"\\n\\nThen, without any change of tone at all, in the voice of a woman reciting:\\n\\n\\"And the river is coming back for the west bank. It has done it twice in my life. A wise man sells while the ground is still worth something.\\"\\n\\nShe stops. She looks up at you properly for the first time, because you are not wearing farm boots, and something goes across her face that is a great deal like being caught.","interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera, afterwards: \\"She said the last part differently. Flatter. That is a line somebody gave her.\\""},{"companion":"ilse","conditions":[{"not":{"companion":"sera","status":"active"}}],"text":"Ilse: \\"She has said that four hundred times. You can hear the grooves in it.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"You have said that before.","effects":[{"start_quest":"the_seers_coin"}],"next":"she_admits_it"},{"label":"Thank you.","end":true}]}],"all_farmers":[{"text":"\\"Because farmers are the only people left who pay for weather,\\" says Nan Vetch. \\"Townsmen want love and money. Farmers want to know when the water is coming, and I am very good at that, and I have been right about it more often than the almanac.\\"\\n\\nShe puts out her hand for the copper.","choices":[{"label":"A copper, then. Read me.","next":"the_reading"},{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}],"she_admits_it":[{"text":"\\"Four hundred and some times,\\" says Nan Vetch, \\"since Candlemas, to every pair of farm boots that has sat down in front of me off the west road.\\"\\n\\nShe does not pretend, and she does not apologise, and both of those take you a moment to catch up with.\\n\\n\\"Two silver a week. In advance. Tuesdays.\\" She folds the cloth. \\"And before you say the thing you are about to say — I have never once said anything untrue. The flats *do* go under. They went under when I was eleven and again when I was thirty-four and they will go under again, and any old woman on this street would tell you so for nothing.\\"\\n\\nA pause.\\n\\n\\"Somebody is paying me to keep saying so,\\" she says, \\"at exactly the season it is worth the most to them. That is the whole of what I have done, and I have thought about it every Tuesday for four months.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_seers_coin","stage":"sit_for_a_reading"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"kessa","text":"Kessa: \\"Two silver a week to say a true thing on a schedule.\\" She sounds almost admiring. \\"That''s cheap.\\""},{"companion":"aldric","conditions":[{"not":{"companion":"kessa","status":"active"}}],"text":"Aldric: \\"You have not lied. You have been aimed.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Who brings the silver?","next":"the_tuesday_man"},{"label":"Stop taking it.","next":"why_should_she"},{"label":"Take mine instead, and say what I tell you.","conditions":[{"gold_at_least":30}],"next":"she_takes_your_coin"}]}],"the_tuesday_man":[{"text":"\\"A man with a satchel, from the counting-house end, between the ninth and the tenth bell.\\" Nan tucks the cloth under her knees. \\"He does not stop and he has never once said good morning, and I could no more follow him than fly, on account of the stool and the knees and the fact that I would lose a Tuesday.\\"\\n\\nShe looks up.\\n\\n\\"You could,\\" she says.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_seers_coin","stage":"follow_the_coin"}}],"choices":[{"label":"I''ll wait for him.","end":true},{"label":"Take mine instead, and say what I tell you.","conditions":[{"gold_at_least":30}],"next":"she_takes_your_coin"}]}],"why_should_she":[{"text":"\\"With what,\\" says Nan Vetch, and there it is again, that sentence, the one this whole town seems to be built on. \\"I am sixty-eight. The stool is mine and the shawl is mine and that is the inventory.\\"\\n\\nShe softens fractionally, which on Nan is enormous.\\n\\n\\"I did not come up out of the fen at fourteen and keep myself for fifty-four years by turning down two silver a week for telling the truth. If you want me to stop, you had better be able to tell me what stops instead.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Then stop. I''ll see you''re not out of pocket.","conditions":[{"gold_at_least":20}],"next":"she_gives_it_back"},{"label":"Tell me who brings the silver instead.","next":"the_tuesday_man"},{"label":"Then keep taking it.","end":true}]}],"she_gives_it_back":[{"text":"She gives the last week''s silver back.\\n\\nYou would not think two coins could take that long to hand over. She has to be talked into the fourth attempt, and she is furious about it in a way that has nothing to do with you, and when it is finally done she sits with her hands in her lap and says, \\"Fifty-four years and I have never given anything back.\\"\\n\\nWithin a fortnight there is a girl of nineteen doing cards outside the Flagon who tells anybody in farm boots that the water is coming. Nan watches her work for a while from six feet along the wall.\\n\\n\\"She is not very good,\\" says Nan Vetch. \\"She will get better.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_seers_coin","stage":"she_stopped"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse: \\"Two silver a week is not a person. It is a place in a list. Places in lists get filled.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"It was still worth doing.","end":true}]}],"she_takes_your_coin":[{"text":"\\"Thirty gold,\\" says Nan Vetch, and does not count it, which is a courtesy and also a way of not being seen to. \\"Tell me what you want said, and to whom, and I shall say it to them the way I say things — which is nine parts weather and one part the thing you paid for, because that is the whole trade and the rest of it is theatre.\\"\\n\\nA pause.\\n\\n\\"And I shall keep the Tuesday silver,\\" she says, evenly. \\"You knew that before you sat down. I could see you knowing it.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_seers_coin","stage":"turned_her"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric: \\"There are now two people paying an old woman to point a town in a direction. That is not an improvement. That is a competition.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"I knew.","end":true}]}],"the_copyist_answer":[{"text":"Nan Vetch listens to the whole thing and then laughs, once, without much in it.\\n\\n\\"So there is nobody,\\" she says. \\"There is a boy at a desk being paid by the letter, and a satchel, and a Tuesday. Somewhere at the far end of it there is a person, and between me and them there are four rooms full of people who have all done nothing wrong.\\"\\n\\nShe pulls the shawl up.\\n\\n\\"That is the cleverest thing I have ever heard of,\\" she says. \\"And I have spent fifty years being paid for knowing what people are up to.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Will you stop?","next":"will_you_stop"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}],"will_you_stop":[{"text":"\\"I shall say it slower,\\" says Nan Vetch. \\"That is what I have got. If I say it slower and I put more weather round it, fewer of them will hear it as advice.\\"\\n\\nShe looks down the High Street at nothing in particular.\\n\\n\\"It is not much of a rebellion,\\" she says. \\"It is what a woman on a stool has got.\\"","choices":[{"label":"It counts.","end":true}]}],"still_taking_it":[{"text":"\\"Every Tuesday,\\" says Nan, \\"and yours as well, and I have not lied to a single soul in either direction.\\" She adjusts the new shawl. \\"You have bought a woman who tells the truth for money. There is nothing in that arrangement that will ever break, and you should be a good deal more uneasy about that than you look.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Nothing today.","end":true}]}],"the_girl":[{"text":"\\"Petra Coule. Her mother did cards on the ford road until the ford road stopped having anybody on it.\\" Nan watches her deal. \\"She got the Tuesday within a fortnight of my giving it up, which tells you they were not looking for me. They were looking for a stool.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Read the weather, then.","next":"the_weather"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}],"the_weather":[{"text":"\\"Rain by Thursday, hard, off the west, and it will not last,\\" says Nan Vetch. \\"And the flats will go under one of these years, and I shall be dead, and it will still be true.\\"\\n\\nShe holds out her hand for the copper.","choices":[{"label":"Worth a copper.","end":true}]}]}}',0,1,0,NULL),
('street_stranger','A stranger','Townsfolk',NULL,'assets/images/npcs/warlock.png','warlock',1,'{"start":"hail","nodes":{"hail":[{"text":"I''m just passing through. Everyone in this town is just passing through, slowly.","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}]}}',0,0,1,NULL),
('tam_ryke','Tam Ryke','Sailmaker''s forewoman','Runs twenty women, a hundred metres of cutting floor and the best information network in Waerhaven, out of a loft with its windows on the quay. What she knows is not for sale at any price. She will trade it, once, for help with something the Watch will not touch.','assets/images/npcs/skulker.png','skulker',1,'{"start":"greeting","nodes":{"greeting":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"charter_fate","equals":"seized"}],"text":"The cutting floor is quiet. Twenty women are working and nobody is at the windows, which in this loft is a statement.\\n\\n\\"Eighty quarters into the League''s granary,\\" Tam Ryke says, cutting. \\"And nineteen hundred people outside the wall who were being fed off it and are not being fed off anything now.\\"\\n\\nShe does not look up.\\n\\n\\"I asked you to find out what it was. I did not ask you to decide.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"It was a theft.\\"","next":"the_argument"},{"label":"\\"You are right. I decided.\\"","next":"she_accepts_it"},{"label":"Leave her to it.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"charter_fate","equals":"burned"}],"text":"Tam Ryke is at the window and has been for some time.\\n\\n\\"Six years that ran under my floor,\\" she says, \\"and I watched every load of it come up out of that stair, and I have never told a living soul, and now there is nothing to tell.\\"\\n\\nShe turns round.\\n\\n\\"I do not know whether to thank you. I have been trying to decide since Thursday and I do not know.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Say nothing.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"charter_fate","equals":"bought_in"}],"text":"\\"You are in it,\\" Tam says. She is cutting, and the chalk does not stop. \\"That is fine. That is one of the outcomes I costed.\\"\\n\\nShe marks a curve.\\n\\n\\"Twenty women watch that quay. If it starts riding lower going out, I will come and find you rather than somebody else, and that is not a threat, that is the only favour I have got left to do you.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Understood.\\"","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"deep_store_seen"}],"text":"Tam Ryke sends the nearest three women to the far end of the loft with a look, and they go without a word and without hurrying.\\n\\n\\"You went down,\\" she says. \\"Tell me the room.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"It is a granary. A better one than the League''s.\\"","next":"she_hears_it"},{"label":"\\"League grain. Six years of it. Off the top of every barge.\\"","next":"she_hears_it"},{"label":"Not yet.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"what_goes_out","status":"active"}],"text":"\\"Still up here,\\" says Tam, cutting. \\"The stair is behind the warehouses off Doss''s quay and the ebb is at four. I cannot tell you any of that on a quay and I have just told you all of it in a room with twenty witnesses who will all swear I was talking about canvas.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back to it.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"met_ryke"}],"text":"\\"Back,\\" says Tam Ryke. The chalk does not stop. \\"Mind the bolt, it is wet-marked.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"What have your windows seen?\\"","next":"the_windows"},{"label":"\\"Tell me about the crane.\\"","next":"what_goes_out_q"},{"label":"Another time.","end":true}]},{"text":"A long upper floor with the windows on the quay side and a hundred metres of canvas on it, and twenty women working, and not one of them looks up when you come in, which takes practice.\\n\\nThe forewoman marks a curve, straightens, and looks at you over the chalk.\\n\\n\\"Tam Ryke. Sailmaker''s loft. Whatever you are about to ask me — no, it is not for sale, and yes, we did see it.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"met_ryke"}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"Saw what?\\"","next":"the_windows"},{"label":"\\"Not for sale at any price?\\"","next":"not_for_sale"},{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}],"not_for_sale":[{"text":"\\"Not for sale at any price,\\" Tam says, \\"and I will tell you exactly why, because everybody assumes it is honour and it is arithmetic.\\"\\n\\n\\"Twenty women. Eleven years. What we have got is not a fact here and a fact there — it is eleven years of the same quay, compared every day at this table, by twenty people who all know when one of the others is guessing.\\"\\n\\nShe marks another curve.\\n\\n\\"Sell one piece of it and every woman on this floor knows that what she says at this table can end up on a quay. And then they stop saying it, and then in about a fortnight I have got twenty sailmakers and no loft.\\"\\n\\n\\"You cannot sell it. It only exists because it is not for sale.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Then what would you trade it for?\\"","next":"the_trade"},{"label":"\\"What have the windows seen?\\"","next":"the_windows"}]}],"the_windows":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"charter_known"}],"text":"\\"You have been talking to Doss,\\" Tam says. \\"Then you know as much as the quay knows and considerably less than this floor does.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"So tell me the rest.\\"","next":"what_goes_out_q"},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]},{"text":"\\"Everything,\\" says Tam, without any drama at all. \\"That is the honest answer and it is useless to you, so here is a useful one.\\"\\n\\n\\"Six years ago the Wardenswatch was a lazar house and a cart track. It is nineteen hundred people now. Every one of them came in past that gate or off a boat, and about half of them came off a boat, and boats are ours.\\"\\n\\nShe puts the chalk down, which she has not done.\\n\\n\\"People coming in is a cost. I have been watching that cost be paid for six years and I have never once seen where the money comes from, and I am the person in this city best placed to see it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"And that bothers you.\\"","next":"what_goes_out_q"},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]}],"what_goes_out_q":[{"text":"\\"The Great Crane lifts a load twice a year that nobody logs,\\" Tam says. \\"That is common quay gossip and it is not what I have got.\\"\\n\\n\\"What I have got is this. Twice a year a lighter comes off the outer quay under that crane. Twenty women have watched it every time for six years. And every single time, that lighter is riding lower going out than she was coming in.\\"\\n\\nShe lets that sit.\\n\\n\\"Going out. Loaded. From a crane that is supposedly lifting something in.\\"\\n\\n\\"I cannot say that on a quay. I cannot say it to the Watch, who are bought, or to the Moot, who would want to know how I know, or to the Factor, who would write it down. So it has been sitting in this loft for six years being true.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"I will go and look.\\"","next":"the_trade"},{"label":"\\"Why me?\\"","next":"why_you"},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]}],"why_you":[{"text":"\\"Because you have got no loft,\\" Tam says. \\"No bench, no gang, no family in Old Waer, no debt to Ilber Cass, nothing on the ways and nobody to be got at.\\"\\n\\n\\"There are twenty-six thousand people in this city and every one of them is holding something they cannot afford to have shaken. That is not a moral failing. That is what a city is.\\"\\n\\nShe picks the chalk up.\\n\\n\\"And then somebody walks in off the Hallowgate road with nothing in the water at all. Do you know how rare that is? I have been waiting six years for a pair of hands with nothing in the water.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"All right.\\"","next":"the_trade"},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]}],"the_trade":[{"text":"\\"Then here is the trade,\\" says Tam Ryke, \\"and it is the only one I do.\\"\\n\\n\\"You go under the quay and you find out what is down there, and you come back here and tell me — me, this floor, first, before the Moot and before the Factor and before whichever of them you have decided you like.\\"\\n\\n\\"And in exchange you get eleven years of what twenty women have seen out of these windows. Once. Whatever you ask me, I answer, and I answer straight.\\"\\n\\nShe holds out a hand with chalk on it.\\n\\n\\"That is a better price than anybody in Waerhaven has ever been offered and I want you to know I know it.\\"","on_enter":[{"start_quest":"what_goes_out"}],"choices":[{"label":"Take the hand.","end":true}]}],"she_hears_it":[{"text":"Tam Ryke listens all the way through without cutting, which she has not done once since you met her.\\n\\n\\"A granary,\\" she repeats.\\n\\nShe sets the shears down.\\n\\n\\"Six years. Off the top of every barge, before the Factor''s seals ever touch it, and put somewhere with new boards and a broom in the corner.\\"\\n\\n\\"I costed four things it could be. Smuggled arms. Charts. Somebody''s private fortune. A slave run.\\" She almost laughs. \\"I did not cost bread. I have watched that lighter ride low for six years and I never once thought that somebody down there was doing the one thing this city refuses to do, which is plan for a winter.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"what_goes_out","stage":"whose_grain"}}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"It is still the League''s grain.\\"","next":"the_argument"},{"label":"\\"Who is keeping it?\\"","next":"the_name"}]}],"the_name":[{"text":"\\"I do not know,\\" Tam says, and it visibly costs her. \\"Eleven years of windows and I do not know.\\"\\n\\n\\"Three names get said and I can tell you which two are wrong. Ilber Cass wants it to be him so badly that it cannot be — he has been dropping hints about the Charter for four years and no man running a thing like that would. And Vennik reports to Lathmere, and Lathmere is the one party in the world that must never be told.\\"\\n\\nShe looks out of the window at the quay.\\n\\n\\"The third name I have never heard said, because nobody has one. There is a book on that table in nine hands. Nine. That is not a person, that is a rota. Whoever set that up did it so that there would be no name to give.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Then I will go and sit in the room until somebody comes.\\"","end":true},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]}],"the_argument":[{"text":"\\"It is the League''s grain,\\" Tam agrees. \\"It is. And the League''s grain is bought with the League''s money out of the League''s tax on a city that is not allowed to have nineteen hundred of the people who are eating it.\\"\\n\\nShe picks the shears back up.\\n\\n\\"I am not telling you what to do. I asked you to find out and you found out, and you did the part of it I could not do and I am not going to now stand here and tell you the answer. That would be the cheapest thing anybody has done in this loft in eleven years.\\"\\n\\n\\"But do not tell me it is simple. You have been in that room. It is not simple and you know it is not, and I would think a good deal less of you if you came up out of there certain.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"I am not certain.\\"","effects":[{"xp":60}],"end":true},{"label":"Say nothing.","end":true}]}],"she_accepts_it":[{"text":"Tam Ryke stops cutting. She stands with both hands flat on the canvas for a while.\\n\\n\\"Yes,\\" she says. \\"You did. And somebody was always going to, and it was never going to be me, because I have twenty women and a loft and I have spent eleven years being the person who watches.\\"\\n\\nShe straightens.\\n\\n\\"That is my share of it and I will carry it. Come up here again. I said once and I meant once, and I have decided that was a stupid thing to say to the only person who has ever come back up those stairs to tell me what they did.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"I will come back.\\"","effects":[{"xp":80}],"end":true},{"label":"Go.","end":true}]}]}}',0,1,0,NULL),
('tar_gaol_prisoner','A prisoner','Prisoner',NULL,'assets/images/npcs/prisoner_1.png','prisoner_1',1,'{"start":"greeting","nodes":{"greeting":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"gaol_opened"}],"text":"The doors are open and most of them have gone and this one has not.\\n\\n\\"Where,\\" he says, reasonably. \\"That is the whole of it. Everybody keeps saying go. Go where. I was picking oakum outside the wall for a token before I was picking it in here for nothing, and the shed is the same shed.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]},{"pool":"idle","conditions":[{"flag":"met_reth"}],"text":"\\"Held for what?\\" the prisoner says through the grille. \\"Ask them. Go on, ask them, they cannot say it either. There is no word for it. I am here because there is no word for what I am.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]},{"pool":"idle","conditions":[{"flag":"met_reth"}],"text":"He is picking oakum. It is four in the afternoon, nobody has told him to, and there is nothing in it for him.\\n\\n\\"Hands want something,\\" he says, when he sees you notice.","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]},{"pool":"idle","conditions":[{"flag":"met_reth"}],"text":"\\"That slate,\\" he says, nodding at the turnkey''s board. \\"That is the whole record of every one of us. Wiped and written, wiped and written. You could walk out of here and out of the world and it would take a wet rag.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]},{"text":"A face at the grille of the second room, patient in a way that has taken practice.\\n\\n\\"You are not the Houses,\\" he says. \\"You are not the Watch either, they do not come in here. So you are lost, or you are looking for somebody. I hope you are looking for somebody.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}]}}',0,0,1,NULL),
('tibald_ashe','Tibald Ashe','Counting-House Clerk','Keeps Rivermark''s town account. Will tell you anything that is written down and nothing that is not.','assets/images/npcs/monk.png','tibald_ashe',1,'{"start":"greeting","nodes":{"greeting":[{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_anvil_debt","stage":"the_assignments"}],"text":"The clerk at the second desk has been watching you come across the floor since the door. He is a narrow man of about fifty with ink to the second knuckle and no expression whatever, and when you put Brenna Coalwright''s note down in front of him he reads all of it, both sides, before he says a single word.\\n\\n\\"Tibald Ashe,\\" he says. \\"You will want to know whether the fourth assignment is any good.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Is it?","next":"the_fourth_assignment"},{"label":"You knew what this was before you read it.","next":"he_knew"}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_watch_payroll","stage":"ask_the_clerk"}],"text":"\\"The watch roll.\\" Ashe says it before you have finished asking, and puts down the pen he was using, which for a clerk is a considerable act. \\"I have been waiting fourteen months for somebody to come across this floor and say those two words to me.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Then say it.","next":"the_shortfall"},{"label":"Waiting for whom?","next":"waiting_for_whom"}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_seers_coin","stage":"sit_for_a_reading"}],"text":"\\"Two silver a week to a fortune-teller,\\" Ashe repeats, in the voice of a man reading a weight off a slate. \\"That is not the town''s money. If it were the town''s money I would know the vote it came out of, because I write the votes down.\\" A very small pause. \\"It is not the first of these you have asked me about, and it will not be the last.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Then whose money is it?","next":"the_copyist"},{"label":"How many of these are there?","next":"how_many"}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"concern_answering"}],"once":true,"text":"Ashe puts the pen down before you have crossed the floor, which he has not once done. \\"I am instructed not to draw for you,\\" he says. \\"Not refused — instructed. It is a different word and it is the correct one. I have written to ask on whose authority and I shall have an answer in about nine days that will be perfectly courteous and will name nobody.\\" He squares the ledger. \\"Your money is in this house. I am simply not the man who is allowed to hand it to you.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Then who is?","next":"he_will_not_say"},{"label":"Leave it.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"concern_asking"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"A man came to the second desk on the Tuesday and asked to see the standing orders,\\" Ashe says, and goes on writing. \\"He had every right to. He is a factor''s clerk of a house we honour paper for, he asked properly, and I fetched them.\\" He turns a page. \\"He read four years of them, copied nothing, and asked me one question, which was whether you had ever been paid out of this house. I said no. That was true on the Tuesday. I have thought about it every day since and I find that I would like it to go on being true.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Which house?","next":"he_will_not_say"},{"label":"Leave him to it.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"concern_noticed"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"There is a note against you in the margin,\\" Ashe says, without looking up and without stopping. \\"Not a debt. Not a caution. A note. It came down with the quarter''s corrections, in the same hand as the corrections, which is a hand I have read for four years and have never met.\\" He finishes the line and blots it. \\"It sets out what you have done, in the order you did it. It is accurate. That is the part I would think about.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Accurate how?","next":"he_will_not_say"},{"label":"Leave him to it.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"copyist_found"}],"text":"\\"He has given notice,\\" says Ashe, without looking up. \\"The boy upstairs. Not on my account and not on yours — the work has stopped coming, that is all. Post arrived on the Tuesday with the money in it and no letter to copy, and then no post at all.\\"\\n\\nHe turns a page.\\n\\n\\"Eleven days after you went up those stairs,\\" he says. \\"I have written the date in my own book because I could not think of anywhere else it belonged.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Does he know why?","next":"he_does_not_know_why"},{"label":"What else is on your books?","next":"the_town_account"},{"label":"Nothing today.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"ashe_helped"}],"text":"\\"The smith,\\" Ashe says, by way of hello. \\"She came in. She said thank you to four of us in turn because she did not know which one of us it had been.\\" He turns a page. \\"I did not tell her. It seemed a small enough thing to keep.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Why did you do it?","next":"why_he_did_it"},{"label":"What else is on your books?","next":"the_town_account"},{"label":"Nothing today.","end":true}]},{"text":"The clerk at the second desk finishes his line before he looks up, which takes some time, and is not rudeness — you can see him decide that stopping mid-figure would cost more than making you wait. \\"Tibald Ashe. This is the town''s account, not a bank. If you want money kept, the nearest people who will do that are at Greyhythe.\\"","choices":[{"label":"What is on the town''s account?","next":"the_town_account"},{"label":"Nothing today.","end":true}]}],"he_does_not_know_why":[{"text":"\\"He is entirely untroubled,\\" says Ashe. \\"He assumes the arrangement is finished, because arrangements finish. He asked me whether I knew of any other copying work and I said I would ask about, and I shall.\\"\\n\\nHe squares his pen against the edge of the desk.\\n\\n\\"A year and seven months of somebody else''s instructions through his hand,\\" he says, \\"and he could not tell you one thing about them that he has not already told you, because there is nothing else in them. That is not a man who was careful. That is a job that was built so that carefulness would not be needed.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Thank you.","end":true}]}],"the_town_account":[{"text":"\\"Tolls, tariffs, the market rents, the fines. Out of it: the watch, the wells, the lamp oil, the road, the parish dole and the bridge that we have been not repairing since I was thirty.\\" Ashe sets his pen down flat and square. \\"Every figure on that list is public and I will read any of it to anybody who asks. Nobody asks. In nineteen years two people have asked and one of them was the previous captain of the watch.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Who was the other?","next":"the_other_one"},{"label":"Thank you.","end":true}]}],"the_other_one":[{"text":"\\"A young man with a very good coat, three summers ago, who asked to see the market rents and the toll book and the list of holdings west of the river.\\" Ashe considers. \\"He was extremely polite. He read for two days, he took nothing away, and he thanked me by name on the way out, which I had not given him.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Did you get his?","next":"no_name"},{"label":"Thank you.","end":true}]}],"no_name":[{"text":"\\"He gave me one. It was not written anywhere and I have no reason at all to remember it, and I do not.\\" Ashe picks the pen back up. \\"That has bothered me for three years. I am extremely good at names.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Thank you.","end":true}]}],"he_knew":[{"text":"\\"She has been in three times,\\" Ashe says. \\"The first time I told her it was a matter for a lawyer, which was true and was also the sort of thing a man says when he does not want an afternoon spoiled. The second time I read it properly. The third time she did not ask me anything, she only stood there, and I have been thinking about it ever since.\\"","choices":[{"label":"So is the fourth assignment any good?","next":"the_fourth_assignment"}]}],"the_fourth_assignment":[{"text":"\\"No.\\" He turns the note over and puts two fingers on it. \\"An assignment of a secured debt wants two witnesses in this town and has done since before either of us. The first three have them. The fourth does not, and the fourth is the one that moved it out of the reach of anybody who could be argued with.\\" He looks up. \\"Which means that as far as Rivermark''s book is concerned, that debt is still owned by a corn factor in Greyhythe. Who died in the winter. Whose estate is a thicket.\\"","interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera: \\"Say that out loud in front of witnesses and it is a fact. Say it in a back room and it is an opinion.\\""},{"companion":"kessa","conditions":[{"not":{"companion":"sera","status":"active"}}],"text":"Kessa: \\"So the man taking her forty gold a quarter has no right to it.\\" A beat. \\"And has been taking it anyway.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Then strike it. Out loud, where people can hear you.","next":"he_strikes_it"},{"label":"Who would I have to argue with to buy the note outright?","next":"buying_it"},{"label":"You said that quickly for a man who has sat on it three years.","check":{"skill":"insight","dc":12,"on_success":"why_he_did_it","on_failure":"he_says_nothing"}}]}],"he_strikes_it":[{"text":"He does it there, standing, with the floor listening. He reads the fourth assignment aloud, states that it is unwitnessed and therefore not effective against the security, writes the finding in the margin of the town''s own copy in his own hand, and calls two clerks over to sign under it. It takes four minutes. Nobody raises their voice at any point.\\n\\n\\"That is all it ever was,\\" says Tibald Ashe. \\"Four minutes, and somebody willing to be the one who did it.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_anvil_debt","stage":"voided_the_note"}},{"set_flag":"ashe_helped"}],"interjections":[{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric, on the steps outside: \\"He has just made an enemy he will never meet.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Will this come back on you?","next":"come_back_on_him"},{"label":"Thank you.","end":true}]}],"come_back_on_him":[{"text":"\\"Very likely,\\" Ashe says, already back at his line. \\"Not soon, and not loudly. That is not how it is done.\\" The pen moves. \\"I am fifty-one, I have no children, and I have written this town''s arithmetic down honestly for nineteen years. I would prefer that to be the sentence rather than the other one.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Which other one?","next":"the_other_sentence"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}],"the_other_sentence":[{"text":"\\"That I noticed, and wrote it down, and left it written down.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}],"buying_it":[{"text":"\\"Nobody,\\" Ashe says. \\"That is the odd part. Small accounts a long way from home are a nuisance to whoever has them — they cost more to collect than they are worth, unless the collecting is the point. Offer a hundred and twenty and a clerk somewhere will take it inside the month to be rid of the line.\\" He does not add anything. He does not need to; it is all there in how flatly he said it.","choices":[{"label":"Then I''ll buy it.","conditions":[{"gold_at_least":120}],"next":"he_writes_the_transfer"},{"label":"No. Strike it instead.","next":"he_strikes_it"},{"label":"I''ll think about it.","end":true}]}],"he_writes_the_transfer":[{"text":"It takes nineteen days and two letters and at no point does anybody argue. The paper comes back assigned to you, witnessed twice, entirely correct.\\n\\nAshe hands it over without any expression at all. \\"There,\\" he says. \\"Now it is a small account a long way from home, and you are the one a long way from it.\\" He returns to his figures. \\"She will still pay forty a quarter. To you. Whether you ask for it or not, she will bring it, on the day, because that is what fourteen years does to a person.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_anvil_debt","stage":"bought_the_note"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"kessa","text":"Kessa says nothing the whole way back up the High Street, which is not a thing Kessa does."}],"choices":[{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]}],"why_he_did_it":[{"text":"\\"Because it is arithmetic,\\" Ashe says, \\"and because for nineteen years I have written down what people tell me and gone home.\\" He squares the pen against the edge of the desk. \\"I am not brave. I want that understood. I have simply reached the age where the things I did not say are heavier to carry about than the things I did.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Then strike the fourth assignment.","conditions":[{"quest":"the_anvil_debt","stage":"the_assignments"}],"next":"he_strikes_it"},{"label":"That is braver than you think.","end":true}]}],"he_says_nothing":[{"text":"\\"I read what is in front of me and I say what it says,\\" Ashe replies, and goes back to his line, and that is a door closing.","choices":[{"label":"Then strike it.","conditions":[{"quest":"the_anvil_debt","stage":"the_assignments"}],"next":"he_strikes_it"},{"label":"Leave it.","end":true}]}],"waiting_for_whom":[{"text":"\\"Anybody,\\" Ashe says. \\"The captain signs the roll and the roll is met and nobody has ever asked me out of what. I am not permitted to volunteer it. I am permitted to answer it.\\" He almost smiles and does not quite manage. \\"That is not a rule anybody wrote. It is a rule I made, because the alternative was to say nothing at all, and I could not get comfortable in that one.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Then I am asking.","next":"the_shortfall"}]}],"the_shortfall":[{"text":"He turns the ledger round so that you can read it, which he does not have to do.\\n\\n\\"The watch roll is two hundred and ninety gold a quarter. The town''s account can find about two hundred of it and has not been able to find more since spring. The rest arrives four days before each quarter day, from a house account at Greyhythe, and it has arrived on time fourteen times.\\" He puts one finger on a column. \\"It comes with a letter of instruction. The instruction each time is that the letter is not to be kept. So I do not keep it. I have not been told I may not remember it.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_watch_payroll","stage":"the_standing_order"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera: \\"Fourteen quarters. Nobody keeps a thing like that up for fourteen quarters as a favour.\\""},{"companion":"aldric","conditions":[{"not":{"companion":"sera","status":"active"}}],"text":"Aldric: \\"Somebody has been buying the watch a quarter at a time, and doing it kindly, which is the part I cannot get past.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"What do you remember?","next":"what_he_remembers"},{"label":"Reed has to be told.","end":true}]}],"what_he_remembers":[{"text":"\\"That it is the same hand every time. That the hand is a hired copyist''s, not a principal''s — the letters are formed to be read quickly rather than to be admired.\\" Ashe closes the ledger. \\"And that the sum is never round. It is always exactly what is short. Which means somebody is being told, every quarter, what Rivermark''s watch costs and what Rivermark can afford, four days before Rivermark works it out.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Told by whom?","next":"not_by_me"},{"label":"Reed has to be told.","end":true}]}],"not_by_me":[{"text":"\\"Not by me,\\" says Tibald Ashe, and for the first time there is an edge on it. \\"I have gone through that four times. The figure is on the market rent board a fortnight before quarter day, in public, where anybody may stand and copy it, and about eleven people do, every quarter, because rents follow it.\\" The edge goes. \\"Nobody had to be bought. It was only ever a matter of being the one who bothered to read.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Thank you.","end":true}]}],"how_many":[{"text":"\\"Standing payments through this floor that are nobody''s business of mine? Six.\\" Ashe does not have to look them up. \\"A fortune-teller. A gateman. A weigher. A copyist. And two I will not name, because naming them would be a kindness to you and an unkindness to them, and one of them is a widow.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Who pays them?","next":"the_copyist"},{"label":"Leave it there.","end":true}]}],"the_copyist":[{"text":"\\"A copyist in a room above this one. He is paid by the letter, he has never met whoever engages him, and his instructions come down the river with the ordinary post.\\" Ashe squares his pen. \\"He is not hiding. He is at a desk between nine and six and he will tell you all of it, because none of it is a secret to him. That is the thing about work of this kind: it is never done by anybody who knows what it is.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_seers_coin","stage":"follow_the_coin"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Take me up to him.","next":"the_room_above"},{"label":"Not yet.","end":true}]}],"the_room_above":[{"text":"The room above is one desk, one window and a man of about thirty who is delighted to be interrupted and answers everything.\\n\\nHe is paid by the letter. He has never met his employer. His instructions arrive by post with the money for the payments folded inside, already counted. There are five other standing payments on the list; he can recite them and does. He has been doing it for a year and seven months and has never once wondered about it, and when you ask him why not he looks honestly puzzled and says: it is a very steady wage, and everything I am asked to write is true.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_seers_coin","stage":"traced_the_hand"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse, going back down the stair: \\"Nobody in that room did anything wrong. That is what I keep getting stuck on.\\""},{"companion":"sera","conditions":[{"not":{"companion":"ilse","status":"active"}}],"text":"Sera: \\"He has never met them, he is paid by the letter, and every word is true. That is exactly how I would set it up.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Go back down.","end":true}]}],"he_will_not_say":[{"text":"\\"I do not know,\\" Ashe says, \\"and I should like to be exact about that, because a clerk who says he does not know is usually saying something else. I have the hand. I have four years of the hand. I do not have a name, and I have looked, in my own time, on a Sunday, more than once.\\" He picks the pen back up. \\"There is a very great deal of paper in this room and not one sheet of it says who is at the other end of the paper. I assumed for years that that was an accident.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave him to it.","end":true}]}]}}',0,0,0,NULL),
('tobin','Tobin Brassscale','Merchant','Half-dragonborn, dull brass scales, keeps his prices where the road puts them.','assets/images/npcs/saurial.png','saurial',8,'{"start":"greeting","nodes":{"greeting":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"town_standing","equals":"fallen"}],"text":"There is no stall. There is a dragonborn with dull brass scales sitting on a crate at the west milestone with everything he still owns in two panniers. \\"Buying,\\" says Tobin. \\"Not selling. Not for a while.\\"","choices":[{"label":"I''m sorry.","next":"no_use_for_that"},{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"consignment_fate","equals":"kept"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"Forty gold clear,\\" Tobin says, \\"and a history.\\" He lays out the mace-heads with more care than they need. \\"My name is in the quay book now, in my own hand, one line under eleven consignments I have nothing whatever to do with. Anybody who reads that book from the top will find a pattern and then find me at the bottom of it, sitting in it, having signed.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Open shop.","effects":[{"open_shop":"tobin"}],"end":true},{"label":"Then unsign it.","next":"cannot_unsign"},{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"consignment_fate","equals":"watch"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"Nineteen days,\\" says Tobin, \\"for Reed to write to Greyhythe and be told that the man is real, of a real address, and has been dead two years.\\" He sets down what he is holding. \\"I have been in trade thirty-one years. A dead man''s name on a live consignment is not a mistake and it is not a fraud either, because nothing was taken from anybody. It is a *convenience*. Somebody wanted goods to arrive in a town and belong to nobody in it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Open shop.","effects":[{"open_shop":"tobin"}],"end":true},{"label":"Convenient for what?","next":"convenient_for_what"},{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"consignment_fate","equals":"refused"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"They came for it on the fifth night,\\" Tobin says. \\"Two men, a handcart, no paper, no hurry.\\" He weighs a mace-head and does not look at it. \\"Which means it was never mine, and it was never a trick, and somebody simply needed twelve hundredweight of salt to sit on a public quay for four days under a name that was not theirs. I have been trying to think of an innocent reason for that for a fortnight.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Open shop.","effects":[{"open_shop":"tobin"}],"end":true},{"label":"Any luck?","next":"no_innocent_reason"},{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"tobins_consignment","stage":"the_quay_book"}],"text":"Tobin reads the bill of lading twice and the copied page of the quay book three times, and then puts both down on the crate and sits on the crate, which is not a thing you have seen him do.\\n\\n\\"Eleven,\\" he says. \\"Eleven consignments to a man nobody has met. And the twelfth has my name on the invoice.\\" He looks up. \\"So. Three doors. I pay it and sell the salt and I am a man who received that consignment. I refuse it and we find out who actually comes for it. Or I take this to Reed and it becomes the town''s problem, slowly, in writing, over about a month.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Pay it. Sell the salt.","next":"pays_and_keeps"},{"label":"Refuse it. Let''s see who collects.","next":"refuses_it"},{"label":"Take it to Reed.","next":"to_the_captain"}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"tobins_consignment","status":"active"}],"text":"\\"River stair,\\" says Tobin, jabbing a claw at the door. \\"Four crates under a tarpaulin, and a porter called Wenn who has been sitting next to them for three days out of what I can only call professional discomfort. Go and look at the paper. I have looked at the paper and I would like a second person to look at the paper.\\"","choices":[{"label":"I''ll go and look.","end":true},{"label":"Open shop.","effects":[{"open_shop":"tobin"}],"end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"concern_answering"}],"once":true,"text":"Tobin is in front of the crate rather than behind it, and he has the door half open at your back before you have said anything. \\"I cannot,\\" he says. \\"Before you ask. My carriage comes up from Greyhythe and this morning it came up with a line on the manifest about who I may sell to, and there is no law in it anywhere, and there does not need to be.\\" The brass along his jaw has gone dull. \\"Thirty-one years in trade. I have never once been told who my customers are, and I am being told now, politely, by a clerk, in writing.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Sell to me anyway.","next":"he_will_not_risk_it"},{"label":"I''ll have it lifted.","next":"he_will_not_risk_it"},{"label":"Leave him be.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"warlord_dead"}],"text":"\\"Best month I have had in four years and I feel wretched about every coin of it,\\" Tobin says, laying out mace-heads. \\"Carters are through in numbers, the road''s clear, and half of them want to see the quarry on the way past. I have started selling them lanterns.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Open shop.","effects":[{"open_shop":"tobin"}],"end":true},{"label":"What''s the town saying?","next":"the_rumours"},{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"warren_truce"}],"text":"Tobin has a new line of goods and is extremely pleased about it. \\"Quarry stone,\\" he says. \\"Dressed. Cheap. Delivered to the east gate on market day by a goblin with a slate who corrects my arithmetic.\\" He shrugs, enormously. \\"Half my regulars won''t come to the stall now. The other half have doubled their orders. Net, I am up.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Open shop.","effects":[{"open_shop":"tobin"}],"end":true},{"label":"What''s the town saying?","next":"the_rumours"},{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"caravan_saved"}],"text":"\\"The Hallowmere wagon,\\" Tobin says, jabbing a claw at you. \\"You walked the Hallowmere wagon through the cut. Two of my crates were on that. Take ten off anything on this table and do not argue with me about it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Open shop.","effects":[{"open_shop":"tobin"}],"end":true},{"label":"What''s the town saying?","next":"the_rumours"},{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"hills_fate","equals":"reroute"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"The northern ford,\\" Tobin says, without enthusiasm. \\"Yes. Nothing has been raided, and it is nine miles longer, and I have lost the two carters who would not do nine extra miles for what I pay.\\" He sets out the mace-heads anyway. \\"Safe is not the same as good. Nobody in this town wants to hear it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Open shop.","effects":[{"open_shop":"tobin"}],"end":true},{"label":"What''s the town saying?","next":"the_rumours"},{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]},{"text":"\\"Arms, armour and potions, fair prices, no questions.\\" The half-dragonborn does not look up from his crate. \\"That last part is advertising. I ask a great many questions. I simply do not act on the answers.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Open shop.","effects":[{"open_shop":"tobin"}],"end":true},{"label":"What''s the town saying?","next":"the_rumours"},{"label":"You have been staring at that invoice since I came in.","next":"the_invoice"},{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]}],"the_invoice":[{"text":"\\"I have been staring at it since Tuesday.\\" Tobin turns it round on the crate so that you can see it. \\"Twelve hundredweight of coarse salt, landed at the river stair, carriage paid, invoiced to me. I did not order it. I cannot prove I did not order it, because a man cannot prove a thing he did not do — that is not a legal difficulty, it is a difficulty with the shape of the world.\\"\\n\\nHe taps the paper with one claw.\\n\\n\\"And here is what has kept me up. It is *good salt*. I could sell it inside a fortnight at a profit. Nobody plays a trick on a man by handing him something he can sell.\\"","interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera: \\"Then it is not a trick on you. You are somewhere for it to be, that is all.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"I''ll go and look at it.","effects":[{"start_quest":"tobins_consignment"}],"next":"he_is_relieved"},{"label":"Then pay it and sell the salt.","next":"not_that_simple"},{"label":"That is between you and the quay.","end":true}]}],"he_is_relieved":[{"text":"\\"Thank you,\\" Tobin says, and means it enough to be brisk about it. \\"River stair. The porter is Wenn — big, cheerful, cannot read a word and remembers everything, which is a combination I would pay for if I could find it twice. Look at the bill and look at the quay book, in that order.\\"","choices":[{"label":"I''ll come back.","end":true}]}],"not_that_simple":[{"text":"\\"It is exactly that simple and that is the trouble,\\" says Tobin. \\"The moment I pay the carriage I have received that consignment. In writing. In the quay book. Whatever it was there for, I have now been the man it was there for.\\" He shrugs, enormously. \\"I have been in trade thirty-one years, and I have learned that a thing which costs nothing and profits you is the only kind of thing worth being frightened of.\\"","choices":[{"label":"All right. I''ll go and look at it.","effects":[{"start_quest":"tobins_consignment"}],"next":"he_is_relieved"},{"label":"Your problem, then.","end":true}]}],"pays_and_keeps":[{"text":"He pays it, and signs for it, and the salt is gone inside a fortnight at a price he is not comfortable with.\\n\\n\\"Forty gold clear,\\" says Tobin Brassscale, counting sixty of it into your hand for the trouble, \\"and one line in a public book, in my own hand, directly under eleven consignments belonging to a man nobody has ever met.\\"\\n\\nHe closes the drawer.\\n\\n\\"I have made money,\\" he says, \\"and acquired a history. I have done that before, in two other towns, and both times it took about four years to find out what the history was for.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"tobins_consignment","stage":"paid_and_kept"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Four years is a long time.","next":"cannot_unsign"},{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]}],"refuses_it":[{"text":"He refuses it, formally, in front of the quay master, and the crates sit on the landing for four days going nobody''s.\\n\\nOn the fifth night two men come up the stair with a handcart. They produce no paper. They argue with nobody. They go straight to the right crates without looking, load them, and take them away downriver, and the whole thing takes nine minutes.\\n\\nMott Wenn watches it done and does not stop them, because nobody has ever told him to stop anybody, and because they are extremely ordinary men doing an extremely ordinary job.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"tobins_consignment","stage":"refused_the_crate"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"kessa","text":"Kessa, from the dark at the top of the steps: \\"Nine minutes. They''ve done that before.\\""},{"companion":"ilse","conditions":[{"not":{"companion":"kessa","status":"active"}}],"text":"Ilse: \\"They knew which crates in the dark. Somebody told them where it would be stacked.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Say nothing and let them go.","next":"no_innocent_reason"}]}],"to_the_captain":[{"text":"Reed takes the bill of lading, reads it twice, and writes to Greyhythe the same afternoon, which is faster than the town does anything.\\n\\nThe answer comes back in nineteen days. The consignee is a real man, of a real address in Greyhythe, and he has been dead two years, and his estate has never been closed.\\n\\nShe writes it in the day book, dated, in her own hand. Then she puts the pen down and says, to nobody in particular, that there is nothing in Rivermark''s law which makes a dead man receiving salt an offence.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"tobins_consignment","stage":"gave_reed_the_bill"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric: \\"She has written it down. That is not nothing. It is very nearly nothing, and it is not nothing.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Convenient for what?","next":"convenient_for_what"},{"label":"Leave it with her.","end":true}]}],"cannot_unsign":[{"text":"\\"You cannot unsign a quay book,\\" Tobin says. \\"That is rather the point of a quay book.\\" He goes back to the crate. \\"I shall do what I have always done, which is to keep excellent records of my own, so that when somebody eventually comes to ask me about it I am the man with the better book. It has worked twice. I would not call it a plan.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]}],"convenient_for_what":[{"text":"\\"For arriving,\\" says Tobin. \\"Salt, lime, oil, rope, boards. Those are not goods, that is a *list* — that is what a man buys who is about to keep something dry, light something, and hold something up.\\" He spreads his claws. \\"And every one of them landed in Rivermark, and none of them belongs to anybody in Rivermark, and none of them is still on the quay.\\"\\n\\nA pause.\\n\\n\\"I sell rope,\\" he says. \\"Nobody bought their rope from me.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]}],"no_innocent_reason":[{"text":"\\"None,\\" says Tobin. \\"And I have been generous with myself about it, because the alternative is unpleasant.\\" He sets the mace-head down. \\"The best I have managed is: somebody wanted goods to be in this town, on a public quay, under a name that could not be asked anything, until they were ready to take them somewhere they did not want carried through a gate.\\"\\n\\nHe looks at you.\\n\\n\\"That is not a crime. That is not even unusual. I have done it myself, in a small way, over a customs matter, and I slept perfectly well.\\" He picks the mace-head back up. \\"Twelve consignments, though. Twelve is not a customs matter.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]}],"the_rumours":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"reed_absolute"}],"text":"Tobin lowers his voice, which for him is still audible across a market. \\"Nobody has called a council since the quarry. Reed signs things and they happen and it is, I will be honest with you, extremely convenient.\\" He weighs a mace-head. \\"I have been in three towns where that was extremely convenient. I left all three.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back","next":"greeting"}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"met_hollis"}],"text":"\\"You have been to the granary.\\" Tobin does not look up. \\"Everybody who has been to the granary has the same face afterwards. He is charming, he is entirely reasonable, and you come away having agreed to something.\\" A pause. \\"He holds my winter credit. I want that said out loud so that you know what my opinion is worth.\\"","choices":[{"label":"What does he hold over you?","next":"the_credit"},{"label":"Back","next":"greeting"}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"met_sera"}],"text":"\\"The Vance girl keeps the mill''s books and has done since she was twenty-one,\\" Tobin says. \\"Alone. Nobody checks. I have said that to two watch captains and one of them wrote it down.\\" He shrugs. \\"That is the whole of my contribution to public life.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back","next":"greeting"}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"cellar_cleared","equals":"cause"}],"text":"\\"Spoiled grain in Hearthstone''s cellar,\\" Tobin repeats, and puts down what he is holding. \\"With a tally on it. With a *stamp* on it.\\" He looks at you with real, unhappy respect. \\"You want to be careful who you show that to and in what order.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back","next":"greeting"}]},{"text":"\\"Goblins in the quarry, gnolls on the road, and the price of salt.\\" Tobin ticks them off. \\"Those are the three subjects. There has not been a fourth in this town since the mill roof.\\" He looks up. \\"There is a warden works the east hills. Thornwood. She has a fourth subject and nobody will sit still for it.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"met_ilse"}],"choices":[{"label":"Back","next":"greeting"}]}],"the_credit":[{"text":"\\"Two hundred and forty gold, rolled every winter for six years,\\" Tobin says. \\"He has never once called it in and he has never once let me forget he could.\\" He goes back to the crate. \\"That is not a complaint. It is a very good business arrangement. It is simply also a leash, and I would rather you heard me say the word than worked it out later and wondered.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back","next":"greeting"}]}],"no_use_for_that":[{"text":"\\"Nor am I, particularly,\\" Tobin says. \\"I have my stock, I have my scales, and I have a road west. That is more than most of them got out with.\\" He looks back once, at the smoke line. \\"Do not travel with me. I mean that pleasantly.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]}],"he_will_not_risk_it":[{"text":"\\"No,\\" says Tobin, and it costs him something to say it. \\"Because it would not be me they came to. It would be the carriage, and the carriage is four men with families in Greyhythe, and they would not be told either — the price would simply go up and stay up and nobody would ever say why.\\" He gets the door the rest of the way open. \\"When it changes, come back and I will sell you this whole table at cost and be glad of it. It is not going to change this month.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Go.","end":true}]}]}}',1,1,0,NULL),
('tull_barrow','Tull Barrow','Drover','Sixty beasts, four dogs, and a route he has walked since he was nine. He is walking it in the wrong direction this year and has not told anybody why.','assets/images/npcs/barbarian.png','tull_barrow',1,'{"start":"hail","nodes":{"hail":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"drove_fate","equals":"out"}],"once":true,"text":"He is at the horn-post at the fen road head with the herd already across in three lots and the dogs sitting where he told them to sit an hour ago.\\n\\n\\"That''s it, then,\\" Tull Barrow says. \\"Forty years my father walked this road east. I''ve walked it west once and I''m not walking it back.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Good luck on the crossing.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"drove_fate","equals":"back"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"Worse price,\\" Tull says. \\"Two coppers a head worse and a day longer on the road for it.\\" He is standing in Rivermark''s own weighing yard with sixty beasts behind him and three other holders in the doorway who came to find out why. \\"Aye. Worth it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"They''re still asking questions in there.","next":"still_asking"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"drove_fate","equals":"lost"}],"once":true,"text":"He has counted what is left twice and said the number out loud both times, and he is not going to make the crossing with it and he knows that and is walking anyway.\\n\\n\\"Forty-nine,\\" he says. \\"You don''t have to stand there.\\"","choices":[{"label":"I''m sorry.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"wolves_fate"}},{"any":[{"flag":"winter_field_cleared"},{"flag":"howler_killed"}]}],"text":"The herd is off the corner and spread across the low half of the field for the first time in three weeks, and Tull Barrow is standing in the middle of them counting, which he does not need to do and is doing anyway.\\n\\n\\"Four of them,\\" he says. \\"All thin. I''ve seen thin. That was hungry in a way that had been going on a while.\\"","choices":[{"label":"That''s the end of it. The field''s clear.","next":"the_field_is_clear"},{"label":"We went and found where they were denning.","next":"the_den"},{"label":"Later.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"wolves_fate"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"Field''s quiet,\\" Tull says. \\"Beasts are grazing the far corner. First time in three weeks.\\" He does not thank you in words; he pours you something out of a flask instead and it is not the flask he offers to strangers.","choices":[{"label":"About the drove.","next":"the_work"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_drovers_road","stage":"where_the_drove_is_going"}],"text":"He knows what you are going to ask before you ask it, because you have been walking west for a day and a half and there is nothing west but the fen.\\n\\n\\"Go on,\\" he says. \\"Say it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"The market''s east.","next":"the_market_is_east"}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_drovers_road","stage":"take_the_work"}],"text":"The man at the third fire has sixty beasts in the fold, four dogs working the edge of it without being told, and a six-foot ash staff across his knees.\\n\\n\\"You''ll be the swords,\\" Tull Barrow says. \\"Two days, fold to the fen road head, paid at the horn-post. It''s ordinary work.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"met_barrow"}],"choices":[{"label":"Nobody robs cattle. Why are you paying for swords?","next":"why_swords"},{"label":"Two days, then.","next":"the_work"},{"label":"Not interested.","end":true}]},{"text":"Three fires along the fold wall and none of them talking to each other. The man at the third has four dogs, sixty beasts, and an ash staff across his knees with an iron ferrule on it that has been used for something other than turning cattle.\\n\\n\\"Fold''s common,\\" he says. \\"Fire''s mine.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"met_barrow"},{"start_quest":"the_drovers_road"}],"choices":[{"label":"You''re hiring.","next":"why_swords"},{"label":"What''s happening on Winter Field?","next":"the_field"},{"label":"Nothing.","end":true}]}],"still_asking":[{"text":"\\"They are,\\" Tull says. \\"Bewick''s in there, and two of the Ellerys, and a man from the counting house who came because the counting house heard the word ''common'' said in a loud voice.\\"\\n\\nHe watches the door.\\n\\n\\"None of them are going to get anywhere. But it''s been said in a room with a clerk in it. That''s different from being said at a fire.\\"","choices":[{"label":"It is.","end":true}]}],"why_swords":[{"text":"Tull Barrow looks at the fire for a bit.\\n\\n\\"Because I''m paying for swords,\\" he says. \\"That''s the answer you''re getting at a fire. You want the other answer, you can have it on the road on the second day, and if you don''t like it then you can turn round and I''ll still pay you.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Two days, then.","next":"the_work"},{"label":"I''d rather have it now.","next":"not_now"}]}],"not_now":[{"text":"\\"Then you''d rather have it now and I''d rather give it to you on the road,\\" Tull says, \\"and one of us is paying, so.\\"\\n\\nHe pokes the fire.","choices":[{"label":"Two days, then.","next":"the_work"},{"label":"Keep your money.","end":true}]}],"the_work":[{"text":"\\"Right,\\" Tull says. \\"Dawn. We take them off the fold, down the west side of Winter Field, and up the drove track to the fen road head. Dogs do the work. You do the standing about.\\"\\n\\nHe hands over a staff without being asked, which is either a courtesy or a test.","on_enter":[{"give_item":"drovers_staff","quantity":1},{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_drovers_road","stage":"where_the_drove_is_going"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Dawn.","end":true},{"label":"The market''s east, Barrow.","next":"the_market_is_east"}]}],"the_market_is_east":[{"text":"\\"It is,\\" Tull agrees. He does not stop walking.\\n\\n\\"Common grazing on Winter Field was bought in the spring. Not fenced. Not stocked. Nobody''s come to look at it. What they''ve done is stop renewing the commoners'' rights as they fall due, one name at a time, and mine fell due at Michaelmas.\\" He whistles the far dog round. \\"I''m not being turned off. I''m being not-renewed. There''s nobody to shout at.\\"\\n\\nA long pause and about two hundred yards.\\n\\n\\"I didn''t tell you because I thought you''d been sent to stop me.\\"","interjections":[{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse: \\"How many others.\\" Tull: \\"Three since spring that I know of. There''ll be more that didn''t say.\\""},{"companion":"kessa","text":"Kessa: \\"Sent by who? Who''d send us?\\" Tull, not looking round: \\"That''s the thing. I don''t know either. That''s why I thought it.\\""},{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera: \\"Not renewing is cheaper than evicting and it does not go anywhere near a court. Somebody has done this before.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Then we walk you out. Slowly, in daylight.","next":"walked_out"},{"label":"No. We turn them round and walk them into Rivermark market.","next":"turned_back"},{"label":"Push on through the night. We can be at the fen road head by morning.","next":"the_night_crossing"}]}],"the_night_crossing":[{"text":"Tull Barrow does not like it and says so once, which for him is a formal protest.\\n\\n\\"Sixty head across the bottom of Winter Field in the dark,\\" he says. \\"Past the thorn. With four dogs.\\"\\n\\nHe whistles the far pair in anyway, because you are the swords and he is paying, and that is a bargain he has kept the whole way.","choices":[{"label":"Take them down the fen side of the field.","check":{"skill":"survival","dc":14,"on_success":"walked_out","on_failure":"the_drove_breaks"}},{"label":"You''re right. We wait for light.","next":"walked_out"}]}],"the_drove_breaks":[{"text":"It goes wrong at the thorn brake, in the dark, in about ninety seconds.\\n\\nYou lose eleven head and both of the far dogs, and after that the herd will not be held by anything — not by dogs, not by the wall, not by four people with ash staffs shouting into the wind off the fen.\\n\\nTull Barrow does not blame anybody. He counts what is left twice, says the number out loud both times, and starts walking.\\n\\nHe is not going to make the crossing with what he has, and he knows that, and he starts walking.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_drovers_road","stage":"lost_the_drove"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"kessa","text":"Kessa went into the thorn after the second dog and came out without it and has not said anything since."},{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse: \\"Forty-nine.\\" She says it once. Tull says it again about a mile later, to himself, and gets the same number."}],"choices":[{"label":"Walk with him.","end":true}]}],"turned_back":[{"text":"It takes an extra day and costs him two coppers a head, and he does it, and he does it because you say it out loud in front of his own dogs and there is nowhere for him to put the argument.\\n\\nSixty beasts through Rivermark''s east gate at noon into a weighing yard that was not expecting them. Tull says the word ''common'' in front of a clerk, and then in front of a different clerk, and by dusk there are three other holders in the room and a man from the counting house who came because somebody said ''common'' loudly enough to carry.\\n\\nThey will get nowhere. It has been said in a room with a clerk in it, which is not the same as being said at a fire.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_drovers_road","stage":"turned_them_back"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera: \\"Nothing will come of it.\\" A pause. \\"It is on a page now. Things that are on a page take longer to disappear.\\""},{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric: \\"Three men and a clerk. That is what a town is, when it works.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Get the beasts watered.","end":true}]}],"walked_out":[{"text":"The fen wardens take them across the causeway in three lots, which is a thing you did not know could be done to cattle and would rather not have watched.\\n\\nTull Barrow pays at the horn-post, shakes hands, whistles the dogs in, and goes west. He is the fourth this year, on this road, that anybody knows about.\\n\\nNobody in Rivermark is counting.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_drovers_road","stage":"walked_them_out"}},{"set_flag":"grazing_bought_known"}],"choices":[{"label":"Watch him go.","end":true}]}],"the_field":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"wolves_fate"}],"text":"\\"Quiet,\\" Tull says. \\"You''d know.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"text":"\\"Beasts have been stood in the far corner three days,\\" Tull says. \\"Facing the thorn. Not eating.\\" He turns the staff over. \\"That''s not wolves being wolves. Wolves being wolves is you lose one and the rest graze. This is the whole herd deciding, together, to starve rather than go down that end.\\"\\n\\nHe looks west.\\n\\n\\"Nobody''s cut that thorn back in two years and nobody''s going to, because the field''s common and common ground belongs to whoever will do the work, and there isn''t anybody.\\"","on_enter":[{"start_quest":"wolves_in_winter_field"}],"interjections":[{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse: \\"Four of them, and they are working it from the fen side. That is the wrong side. There is nothing to eat on the fen side.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"We''ll go into the thorn.","next":"into_the_thorn"},{"label":"Then it''s not my field either.","next":"not_my_field"},{"label":"Later.","end":true}]}],"into_the_thorn":[{"text":"\\"Take the channel,\\" Tull says. \\"There''s a dragged track through the grass into the brake and it''s been used more than once. Go in on that and don''t go in abreast, because whatever''s in there has the thorn on its side and you don''t.\\"\\n\\nHe whistles the dogs off the fold wall and puts them behind him, which he has not done once while you have been standing here.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"wolves_in_winter_field","stage":"the_thorn_brake"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Go in now.","effects":[{"start_combat":"the_field_howler"}],"end":true},{"label":"In the morning.","end":true}]}],"the_field_is_clear":[{"text":"\\"That''ll do,\\" Tull says, and goes back to counting. \\"Beasts were grazing the far corner inside the hour. That''s the whole of what I wanted and I''m not going to pretend I wanted more.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"wolves_in_winter_field","stage":"killed_the_pack"}}],"choices":[{"label":"That''s the whole of it.","end":true}]}],"the_den":[{"text":"The den is on the fen side of the old bank, dug well in, and it is dry.\\n\\nIt should not be dry. It is four feet below the level of the winter field and the winter field is standing in water in half its ruts. The water is on the wrong side of the bank, and it has been coming up that side for at least two seasons, because there are two seasons of old bedding in there and the lower layer is rotted and the upper is not.\\n\\nThey did not come up onto the grazing because they were hungry. They came up because their ground went under, and then they were hungry.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"wolves_in_winter_field","stage":"found_the_den"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse is on her knees at the mouth of it with a handful of the lower bedding. \\"Two years,\\" she says. \\"The fen has come up two feet in two years and nobody living on it has said a word.\\""},{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric: \\"They were pushed.\\" He says it the way a man says a sentence he has said recently about something else."}],"choices":[{"label":"Fill it in and go back to Barrow.","end":true}]}],"not_my_field":[{"text":"\\"No,\\" Tull agrees. \\"It isn''t.\\"\\n\\nHe puts the staff back across his knees.\\n\\n\\"It''s not mine either, as of Michaelmas. That''s how it is with common ground — it belongs to whoever''ll do the work, right up until nobody will, and then it turns out it belonged to somebody else the whole time.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"wolves_in_winter_field","stage":"left_them_to_it"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Good night, Barrow.","end":true}]}]}}',0,1,0,NULL),
('vennik_tallowglass','Vennik Tallowglass','Instrument-maker','A gnome of Lathmere, nine years resident, who sells glasses and compasses and dividers and reports everything he hears to the League. He has lately begun leaving things out of his reports, because he has grown fond of the place, and he is aware of exactly what that is called.','assets/images/npcs/wizard.png','wizard',1,'{"start":"greeting","nodes":{"greeting":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"granary_fate","equals":"reported"}],"text":"The sealed packet for Lathmere has gone off the counter. Vennik Tallowglass is polishing a lens with enormous concentration and has been for some time.\\n\\n\\"It went under my seal,\\" he says. \\"Nine years I have sent them everything and left out nothing that mattered, and the one report I have ever been proud of is the one that ends my posting.\\"\\n\\nHe holds the lens up.\\n\\n\\"They will recall me. Not for the report — for the eleven months before it, when I sent them cheerful nothings about a granary I had not asked to see. They will be quite right to.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"You could have sent it eleven months ago.\\"","next":"the_omissions"},{"label":"\\"Show me what you sell.\\"","effects":[{"open_shop":"vennik_tallowglass"}]},{"label":"Leave him to it.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"granary_fate","equals":"broken"}],"text":"Vennik is standing at the window with his hands behind his back, watching Highstrand not do anything about what happened on the quay.\\n\\n\\"I have written it four times,\\" he says. \\"Four drafts. Every one of them is accurate and every one of them ends with Lathmere sending soldiers up a road that is fifty-five miles long in the winter.\\"\\n\\nHe turns round.\\n\\n\\"So I am now a League officer sitting on a fact. Which is a thing I have been telling myself for two years that I would never be, in the specific sense that I have been it for two years.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Then do not send it.\\"","next":"the_omissions"},{"label":"\\"Show me what you sell.\\"","effects":[{"open_shop":"vennik_tallowglass"}]},{"label":"Leave him.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"granary_fate","equals":"filled"}],"text":"\\"Twenty weeks,\\" says Vennik, \\"in the bays, countable, by a man with a rule.\\"\\n\\nHe adjusts something on the little beam under its dome.\\n\\n\\"And my quarterly return to Lathmere for the first time in eleven months says a true thing without my having to arrange the sentence. You have no idea what a relief that is. I have been writing accurate reports about the wrong subjects since spring.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Show me what you sell.\\"","effects":[{"open_shop":"vennik_tallowglass"}]},{"label":"Leave him to it.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"box_opened"}],"text":"Vennik has the glass dome off the little beam and is standing beside it with his hands folded, waiting, which he has clearly been doing for some time.\\n\\n\\"You have got it,\\" he says. \\"Do not tell me, I can see the way you are carrying that bag. Put it on. Please put it on, I have been thinking about nothing else since you left.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Put the League''s weight on the beam.","next":"the_weighing"},{"label":"\\"Not yet.\\"","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"met_vennik"}],"text":"\\"Back,\\" says Vennik, over a lens. \\"You will want the third element. Everybody wants the third element and nobody knows they do.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Show me what you sell.\\"","effects":[{"open_shop":"vennik_tallowglass"}]},{"label":"\\"Tell me about the packet on your counter.\\"","next":"the_packet"},{"label":"\\"You report to Lathmere.\\"","next":"the_reporting"},{"label":"Another time.","end":true}]},{"text":"Glasses, compasses, dividers, and on the counter under a glass dome a beam-scale small enough to weigh a letter.\\n\\nThe gnome behind it breathes on a lens, holds it up, and finds a fault in it you cannot see.\\n\\n\\"Vennik Tallowglass, of Lathmere,\\" he says. \\"Nine years on this rock. I sell instruments to men who navigate by smell and I will sell you one too, and you will use it twice and then put it in a drawer, and I have made my peace with that.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"met_vennik"}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"Show me what you sell.\\"","effects":[{"open_shop":"vennik_tallowglass"}]},{"label":"\\"That beam under the dome.\\"","next":"the_dome"},{"label":"\\"You are League, then.\\"","next":"the_reporting"},{"label":"Leave him to it.","end":true}]}],"the_dome":[{"text":"\\"That,\\" says Vennik, with real pleasure, \\"is the most accurate beam north of Lathmere and probably north of Thrailgate, and there is nothing clever about it whatsoever.\\"\\n\\n\\"It is a beam. The knife edges are agate. It lives under a dome because a beam that will read a letter will also read the draught from a door, and the draught from a door is what everybody else''s beam is actually measuring.\\"\\n\\nHe taps the glass.\\n\\n\\"Put a thing on that and there is no argument to be had. That is not a boast. It is the opposite of a boast. It is the whole reason nobody in this city has ever asked me to weigh anything.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Nobody?\\"","next":"nobody_asks"},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]}],"nobody_asks":[{"text":"\\"Nine years,\\" Vennik says. \\"Not the Moot, not the Watch, not the Counting Boom, not the Factor. I have the only instrument on this promontory that settles a question and I have never once been asked to settle one.\\"\\n\\nHe puts the lens down.\\n\\n\\"I used to think that was because they did not know. I have stopped thinking that.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]}],"the_reporting":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"granary_lie_known"}],"text":"\\"You know what is in that granary,\\" says Vennik. He says it flatly, as a fact he has checked.\\n\\n\\"So do I. I have known since the spring. I have sent Lathmere eleven perfectly accurate reports about pitch prices, mast counts, the Sawyers'' Hall vote and the price of a bed at the Sounding Line, and not one word about the only building in this city that matters.\\"\\n\\nHe sets down the lens.\\n\\n\\"That is not an omission. Nine years of my professional life have been about the difference between an omission and a lie, and I lost the argument with myself about that particular distinction in about April.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Why?\\"","next":"the_omissions"},{"label":"\\"You could ruin Culm tomorrow.\\"","next":"about_culm"},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]},{"text":"\\"I am,\\" Vennik agrees, without the smallest hesitation. \\"Everybody in this city knows it and about half of them think it is a secret.\\"\\n\\n\\"I sell instruments and I write to Lathmere. Prices, hulls, tempers, who is speaking to whom. It is not spying so much as bookkeeping with opinions in it. Halvia Corr has one in every chartered city and we all know each other''s names.\\"\\n\\nHe smiles slightly.\\n\\n\\"The useful thing about being an open secret is that people stop performing for you after about the second year. You cannot imagine what they say in front of me now.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"And what have you not been telling them?\\"","next":"the_packet"},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]}],"the_packet":[{"text":"Vennik looks at the sealed packet on the counter, which has been there long enough to have dust on it.\\n\\n\\"That is the spring return,\\" he says. \\"It has been sealed since the first of the month and the courier goes Thursdays.\\"\\n\\nHe turns it round so the seal faces up.\\n\\n\\"It has caught four Thursdays.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Why?\\"","next":"the_omissions"},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]}],"the_omissions":[{"text":"\\"Because I have grown fond of the place,\\" Vennik says, \\"and that is a hanging matter dressed up as a sentiment.\\"\\n\\nHe comes round the counter, which he does not do.\\n\\n\\"I came here to count things for a woman in Lathmere who has never seen this promontory and never will. And for nine years I have watched a city that has no river, no road north, and no business existing, build the best ships on this Mere out of timber it drags in on oxen, and feed nineteen hundred people it is forbidden to feed, and do all of it by means of an arrangement that would not survive being written down.\\"\\n\\n\\"Every accurate sentence I send about it kills a piece of it. Every single one. And they are all accurate. That is what nobody warns you about.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Then stop sending them.\\"","next":"cannot_stop"},{"label":"\\"You will be recalled.\\"","next":"cannot_stop"},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]}],"cannot_stop":[{"text":"\\"I cannot stop,\\" Vennik says, \\"because a silence from Waerhaven is itself a report, and a considerably more interesting one.\\"\\n\\nHe goes back behind the counter and picks the lens up again, and his hands are steady, which is somehow the worst part.\\n\\n\\"So I write. I write true things about small matters, punctually, in a good hand, and I leave a hole in the middle of the page in the shape of a granary, and one day somebody in Lathmere with more patience than the last one will put four of my returns side by side and see the hole.\\"\\n\\n\\"And then it will not be Culm they come for. It will be both of us, and Culm will not even have known I was in it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Then be the one who sends it.\\"","next":"about_culm"},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]}],"about_culm":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"culm_broken"}],"text":"\\"He is finished,\\" Vennik says. \\"He was finished the day he wrote the first one and he has been walking about being finished ever since, which is a thing I have watched a man do for eleven months and would not recommend.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]},{"text":"\\"Bevis Culm,\\" says Vennik, \\"is the only genuinely honest man in Highstrand and he has told the largest lie in the Ythan League, and both of those are true and I have stopped trying to make them agree.\\"\\n\\n\\"He inherited a shortfall. He covered it, once, for a quarter, because the barges would have stopped and it was February. And then it was a lie he had told, and a lie you have told is a thing you have to keep telling, and he is not a man with any talent for the second part.\\"\\n\\nHe looks at the sealed packet.\\n\\n\\"If I send what I know, they will recall him in disgrace and appoint somebody who will not care, and the barges will stop while the audit runs, and the audit will take a season, and the season is winter.\\"\\n\\n\\"I have done that arithmetic eleven times and it comes out the same way every time, and I do not like being a man whose conscience keeps producing convenient answers.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Then check the arithmetic with somebody else.\\"","next":"the_offer"},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]}],"the_offer":[{"text":"Vennik looks at you for rather a long time for a gnome who is normally halfway through a sentence before you have finished yours.\\n\\n\\"Yes,\\" he says. \\"All right. Yes.\\"\\n\\n\\"Here is what I have that nobody else in this city has. A beam that ends arguments. A seal that Lathmere opens without question. And nine years of returns that will show, to anybody who reads them in order, exactly when everything started going wrong.\\"\\n\\nHe puts his hand on the glass dome.\\n\\n\\"All three are yours if you bring me something worth putting under them. And I would rather it were you than me, and I am aware of precisely what kind of man that makes me.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"vennik_offer"}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"I will bring you something.\\"","end":true}]}],"the_weighing":[{"text":"He does it properly, which takes four minutes and is unbearable.\\n\\nThe dome off. The beam released, checked empty, arrested, released again. His own reference set on, read, taken off. Then the League''s standard weight, fourteen pounds of bronze with the Mere and the League''s arms and a date on it, lowered onto the pan with both hands.\\n\\nThe pointer swings, and settles, and does not come back.\\n\\nVennik reads it. Then he reads it again. Then he turns the League''s weight over and looks at the underside, where the stamp is, and where the file marks are, and he goes a colour you have not seen on him.\\n\\n\\"Four ounces,\\" he says. \\"Four ounces and a hair, and the hair is because somebody stopped filing when they got frightened.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"weight_fate","value":"proved"},{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_short_weight","stage":"proved"}},{"give_item":"league_standard_weight","quantity":1},{"xp":200}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"What does that mean, in grain?\\"","next":"in_grain"},{"label":"\\"Who could have done it?\\"","next":"who_filed_it"}]}],"in_grain":[{"text":"Vennik does not need paper for it, which is the frightening part.\\n\\n\\"Four ounces in the hundredweight,\\" he says. \\"On grain alone, at what this city receives in a year, over six years.\\"\\n\\nHe stops.\\n\\n\\"Nine weeks,\\" he says. \\"Nine weeks of bread for twenty-six thousand people. It is not in anybody''s cellar. It never came off the barges at all. Lathmere shipped what Lathmere was paid for and Waerhaven signed for more than it received, every quarter, for six years, in a document I have countersigned eleven times.\\"\\n\\nHe sits down on his own stool.\\n\\n\\"The granary is not short because somebody stole from it. The granary is short because this city has been buying air.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"granary_lie_known"}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"Who filed it?\\"","next":"who_filed_it"},{"label":"\\"Then take it to Lathmere.\\"","next":"the_report_offer"}]}],"who_filed_it":[{"text":"\\"Somebody with a key, six years ago,\\" says Vennik. \\"And the Factor before Culm was recalled to Lathmere six years ago with a commendation and a house in the Silver Quarter, and I am now going to stop talking, because the next sentence is one I can be hanged for and I have not decided yet whether I am going to say it.\\"\\n\\nHe puts the dome back over the beam.\\n\\n\\"It was not stolen for Waerhaven. That is the part I want you to hold on to. Every ounce of it went south.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Then send it south.\\"","next":"the_report_offer"},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]}],"the_report_offer":[{"text":"\\"I can,\\" Vennik says. \\"Under my seal, with the beam reading attached and nine years of returns behind it, and Halvia Corr will open it herself because they all open mine themselves.\\"\\n\\n\\"And the moment she does, the barges stop while an audit runs.\\"\\n\\nHe looks up at you.\\n\\n\\"So it is not mine to send. It never was. I have got the seal and you have got the weight, and I am going to sit in this shop and grind lenses until you tell me what this city is going to do, and I have never in nine years been so relieved to be the second most useful person in a room.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"vennik_offer"}],"choices":[{"label":"Take the weight and go.","end":true}]}]}}',1,1,0,NULL),
('vessa_dann','Vessa Dann','Tally-Keeper of the Pit','She holds the board, sets the odds, pays out and takes her tenth. Everyone who has ever fought here has been quoted a number by her, and nobody has ever been told how she got it.','assets/images/npcs/bard.png','bard',1,'{"start":"hail","nodes":{"hail":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"pit_fate","equals":"broken"}],"once":true,"text":"The board is off the wall and the sand has not been raked in a week. Vessa Dann is sitting on the bottom rung of the ladder with the tally-slate on her knees, not writing on it.\\n\\n\\"Forty people,\\" she says. \\"Forty people who''d have bet on him all winter, and you read them the rate of forfeit.\\" She almost laughs. \\"I''ve been doing the odds here nine years and I never once did that sum out loud.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Neither had they.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"pit_fate","equals":"bought"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"Discharged,\\" Vessa says. \\"I wrote it myself. I''m not a thief and I''ve never claimed to be one.\\" She taps the board. \\"I''m also now a pit with a gate and nothing to put behind it, and that is a worse problem than you think it is.\\"","choices":[{"label":"You''ll find someone.","next":"she_will_find_someone"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"pit_champion_is_you"}],"text":"\\"There you are,\\" Vessa says, without looking up from the board. \\"Twelfth name. The rim''s been asking after you all week and I''ve been quoting them a number.\\"\\n\\nShe turns the board round. The number is not small.","choices":[{"label":"What''s the number for?","next":"the_number"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"pit_champion_beaten"}],"text":"The sand has not been raked yet and it will not be for a while. Vessa Dann comes down the ladder with the belt over her arm and the tally-board under it.\\n\\n\\"Eleven names on it and all of them his,\\" she says. \\"There''s room punched for a twelfth. There''s been room punched for a twelfth for fourteen months.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Take the belt.","next":"take_the_belt"},{"label":"Where''s Orm?","next":"where_is_orm"}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_pit_champion","stage":"the_undercroft"}],"text":"\\"You went through the gate,\\" Vessa says. She has not moved from the board and does not need to have seen it. \\"Everyone goes through the gate eventually and everyone comes back out with the same face on. Say it, then.\\"","choices":[{"label":"He''s bonded.","next":"the_bond_named"},{"label":"I want the bout.","next":"the_bout_offer"},{"label":"Nothing.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_pit_champion","stage":"take_a_bout"}],"text":"She looked you over on the way down the ladder and decided on a number before you reached the bottom, and she does not tell you what it is. The board behind her has three columns on it and only one of them is money.\\n\\n\\"Vessa Dann. I hold the board.\\" She wets the chalk. \\"You''ll want a bout. Everyone who comes down that ladder wants a bout, they just take different amounts of time to say so.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"met_vessa"}],"choices":[{"label":"What''s behind the gate?","next":"behind_the_gate"},{"label":"Who''s your draw?","next":"the_draw"},{"label":"Give me a bout.","next":"the_warmup_offer"}]},{"text":"A woman with a tally-board at the bottom of a twenty-foot cut, in the middle of forty or fifty people making the noise of three hundred. She looks you over, decides on a number, and does not tell you what it is.\\n\\n\\"Vessa Dann,\\" she says. \\"I hold the board. You can watch for nothing and bet for anything and fight for what I say you''re worth, and I''d think about which of those three you came down the ladder for.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"met_vessa"},{"start_quest":"the_pit_champion"}],"interjections":[{"companion":"kessa","text":"Kessa is already looking at the sand rather than at the woman, which tells you what she came down the ladder for."},{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric: \\"There is a gate in the rock at that end and something is leaning on it.\\" Vessa, without turning: \\"There is. It''s fed.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Who''s your draw?","next":"the_draw"},{"label":"What''s behind the gate?","next":"behind_the_gate"},{"label":"Give me a bout.","next":"the_warmup_offer"},{"label":"I''ll watch.","end":true}]}],"she_will_find_someone":[{"text":"\\"I''ll find someone,\\" she agrees. \\"That''s the part people never think about when they''re feeling good about themselves on the rim. There is always someone, and the reason there is always someone is forty-one gold.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Then it wasn''t worth much.","end":true}]}],"the_number":[{"text":"\\"Odds,\\" she says. \\"Yours. Against whatever I put up next.\\" She rubs it out and writes it again slightly worse. \\"You''re the draw now. That''s not a compliment, it''s a column.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Understood.","end":true}]}],"the_draw":[{"text":"\\"Orm Lisser,\\" Vessa says. \\"Eleven names on the belt and all of them his. Hasn''t lost in fourteen months and hasn''t killed anybody in his life, which the rim''s started to hold against him.\\" She chalks something. \\"He sleeps behind the gate. Not in the gate. Behind it, in the undercroft, in a stall with a bed in it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Why does he sleep down here?","next":"why_he_sleeps_here"},{"label":"Then I''ll fight him.","next":"the_bout_offer"},{"label":"What''s behind the gate itself?","next":"behind_the_gate"}]}],"why_he_sleeps_here":[{"text":"Vessa looks at you for slightly longer than the question deserves.\\n\\n\\"Because he does,\\" she says. \\"Door''s not locked. Nobody''s chained. Go through the gate and ask him yourself, and mind the fourth stall, because what''s in the fourth stall is not a conversation.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_pit_champion","stage":"the_undercroft"}}],"choices":[{"label":"I''ll do that.","end":true}]}],"behind_the_gate":[{"text":"\\"A corridor,\\" Vessa says. \\"Which disappoints everybody. Quarry gallery, six stalls, a drain. Four of the stalls have animals in them and two of them have beds.\\" She chalks. \\"What comes out of the gate when I want it to come out of the gate is in the fourth stall and it has been leaning on the boards for about an hour. Would you like to meet it, or would you like to keep the afternoon you had planned?\\"","choices":[{"label":"Put it up. Let''s see.","next":"the_warmup_offer"},{"label":"The corridor, then. I''ll walk through.","next":"why_he_sleeps_here"},{"label":"Neither.","end":true}]}],"the_warmup_offer":[{"text":"\\"Right,\\" Vessa says, and the whole rim knows it before she has finished saying it, because forty people who have been standing in the cold for an hour can read a chalk-stroke at twenty feet.\\n\\nThe rope goes up on the gate. The counterweight drops. The thing that has been leaning on the boards comes out into the light already moving.","on_enter":[{"start_combat":"pit_gate_beast"}],"choices":[{"label":"Sand under your feet.","end":true}]}],"the_bond_named":[{"text":"\\"He is,\\" Vessa says. No pause at all. \\"Forty-one gold advanced against eighteen months of bouts, mark witnessed, forfeit at a fifth per missed. It''s lawful, it''s on paper, and I did not write it — I bought the pit and the bond came with the pit, the same as the ladder and the gate.\\"\\n\\nShe puts the chalk down, which she has not done once.\\n\\n\\"That''s the honest answer. Here''s the rest of it: I have never once offered to discharge it, and I could have, any month of the fourteen.\\"","interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera: \\"A fifth per missed bout. On forty-one gold. He has been paying interest on a debt he cleared in the first year.\\" Vessa: \\"Yes.\\""},{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric: \\"You said that very quickly for a woman who has not been asked before.\\" Vessa: \\"I''ve been asked. I''ve never been asked twice.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Then I''ll fight him for it.","next":"the_bout_offer"},{"label":"What would it take to discharge it?","next":"the_price"},{"label":"I''ll talk to him first.","end":true}]}],"the_price":[{"text":"\\"Forty-one, and eleven accrued,\\" Vessa says. \\"Fifty-two. Paid on the rim, in front of witnesses, and I''ll write ''discharged'' on it in my own hand because I am not going to have it said that I held a man on a technicality.\\"\\n\\nShe picks the chalk back up.\\n\\n\\"I''d rather you didn''t. I''ll be straight with you about that. He''s the only thing down here worth the ladder.\\"","choices":[{"label":"I''ll go and talk to him.","end":true},{"label":"Then I''ll fight him instead.","next":"the_bout_offer"}]}],"the_bout_offer":[{"text":"\\"Bout, then,\\" Vessa says, and chalks it, and the noise on the rim changes shape.\\n\\n\\"He comes down the ladder, not out of the gate — he''s not what''s kept behind the gate and he never has been. Sand''s raked. It won''t stay raked.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_pit_champion","stage":"the_undercroft"}},{"start_combat":"pit_bout_champion"}],"interjections":[{"companion":"kessa","text":"Kessa, low: \\"He''s not going to finish you. Don''t let that make you slow.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Sand.","end":true}]}],"take_the_belt":[{"text":"She punches the twelfth name in herself, on the rim, in front of everybody, with a bradawl and about four seconds'' care.\\n\\n\\"Terms of the bond,\\" she says, handing it over. \\"A champion who loses is a champion nobody pays to see, and a bond you can''t work off is discharged. He''s free at dusk with nothing, and he says that''s more than he had at noon, and he says it like he means it, which I find irritating.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_pit_champion","stage":"took_the_belt"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"kessa","text":"Kessa: \\"Eleven names and then yours. That''ll follow you further than you think.\\""},{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric takes the belt out of your hands to look at the eleven names and gives it back without a word."}],"choices":[{"label":"Take it.","end":true}]}],"where_is_orm":[{"text":"\\"Up the ladder,\\" Vessa says, \\"which he has not been in fourteen months, and he went slowly, and I am not going to pretend that was easy to watch.\\"\\n\\nShe holds the belt out again.","choices":[{"label":"Take the belt.","next":"take_the_belt"}]}]}}',0,1,0,NULL),
('waer_singer','Somebody singing','Waerfolk',NULL,'assets/images/npcs/jester.png','jester',1,'{"start":"greeting","nodes":{"greeting":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"mere_fate","equals":"answered"}],"text":"The singing has changed. It is the same tune and there are words in it now, and the words are names, and every so often somebody in a doorway supplies one and the singer takes it and puts it in.","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]},{"pool":"idle","conditions":[{"flag":"met_ellsa"}],"text":"\\"It is not a good song,\\" the singer says, breaking off. \\"It has been sung here for four hundred years and it is not a good song. That is not what it is for.\\" And carries on.","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]},{"pool":"idle","conditions":[{"flag":"met_ellsa"}],"text":"\\"Six houses,\\" the singer says, tapping a corner stone with a knuckle as they pass it. \\"That mark. And that one. All six still standing and all six still in it. Go and look at the charter and see how many of the League''s names are still in Lathmere.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]},{"pool":"idle","conditions":[{"flag":"met_ellsa"}],"text":"They are singing the third verse, which is about a boat that did not come back, and the two women mending nets in the doorway have joined in on it without appearing to have decided to.","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]},{"text":"Somebody is singing in the square, badly, with total confidence, and stops when you come near enough to be a stranger.\\n\\n\\"You are up from the gate,\\" they say, not unkindly. \\"You can tell. You are walking like the lanes are going to open out again.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}]}}',0,0,1,NULL),
('wat_fenner','Wat Fenner','Gateman','Turns the wheel on the flood gate when he is told to. Has never been told why, and has never made that his business.','assets/images/npcs/miner.png','wat_fenner',1,'{"start":"greeting","nodes":{"greeting":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"flood_gate_fate","equals":"opened"}],"once":true,"text":"The gate stands up in its frame and the eastern cut is a river.\\n\\nWat Fenner is sitting on the stone sill with the wheel key across his knees, watching the water go east, and he has plainly been sitting there some hours.\\n\\n\\"Nothing came on the second,\\" he says. \\"First time in a year and two months.\\" He turns the key over. \\"I did not expect it to. I sat here anyway, on account of it being the second.\\"","choices":[{"label":"What will you do?","next":"what_now"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"flood_gate_fate","equals":"key"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"Second of the month,\\" says Wat Fenner, by way of hello, \\"two hours, and then down again. Same as ever.\\" He nods at your belt, where the other key is. \\"And it cannot be worked hard against either of us now, which is the first night''s sleep I have had in a year and two months.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Has anyone asked about the second key?","next":"nobody_has_asked"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"flood_gate_fate","equals":"left"}],"once":true,"text":"Wat Fenner is greasing the worm-screw, which does not need greasing, and both of you know that it is what a man does with his hands when he has been thinking.\\n\\n\\"You came, you looked, you went,\\" he says. \\"That is what everybody does. I do not hold it against anybody and I would like that said, because I can hear how it sounded.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Do you want it opened?","next":"does_he_want_it"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_flood_gate","stage":"who_pays_wat"}],"text":"\\"So now you know the whole of it,\\" says Wat Fenner, \\"which is more than I do, and I have been here a year and two months.\\"\\n\\nHe steps back from the wheel and stands aside, deliberately, so that nothing in the room is between you and it.\\n\\n\\"There it is,\\" he says. \\"I am not going to fight anybody over a gate.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Wind it up. Let the water in.","next":"opens_it"},{"label":"Give me the key. Then it takes two of us either way.","next":"the_key"},{"label":"Leave it shut. Go on with your Tuesdays.","next":"leaves_it"}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_flood_gate","status":"active"}],"text":"\\"Still here,\\" says Wat Fenner. \\"Still shut, except the second, and I have still not been told why, and you have still not stopped looking at the grease.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Tell me about the second of the month.","next":"the_second"},{"label":"Later.","end":true}]},{"text":"There is a man here, which you were not expecting.\\n\\nHe is about sixty, in a good oilskin, and he is sitting on the stone sill beside a gate the size of a barn door with a lamp that has proper glass in it, and he watches you come the whole way up the cut without getting up.\\n\\n\\"Wat Fenner,\\" he says. \\"I keep the gate. Do not touch the wheel.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Somebody has spent money on this gate.","next":"the_new_work","effects":[{"start_quest":"the_flood_gate"}]},{"label":"Who do you keep it for?","next":"the_new_work","effects":[{"start_quest":"the_flood_gate"}]},{"label":"I won''t touch the wheel.","end":true}]}],"the_new_work":[{"text":"\\"They have,\\" Wat agrees, which is not what you expected either.\\n\\n\\"New pintles. New packing. A worm-screw off a proper millwright and not a smith, which you can tell by the cut of the thread.\\" He pats the frame. \\"Two hundred years of brick down here and every yard of it going soft, and this is the one thing in Undertown that anybody has spent a coin on since the second flood.\\"\\n\\nHe looks at it with a professional''s plain pleasure.\\n\\n\\"I was a lock-keeper on the river for thirty-one years,\\" he says, \\"before the barges stopped. I know what good work costs. Somebody paid for this properly and did not ask anybody''s leave and did not tell the town, and I would love to be able to tell you who.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_flood_gate","stage":"look_at_the_gate"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera: \\"Nobody repairs a thing they do not intend to use. Ask him how often it moves.\\""},{"companion":"ilse","conditions":[{"not":{"companion":"sera","status":"active"}}],"text":"Ilse is looking east, past the gate, at four hundred feet of tunnel that is not wet. \\"Everything down here is wet,\\" she says. \\"That is not.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"You''d love to. So you don''t know.","next":"the_second"},{"label":"What''s east of the gate?","next":"what_is_east"}]}],"what_is_east":[{"text":"\\"Dry cut,\\" says Wat Fenner. \\"Four hundred feet of it, straight, and then it turns down and I have not been round the turn.\\"\\n\\nHe says the last part without any embarrassment.\\n\\n\\"I am sixty-one and I am paid to work a wheel,\\" he says. \\"A man who goes round the turn is a man who has decided to find something out, and once you have found a thing out you cannot go back to being the man who works the wheel. I have thought about that a great deal and I would rather you did not sneer at it until you have.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Nobody''s sneering. Who pays you?","next":"the_second"}]}],"the_second":[{"text":"\\"Four silver a month,\\" says Wat Fenner. \\"Wrapped in paper, under the third brick of the sill, and it has never once been late.\\"\\n\\nHe holds up the square iron key.\\n\\n\\"And the whole of my instruction, which came the same way and which I have kept, is this: gate stays shut. On the second of each month, wind it up for two hours, and then wind it down.\\"\\n\\nHe puts the key back inside his coat.\\n\\n\\"A year and two months. Fourteen times. I have never been told why, I have never met anybody, and nobody has ever come to see whether I have done it.\\" A pause. \\"That is the part I mind. Not the money and not the secrecy. That nobody has ever once come to check.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_flood_gate","stage":"who_pays_wat"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera: \\"They do not need to check. If he stopped, the cut would flood, and whatever is down there would tell them for him.\\""},{"companion":"kessa","conditions":[{"not":{"companion":"sera","status":"active"}}],"text":"Kessa: \\"Two hours a month. That''s a delivery. Something goes through and something comes back.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"What comes through in those two hours?","next":"the_two_hours"},{"label":"Wind it up. Let the water in.","next":"opens_it"},{"label":"Give me the key.","next":"the_key"},{"label":"Leave it shut.","next":"leaves_it"}]}],"the_two_hours":[{"text":"\\"Nothing I have seen,\\" says Wat Fenner, \\"and I have looked, and I have been ashamed of looking, and I have looked anyway.\\"\\n\\nHe rubs the grease off his thumb.\\n\\n\\"I wind it up. I sit here. Nothing comes west. Nothing goes east past me. At the two hours I wind it down.\\" He shrugs. \\"So either it is done a long way beyond the turn where I cannot hear it, or the whole thing is a habit that outlived whatever it was for. I have had fourteen months to pick between those and I keep changing my mind.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Wind it up. Let the water in.","next":"opens_it"},{"label":"Give me the key.","next":"the_key"},{"label":"Leave it shut.","next":"leaves_it"}]}],"opens_it":[{"text":"It takes both of you and about a quarter of an hour, and Wat Fenner does not once argue and does not once help more than he is asked to.\\n\\nThe water goes east. It does not stop going for two days.\\n\\nWhatever was being kept dry beyond the turn is not dry now. You never see what it was.\\n\\nWhat you do see is the two lowest bays of the Low Court standing in four feet of it, and Meg Corrow''s whole winter store gone soft, and Aggie Slate making you come down and stand in it and look — not saying anything, which is very much worse than saying something.\\n\\nWat Fenner''s four silver does not come the next month. He sits on the sill on the second anyway.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_flood_gate","stage":"opened_the_gate"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse: \\"You have hurt the people who live here to inconvenience people who do not.\\" A pause. \\"I would probably have done it too. I want to be honest about that.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Leave it standing open.","end":true}]}],"the_key":[{"text":"Wat Fenner hands it over without a word, and then stands there for a moment as though he has put down something heavy.\\n\\n\\"There were two,\\" he says. \\"They gave me the one and I have never known where the other is. Without a key the wheel spins free and the gate does whatever it was last told to do.\\"\\n\\nHe sits back down on the sill.\\n\\n\\"So now it cannot be worked hard against me without you,\\" he says, \\"and it cannot be worked hard against you without me. That is two people in an arrangement that has had one in it for a year and two months.\\"\\n\\nHe looks at the gate.\\n\\n\\"I am relieved,\\" says Wat Fenner, sounding surprised about it. \\"I did not expect to be relieved.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_flood_gate","stage":"took_the_wheel_key"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric: \\"He has been alone with that for fourteen months and nobody has once asked him about it. That is the whole of what was wrong here.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Second of the month, then.","end":true}]}],"leaves_it":[{"text":"\\"Right,\\" says Wat Fenner, and goes back to the sill, and is neither disappointed nor relieved, which somehow lands harder than either would.\\n\\n\\"Second of the month, two hours, and down again,\\" he says. \\"Four silver under the third brick. I shall be here.\\"\\n\\nHe picks up the grease rag.\\n\\n\\"It is the cheapest arrangement I have ever been part of,\\" he says, \\"and I was thirty-one years on the river, where they are all cheap.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_flood_gate","stage":"left_it_shut"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}],"what_now":[{"text":"\\"Sit here,\\" says Wat Fenner. \\"Somebody will come and look at it eventually, and when they do I should like to be the man sitting on the sill rather than the man who was not.\\"\\n\\nHe turns the key over.\\n\\n\\"And they will come,\\" he says. \\"That is the thing I have got out of all this. Somebody paid a millwright''s price to hang that gate. Nobody spends that and then does not come back.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}],"nobody_has_asked":[{"text":"\\"Not a soul,\\" says Wat Fenner. \\"Four silver on the second, under the third brick, same as ever. If they know you have it they do not mind, and if they do not know then nobody is looking, and I could not tell you which of those keeps me up more.\\"\\n\\nHe wipes the wheel down.\\n\\n\\"Come on the second, if you are ever near,\\" he says. \\"It is two hours and there is nothing to see and I would be glad of the company, which I have not said out loud to anybody in a year.\\"","choices":[{"label":"I''ll come on the second.","end":true}]}],"does_he_want_it":[{"text":"Wat Fenner takes a long time over that one.\\n\\n\\"No,\\" he says. \\"There are sixty people at the lamps and the two lowest bays would go, and I have carried a chair for Meg Corrow.\\"\\n\\nHe puts the rag down.\\n\\n\\"But I should like it *found out*,\\" he says. \\"That is a different thing and I have only lately been able to tell the two apart. I want to know. I do not want anybody to drown for my knowing. I have not worked out how a man gets the first without the second and I have had fourteen months at it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}]}}',0,1,0,NULL),
('watch_gate','A watchman','Town Watch',NULL,'assets/images/npcs/captain.png','captain',8,'{"start":"hail","nodes":{"hail":[{"text":"Road''s been quiet since the wilds opened up. Too quiet, the captain says.","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}]}}',0,0,1,NULL),
('watch_relief','A watchman','Town Watch',NULL,'assets/images/npcs/monk.png','monk',1,'{"start":"hail","nodes":{"hail":[{"text":"Half a shift left. Then bread, and a chair, and nobody talking to me.","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}]}}',0,0,1,NULL),
('watch_square','A watchwoman','Town Watch',NULL,'assets/images/npcs/paladin.png','paladin',1,'{"start":"hail","nodes":{"hail":[{"text":"Move along, or buy something. The stalls pay for this square.","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}]}}',0,0,1,NULL),
('watch_street','A watchman','Town Watch',NULL,'assets/images/npcs/fighter.png','fighter',8,'{"start":"hail","nodes":{"hail":[{"text":"Keep to the street after dark. The lamps are there for a reason.","choices":[{"label":"Leave them to it.","end":true}]}]}}',0,0,1,NULL),
('wenna_coyle','Wenna Coyle','Timekeeper','Dry, warm and precise in a hut where nothing else is any of those. She knows where two hundred people were standing at any hour of the last three years, and considers that an ordinary thing to know.','assets/images/npcs/warlock.png','wenna_coyle',1,'{"start":"greeting","nodes":{"greeting":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"works_alarmed"}],"text":"The hut window is shuttered and Coyle has the ledgers off the rack and stacked. \\"Four wardens and an overseer,\\" she says, without looking up from what she is writing. \\"I have them down as an incident. Incidents go in the day book and the day book goes downriver on the fifth.\\" She blots it. \\"I am not frightened of you. I am a great deal more use to this works than Rourke was and everybody including Rourke knew it.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Will they replace him?","next":"by_the_fifteenth"},{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_indentured","stage":"the_arithmetic"}],"text":"\\"Dale''s account,\\" Coyle says, and has it out of the rack before you have finished the sentence, because she has it out of the rack twice a week for somebody. \\"Here is his indenture. Here is the reckoning of days. You will notice they are two sheets.\\" She sets them side by side, squarely. \\"He has been given the first one. He has never asked for the second. I would give it to him. Nobody has ever asked.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Show me the second sheet.","next":"the_second_sheet"},{"label":"Later.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_dig","stage":"the_board"}],"text":"The hut is warm and dry and better lit than anywhere else down here, and the window faces the muster line, and the window was positioned. Coyle looks up from the water clock. \\"You have been on my floor for an hour and forty minutes and you have not got a peg,\\" she says. \\"That is not an accusation. It is a column I cannot fill.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Then give me a peg.","effects":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_dig","stage":"signed_on"}}],"next":"peg_one_oh_four"},{"label":"I''m not going in your book.","effects":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_dig","stage":"walked_in_unasked"}}],"next":"the_other_sheet"},{"label":"Then I''ll go back up the stair.","effects":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_dig","stage":"never_counted"}}],"next":"she_writes_that_too"}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"what_the_quarry_fell_on","stage":"the_works"}],"text":"\\"You have walked the muster floor, the canteen, four of the six faces and the wet way,\\" Coyle says, \\"which I know because there is a boy on each of them whose job is to know.\\" She is not threatening you. She is telling you about her system, of which she is quietly and completely proud. \\"What is it you are counting? I will save you the walk. I count everything here and I am better at it than you are.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Two hundred people and nobody guarding the door.","effects":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"what_the_quarry_fell_on","stage":"walked_the_works"}}],"next":"no_door_to_guard"},{"label":"Nothing. I''m going back up.","effects":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"what_the_quarry_fell_on","stage":"turned_back"}}],"next":"she_writes_that_too"}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"concern_answering"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"I''m to send word if you come through the yard,\\" Coyle says, before you have opened your mouth. \\"There''s a form for it. There''s a space on it for the hour.\\" She puts the form face down under the ledger, which is not the same as tearing it up, and she does not pretend that it is. \\"Word goes at the end of the shift, with the tallies, because that''s when word goes.\\" She turns the water clock a quarter and sets it down. \\"You have until the end of the shift.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Why tell me at all?","next":"the_end_of_the_shift"},{"label":"Then send it.","next":"the_end_of_the_shift"},{"label":"Go.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"concern_asking"}],"once":true,"text":"Coyle has a sheet out that is not a time sheet. \\"You''re on this one,\\" she says, and does not turn it round. \\"It came up on the wages cart with the pegs. It isn''t signed and it doesn''t say what it''s for, and I''ve put it in the box with the ones that are signed, because there''s nowhere else in this hut to put a thing like that.\\" She writes the date in the corner of it. \\"I write down what I see. I''ve written that you were here, and when, and how long, which is what I do for a hundred and four other people, and I''m not going to start doing it differently for you.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Whose hand is it?","next":"not_my_column"},{"label":"Fair enough.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"dig_fate","equals":"signed"}],"text":"\\"One hundred and four,\\" Coyle says, without looking up, because she knew which peg came through the door by the sound of the boots. \\"Lamp back at the end of shift. If you break the gauze you say so; a man who hides a broken gauze kills eleven people and himself.\\"","choices":[{"label":"What''s at the bottom of it all?","next":"not_my_column"},{"label":"Who runs the works?","next":"rourke"},{"label":"Nothing. Carry on.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"dig_fate","equals":"unasked"}],"text":"Coyle glances up, notes the time, and writes it on the second sheet without comment, the way she has every time since. \\"You are on my other list,\\" she says, \\"and you are the only name on it that keeps coming back, which I have found interesting enough to mention to nobody.\\"","choices":[{"label":"What''s at the bottom of it all?","next":"not_my_column"},{"label":"Who runs the works?","next":"rourke"},{"label":"Nothing. Carry on.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"dig_fate","equals":"never"}],"text":"\\"You went back up the stair,\\" Coyle says, \\"and now you are down it again, and both are written down, and I shall write this one down as well.\\" She dips the pen. \\"I am not making a point. It is simply what I do with everything that happens on this floor.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Give me a peg after all.","next":"the_board"},{"label":"Who runs the works?","next":"rourke"},{"label":"Nothing. Carry on.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"quarry_fell_fate","equals":"walked"}],"text":"\\"You have had the whole of it now,\\" Coyle says. \\"Muster, canteen, six faces, the wet way and the office ladder.\\" She turns a peg on the board and does not look at you while she does it. \\"There is nothing else to see. That is what people never believe and it is why they keep coming back down.\\"","choices":[{"label":"What''s at the bottom of it all?","next":"not_my_column"},{"label":"Who runs the works?","next":"rourke"},{"label":"Nothing. Carry on.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"quarry_fell_fate","equals":"back"}],"text":"\\"You were on the muster floor for eleven minutes on the ninth and you left,\\" Coyle says, \\"and here you are.\\" She is not gloating. She is doing what she does. \\"People come back. In three years I have had four who came down, went up, and did not return, and two of those were dead within the year of ordinary things.\\"","choices":[{"label":"What''s at the bottom of it all?","next":"not_my_column"},{"label":"Who runs the works?","next":"rourke"},{"label":"Nothing. Carry on.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"counted_in"}],"text":"\\"One hundred and four,\\" Coyle says, without looking up, because she knew which peg came through the door by the sound of the boots. \\"Lamp back at the end of shift. If you break the gauze you say so; a man who hides a broken gauze kills eleven people and himself.\\"","choices":[{"label":"What''s at the bottom of it all?","next":"not_my_column"},{"label":"Who runs the works?","next":"rourke"},{"label":"Nothing. Carry on.","end":true}]},{"text":"A plank hut with a window in it, warm and dry, with a water clock, a bell rope, a rack of tallies and four ledgers. The woman inside is writing, and goes on writing for exactly as long as it takes her to finish the line. \\"Coyle,\\" she says. \\"Timekeeper. Peg number.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"met_coyle"},{"start_quest":"what_the_quarry_fell_on"},{"start_quest":"the_dig"}],"choices":[{"label":"I haven''t got a peg number.","next":"then_we_shall_see"},{"label":"What is this place?","next":"what_this_is"},{"label":"Sorry. Wrong hut.","end":true}]}],"then_we_shall_see":[{"text":"Coyle puts the pen down and looks at you properly, and it is the most attention anyone has paid you since you came down the stair. \\"Then you are one of four things,\\" she says. \\"New, lost, a thief, or from downriver. Three of those I have a form for.\\" She waits. She is not going to call anybody. She is going to wait.","interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera: \\"She has not asked who we are. She has asked which box we go in. Those are different questions and the second one is much harder to lie to.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Give her a name and take a peg.","effects":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_dig","stage":"the_board"}}],"next":"the_board"},{"label":"What is this place?","next":"what_this_is"},{"label":"Say nothing and walk out.","end":true}]}],"what_this_is":[{"on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"what_the_quarry_fell_on","stage":"the_works"}}],"text":"\\"The works,\\" Coyle says. \\"Six faces, two hundred and eleven on the books, a hundred and ninety on today. Three shifts, two bells, one canteen. Timber is brought down the stair and spoil is carried up it and both are counted.\\" She indicates the board through the window without turning round. \\"You are asking me what it is. That is what it is. There is not a second answer underneath the first one and people are always disappointed by that.\\"","choices":[{"label":"What are you digging for?","next":"not_my_column"},{"label":"Give me a peg, then.","effects":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_dig","stage":"the_board"}}],"next":"the_board"},{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]}],"the_board":[{"text":"She turns the ledger round so you can see it, which nobody down here has ever done for you. Two hundred and eleven names, a peg number against each, and a column of hours going back three years without a single gap in it. \\"That is the works,\\" she says. \\"Not the stone. This. Anybody can dig a hole. Keeping two hundred people in one for three years without a riot is the trade.\\"","interjections":[{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse: \\"Three years. You have been under my warren for three years.\\" Coyle, mildly: \\"Two years and nine months. I would not want you to say three in front of Rourke; he is sensitive about the first winter.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Then put me in it.","effects":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_dig","stage":"signed_on"}}],"next":"peg_one_oh_four"},{"label":"I''d rather not be in anybody''s book.","effects":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_dig","stage":"walked_in_unasked"}}],"next":"the_other_sheet"},{"label":"Who keeps the book above yours?","next":"rourke"}]}],"peg_one_oh_four":[{"text":"\\"One hundred and four,\\" she says, writing it. She does not ask for a second name. She hands you a caged lamp and a tally with the number scratched on it and turns a peg on the board, and that is the whole of it: you are now a fact about this place, and nobody down here will look at you again for as long as the peg is turned.","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"counted_in"}],"interjections":[{"companion":"kessa","text":"Kessa: \\"That''s it? That''s the security?\\" Coyle: \\"That is the security. It has held for three years and your friend the warden''s fence has not.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Go to work.","end":true}]}],"the_other_sheet":[{"text":"\\"Then you go on the other sheet,\\" Coyle says, and gets out another sheet, and writes on it in exactly the same hand at exactly the same speed. \\"Persons on the works not on the books. It is a short list and I have kept it since the first winter and nobody has ever asked to see it.\\" She blots it. \\"I am not annoyed. The works have a column for everything and it is better for everybody if the thing is in the right column.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"coyle_noted_us"}],"choices":[{"label":"Who reads that sheet?","next":"rourke"},{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]}],"she_writes_that_too":[{"text":"She writes down the time you left, because she writes down the time everybody leaves, and goes back to the water clock. Halfway up the stair you realise that in the whole exchange she never once asked your business, and that this is because she has never needed to know anybody''s business in her life.","choices":[{"label":"Keep climbing.","end":true}]}],"no_door_to_guard":[{"text":"\\"There is no door,\\" Coyle agrees, pleased that you have got there. \\"A door needs a man and a man needs paying and a man can be got round. A peg cannot be got round. If you are not on the board you are not fed, not lamped, not paid and not carried out if the roof goes.\\" She dips the pen. \\"Everybody down here could walk up that stair this afternoon. Not one of them will, and I have not spent a penny on stopping them.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"deepworks_known"}],"interjections":[{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric: \\"You have made a prison out of a ledger.\\" Coyle, without heat: \\"I have made a payroll, brother. You are the one who said prison.\\""},{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera has gone white. \\"I could have written this,\\" she says. \\"That is what I keep thinking. I could have written every page of this.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"What is at the bottom of it?","next":"not_my_column"},{"label":"Who runs it?","next":"rourke"},{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]}],"not_my_column":[{"text":"\\"Not my column,\\" Coyle says. \\"I have hours, heads, timber, oil, spoil and the bell. Where the spoil comes from is Rourke''s book and what it is for is downriver''s book, and I have never opened either and I would be very poor at it.\\" She goes back to the clock. \\"You will find that everybody here can tell you their own column exactly. That is not stupidity. It is how a thing this size is done at all.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Who is Rourke?","next":"rourke"},{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]}],"rourke":[{"text":"\\"Overseer. Up the ladder above the headings, with the shutter open, which is where he is meant to be and where he likes to be.\\" A small, dry pause. \\"He came up through the faces and he will tell you so within four minutes. He keeps the working paper. If you want to know what is being cut and why the six faces point where they point, that is his rack and not mine.\\"","on_enter":[{"start_quest":"the_overseers_office"}],"choices":[{"label":"Up the ladder, then.","end":true},{"label":"Back.","next":"not_my_column"}]}],"the_second_sheet":[{"text":"It is the reckoning of days, and it is entirely correct, and it does not balance in his favour anywhere. Days lost to water. Days lost to a bad face. Lamp against wages. Bread against wages at a halfpenny over. Carriage of the cow, entered once and then entered again as keep. Every line of it is honest and the sum of it is that Corrin Dale signed for three years and has bought himself about eight months.","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"saw_the_arithmetic"}],"interjections":[{"companion":"kessa","text":"Kessa: \\"There''s no cheating on this.\\" Coyle: \\"None. I would not keep a crooked book. It would be worse for me and it is not necessary.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Copy it. He should see it.","next":"she_lets_you"},{"label":"Put it back.","end":true}]}],"she_lets_you":[{"text":"She turns the sheet round for you and finds you a pen and does not watch while you copy it, and at the end she blots it for you out of habit. \\"Tell him it is not a trick,\\" she says. \\"That is the thing they never believe, and it is the only part of this that has ever kept me awake. There is no trick in it. It is just the arithmetic, done properly, by somebody who was paid to do it properly.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Take it to him.","end":true}]}],"by_the_fifteenth":[{"text":"\\"By the fifteenth,\\" Coyle says. \\"There is a man at Greyhythe who does nothing else. The works will lose four days and the four days will come off everybody''s reckoning, which is the part you did not think about and which I shall now have to explain a hundred and ninety times.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Leave.","end":true}]}],"the_end_of_the_shift":[{"text":"\\"Because a hundred and ninety people work under me and every one of them knows exactly what I write down about them,\\" Coyle says. \\"That is the arrangement. It is the only thing I have that makes the day book worth anything, and I am not spending it on you.\\" She looks at the shuttered window and then back at the clock. \\"The bell is at the end of the shift. I am not going to ring it early and I am not going to ring it late.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Go.","end":true}]}]}}',0,1,0,NULL),
('wick_lorrimer','Wick Lorrimer','Miller','The mill burned four years ago. Wick did not, and did not leave, and keeps the race clear out of a habit that has outlasted the reason for it.','assets/images/npcs/prisoner_1.png','wick_lorrimer',1,'{"start":"hail","nodes":{"hail":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"wick_in_town"}],"text":"There is nobody at the mill. The pot is gone off the stone by the wheel-pit and the corner under the sound gable has been swept out, which he did before he left, because of course he did.\\n\\nThe race still runs.","choices":[{"label":"Walk on.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"miller_fate","equals":"stayed"}],"once":true,"text":"There is a roof on the sound gable now and a door that shuts, and Wick Lorrimer is standing in the wheel-pit up to his knees clearing weed out of the race.\\n\\n\\"Want doing again Thursday,\\" he says. \\"Everything wants doing again Thursday.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Thursday, then.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"miller_fate","equals":"paper"}],"once":true,"text":"\\"They''ve had it copied,\\" Wick says. \\"Whole thing, into the town''s book, with the date on it.\\" He is up to his knees in the race and does not appear to consider this the important part of his week. \\"Man asked me if I understood what it meant. I said no. He explained it. I still said no.\\"\\n\\nA pause.\\n\\n\\"Does it mean I have to move?\\"","choices":[{"label":"No.","next":"he_does_not_have_to_move"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_miller_who_stayed","stage":"the_race_he_keeps"}],"text":"He is in the race again with the weed-hook, which is where he has been every time you have come.\\n\\n\\"You''ll be back about the letter,\\" he says, without looking up. \\"I did wonder if you''d work out that was the question.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Show me the letter.","next":"the_letter"}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_burnt_mill","stage":"the_counting_loft"}],"text":"\\"You''ve been looking at the beam-ends,\\" Wick says. \\"Everyone who''s ever come out here has looked at the beam-ends for about four seconds and then looked at me instead.\\"\\n\\nHe puts the hook down.\\n\\n\\"Go on, then. Which end did it start.\\"","choices":[{"label":"The counting loft. River side, high.","next":"the_loft"},{"label":"You tell me.","next":"the_loft"}]},{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_burnt_mill","stage":"go_and_look"}],"text":"There is a man in the wheel-pit up to his knees in black water with a weed-hook, and he has heard you coming and has decided to finish the length he is on first.\\n\\n\\"Wick Lorrimer,\\" he says eventually. \\"Milled here eighteen years.\\" He straightens up. \\"It burned four years ago. You''ll have heard that in town. That''s usually all anyone''s heard.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"met_wick"}],"choices":[{"label":"Nobody in town knows which year.","next":"which_year"},{"label":"Why are you clearing a race that turns nothing?","next":"why_the_race"},{"label":"Where did the fire start?","next":"the_loft"}]},{"text":"There is a man in the wheel-pit, up to his knees in black water, working a weed-hook along a race that runs perfectly well past a building that has not needed it in four years.\\n\\n\\"You''ll want the road,\\" he says. \\"It''s downstream.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"met_wick"},{"start_quest":"the_burnt_mill"}],"interjections":[{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric: \\"There is a pot on that stone and it has been used this week.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"You live here.","next":"which_year"},{"label":"Why keep the race clear?","next":"why_the_race"},{"label":"The road, then.","end":true}]}],"he_does_not_have_to_move":[{"text":"\\"Right,\\" he says, and goes back to the weed. \\"Good. Race wants doing Thursday.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Thursday.","end":true}]}],"which_year":[{"text":"\\"Four years,\\" Wick says. \\"Second week after Michaelmas. Everybody in Rivermark can tell you the mill burned and not one of them can tell you the year, and I''ve thought about why that is more than is good for me.\\"\\n\\nHe wipes the hook on his leg.\\n\\n\\"It''s because the mill burning isn''t a thing that happened to anybody. It''s just a thing about the road now. Like the ford.\\"","on_enter":[{"start_quest":"the_miller_who_stayed"}],"choices":[{"label":"Where did it start?","next":"the_loft"},{"label":"Why are you still here?","next":"why_the_race"},{"label":"Leave him to it.","end":true}]}],"the_loft":[{"text":"Every charred beam-end in the standing walls points inward, which is what happens when a roof comes down on a fire rather than a fire going up into a roof.\\n\\nAnd the scorching is worst at the river end, high, on the stone that carried the counting loft — where the mill kept its paper, its tallies and its books, and where nobody ever lit anything, because you do not put a flame in a room full of dust and ledgers.\\n\\n\\"Aye,\\" Wick says, from the pit. \\"I said that to the watch. The watch wrote it down.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_burnt_mill","stage":"the_counting_loft"}},{"set_flag":"mill_loft_scorch"}],"interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera has her hand flat on the scorched stone. \\"No flue,\\" she says. \\"No lamp bracket. No hearth on this wall at any level.\\""},{"companion":"kessa","text":"Kessa: \\"Somebody stood in a room full of paper and started a fire in it.\\" Wick, mildly: \\"That''s the shape of it, aye.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Then I''ll write it down properly and it''ll stay written.","next":"found_the_cause"},{"label":"This place could be a shelter. Let''s make it one.","next":"made_a_shelter"},{"label":"Why do you still clear the race?","next":"why_the_race"},{"label":"Give me the hook. I''ll do the pit.","next":"the_wheel_pit"},{"label":"Three walls and running water. I''ll leave it.","next":"left_the_mill"}]}],"the_wheel_pit":[{"text":"He hands the hook over without an argument, which after four years of doing it himself is either trust or exhaustion.\\n\\nThe pit is stone, ten feet deep, and it holds four years of black water over four years of grain-dust that came off the floor above when the floor above came down. Everything that has been living off that comes up the wall at once.","on_enter":[{"start_combat":"mill_race_vermin"}],"choices":[{"label":"Back to the wall.","end":true}]}],"found_the_cause":[{"text":"It goes down in full: the beam-ends, the inward fall, the scorching high on the river wall where there was never a flue, a lamp bracket or a hearth at any level, and the fact that the room that burned first was the room the paper was in.\\n\\nIt does not tell you who. It is not going to. What it does is exist, in writing, with a date on it, which is more than the four years before it managed.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_burnt_mill","stage":"found_the_cause"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Close the book.","end":true}]}],"made_a_shelter":[{"text":"Two days: the roof-fall out of the south end, hurdles across the gap, a slate over the fire-back, and the door re-hung on the pins that were still in the frame because they were iron and iron does not burn.\\n\\nWick watched all of it and helped with none of it and did not once suggest you stop. On the way back you tell the fold at the drovers'' rest that there is a dry wall two miles up the road. Four of them use it inside the week.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_burnt_mill","stage":"made_it_a_shelter"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric, on the roof, with a hurdle: \\"This is the only thing we have done this month that will still be doing its job in ten years.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Leave the door on the latch.","end":true}]}],"left_the_mill":[{"text":"\\"Right,\\" Wick says, and goes back to the weed.\\n\\nThe race runs. It is the only thing here that still does its job.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_burnt_mill","stage":"left_the_mill"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Go.","end":true}]}],"why_the_race":[{"text":"Wick Lorrimer stops working, which he has not done for anything else you have said.\\n\\n\\"A race that''s kept is a race that''s still got a right on it,\\" he says. \\"A race that''s silted is a ditch, and a ditch hasn''t got a right on it, and after seven years a right nobody''s used stops being a right at all.\\"\\n\\nHe leans on the hook.\\n\\n\\"I didn''t know that. I worked it out about eleven months after the fire, off a letter that came here by mistake, and I''ve cleared this race every week since.\\"","on_enter":[{"start_quest":"the_miller_who_stayed"},{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_miller_who_stayed","stage":"the_race_he_keeps"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera: \\"Seven years. He has kept a water right alive with a hook and a bad back for four of them.\\""},{"companion":"ilse","text":"Ilse: \\"Whose letter.\\" Wick: \\"That''s the question, isn''t it. I''ve had four years on that one.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Show me the letter.","next":"the_letter"},{"label":"Come into town. You can''t winter here again.","next":"bring_him_in"},{"label":"Later.","end":true}]}],"the_letter":[{"text":"It is an instruction to a clerk, not a letter to a man, and it came to the mill because somebody''s boy could not read a direction. It concerns the water right on the mill race: what it is, who holds it, what would need to happen for it to fall in, and what the holding is expected to be worth once it does.\\n\\nIt is dated three weeks before the fire.\\n\\n\\"Aye,\\" Wick says. \\"That''s the bit.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"mill_race_letter_seen"}],"interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera reads it twice and then reads the date twice. \\"Three weeks,\\" she says. \\"That is not a coincidence and it is also not evidence, and I hate that both of those are true.\\""},{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric: \\"He has had this in a burnt building for four years.\\" Wick: \\"Where else was I going to put it.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"This goes to the counting house and gets entered.","next":"to_paper"},{"label":"Come into town with me.","next":"bring_him_in"},{"label":"Then stay, and we''ll make this place fit to stay in.","next":"let_him_stay"}]}],"to_paper":[{"text":"The counting house copies it, dates it, and files it against the mill, and the clerk who does it goes quiet halfway through and starts again from the top.\\n\\nThe debt on the mill was discharged twice: once by the mill, in the year before the fire, and once by whoever bought the holding after it stopped existing. Both discharges are in the town''s own book, three pages apart, and nobody has ever had cause to read the two of them on the same afternoon.\\n\\nWick does not care about any of this. He asks whether it means he has to move.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_miller_who_stayed","stage":"took_it_to_paper"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"sera","text":"Sera: \\"Paid twice. Somebody bought a debt that was already dead so that there would be a purchase on the record.\\" She stops. \\"That is not fraud. That is worse than fraud, because it is legal.\\""}],"choices":[{"label":"Tell him no.","end":true}]}],"bring_him_in":[{"text":"It takes most of an evening to talk him into it and about four minutes for him to pack, which are two facts that sit badly together.\\n\\nHe sleeps in the Flagon''s back room on Mara''s charity, which she will be calling rent inside a fortnight in order to spare him. On the first night he sits up in the common room until the fire goes out, because he has not been indoors after dark in four years and cannot work out what a person does in a room.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_miller_who_stayed","stage":"brought_him_in"}}],"interjections":[{"companion":"aldric","text":"Aldric sits up with him and does not talk, which is the correct thing and is not an obvious one."}],"choices":[{"label":"Let him sit.","end":true}]}],"let_him_stay":[{"text":"A roof on the sound gable, a door that shuts, the wheel-pit swept, and two days'' work that he did not ask for and did not stop.\\n\\nHe does not thank anybody. At the end of it he says the race will want doing again on Thursday, which he says about everything, and which you have started to understand is how this man says most things.","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_miller_who_stayed","stage":"let_him_stay"}}],"choices":[{"label":"Thursday, then.","end":true}]}]}}',0,1,0,NULL),
('wren_tallow','Wren Tallow','Camp Outfitter','Brought a cart up the hill nine years ago meaning to sell it out and go home, and has been restocking it ever since. Prices are what they are because the next shop is a day and a half away and she knows exactly how far that is.','assets/images/npcs/wren_tallow.png','wren_tallow',1,'{"start":"hail","nodes":{"hail":[{"once":true,"text":"The cart has been a shop for so long that the wheels have sunk to the axle and somebody has built steps up to it. Wren Tallow sits in the doorway of it with a lamp in pieces on a cloth across her knees.\\n\\n\\"You''ll want to hear the prices before you look at the stock,\\" she says. \\"Everybody does it the other way round and everybody is disappointed. Nearest shop that isn''t mine is a day and a half. That''s the whole of my pricing and I''ll not pretend otherwise.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Let''s see it, then.","effects":[{"open_shop":"wren_tallow"}],"end":true},{"label":"A day and a half is a day and a half.","next":"the_walk"},{"label":"Not today.","end":true}]},{"text":"\\"Back up, then.\\" Wren does not look up from the lamp. \\"Stock''s the same as it was. It always is, until somebody comes up with something I can put on the shelf.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Let''s trade.","effects":[{"open_shop":"wren_tallow"}],"end":true},{"label":"Why are you still here?","next":"still_here"},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}],"the_walk":[{"text":"\\"It is. And a day and a half back, carrying whatever you bought.\\" She seats a pin and turns the lamp over. \\"I have watched four people work that out standing where you are, and go down the hill for a cheaper flask. Two of them came back up for mine at twice what I''d have charged, because by then they''d already spent the week.\\"\\n\\n\\"I''m not proud of the arithmetic. I just do it before you do.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Show me the stock.","effects":[{"open_shop":"wren_tallow"}],"end":true},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}],"still_here":[{"text":"\\"Came up with a cartload meaning to sell it out and go home before the frost.\\" She holds the lamp to the light and looks through the gauze at nothing. \\"Sold it out in nine days. Went down for another. Sold that.\\"\\n\\n\\"That was the ninth year of it, in the spring. There''s no story in me, if that''s what you''re after. I''m the only one here who never had to be talked into staying.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Let''s trade.","effects":[{"open_shop":"wren_tallow"}],"end":true},{"label":"Goodbye.","end":true}]}]}}',1,0,0,NULL),
('yrsa','Yrsa Fell','Of the Wyrdpines','One of the forty-one who reached the Hallowgate with Aske Vorn, and one of the few who will say so out loud. She shoots, she keeps a fire alight in weather that should not allow one, and she has spent four years being told by kind people that she is safe now.','assets/images/npcs/ranger.png','ranger',8,'{"start":"greeting","nodes":{"greeting":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"yrsa_fate","equals":"told"}],"expression":2,"text":"\\"Do not,\\" says Yrsa, before you have said anything. \\"You are about to check on me. I can see you deciding how to phrase it.\\"\\n\\nShe strings the bow and unstrings it again, which is what she does with her hands.\\n\\n\\"I am all right. I said a thing out loud in a shed and nobody died of it. I am going to be insufferable about this for about a fortnight and then I am going to be normal, and I would take it kindly if you let me have the fortnight.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Sit at the fire a while.","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","status":"active"}],"next":"camp"},{"label":"\\"Take the fortnight.\\"","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"yrsa_fate","equals":"unsaid"}],"expression":3,"text":"\\"You are looking at me,\\" says Yrsa, without looking up.\\n\\n\\"I am fine. I have got three sentences about the eleventh night and I say them very well now. Somebody asks, I say them, everybody is comfortable.\\"\\n\\nShe checks the string.\\n\\n\\"You gave me those. I am not being unkind. It was a kindness and I took it and I am not giving it back.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Sit at the fire a while.","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","status":"active"}],"next":"camp"},{"label":"Say nothing.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","status":"active"},{"flag":"forty_one_fate","equals":"named"}],"expression":2,"text":"\\"Nineteen names in a shed,\\" says Yrsa. \\"By village. In order.\\"\\n\\nShe is quiet a moment.\\n\\n\\"I have been four years in a city where nobody said any of them, and I had got used to that, and it turns out that being used to a thing and being all right about it are not the same and I would like to have found that out somewhere with a door.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"I am here.\\"","end":true},{"label":"Sit at the fire a while.","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","status":"active"}],"next":"camp"},{"label":"Later.","end":true}]},{"pool":"idle","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","status":"active"}],"text":"Yrsa has the bow strung and is not shooting anything with it, which is what she does when she is thinking.\\n\\n\\"Where are we going,\\" she says, \\"and is there anywhere on the way where I can get proper nocks. Waerhaven nocks are cut for men with no fingers.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Sit at the fire a while.","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","status":"active"}],"next":"camp"},{"label":"Later.","end":true}]},{"pool":"idle","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","status":"active"}],"text":"\\"This city has no north road,\\" Yrsa says, apropos of nothing at all. \\"Have you noticed that? Everybody here says it as though it were geography.\\"\\n\\nShe unstrings the bow.\\n\\n\\"It is not geography. It is that nobody in four hundred years has wanted to go.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Sit at the fire a while.","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","status":"active"}],"next":"camp"},{"label":"Later.","end":true}]},{"pool":"idle","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","status":"active"}],"text":"Yrsa has got a fire going in weather that should not allow one, out of wet pine, in about four minutes, and is entirely unimpressed with herself about it.\\n\\n\\"Everybody does this,\\" she says. \\"Where I am from everybody does this. It is like being praised for shutting a door.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Sit at the fire a while.","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","status":"active"}],"next":"camp"},{"label":"Later.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"met_yrsa"},{"flag":"vorn_trusts_you"}],"expression":4,"text":"Yrsa is at the end of the long table with her back to the wall and her face to the only door, along with everybody else in the Green Bough, and she is the only one who appears to have noticed that this is what they are all doing.\\n\\n\\"You have been talking to the Warden,\\" she says. \\"Properly, I mean. She has started saying your name in a tone.\\"\\n\\nShe pushes the bench out with a foot.\\n\\n\\"Sit down. I have got a thing to ask you and I have been waiting for somebody she does not manage.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Ask.\\"","next":"the_recruit"},{"label":"Sit at the fire a while.","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","status":"active"}],"next":"camp"},{"label":"Later.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"flag":"met_yrsa"}],"text":"\\"You again,\\" says Yrsa, from the end of the table. \\"Beer is bad. It is bad properly, which is not the same as being bad badly, and I am not explaining that twice.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"You came south with Vorn.\\"","next":"the_road"},{"label":"Sit at the fire a while.","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","status":"active"}],"next":"camp"},{"label":"Leave her to it.","end":true}]},{"text":"A young woman at the end of the long table has a bow across her knees that is a good deal better than anything else in the room, and is watching the door in a way that would be tactful if forty other people in here were not doing it too.\\n\\n\\"Yrsa,\\" she says, when she catches you looking. \\"Fell, if you need the other half, which nobody here does.\\"\\n\\nShe nods at the bench.\\n\\n\\"You are not Watch and you are not Charter. That leaves about four things and I would rather guess than ask.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"met_yrsa"}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"You came south with Warden Vorn.\\"","next":"the_road"},{"label":"\\"That is a very good bow for this room.\\"","next":"the_bow"},{"label":"Sit at the fire a while.","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","status":"active"}],"next":"camp"},{"label":"Leave her to it.","end":true}]}],"the_bow":[{"text":"\\"It is,\\" Yrsa agrees. \\"It is the best thing in this room and the second best is the door.\\"\\n\\nShe turns it over.\\n\\n\\"Wyrdpine yew, cut by somebody in a village that has not sent anybody south in two years, which means there is not going to be another one. I have it restrung twice a year by a man in Tarrow who charges me nothing and will not say why.\\"\\n\\nShe puts it back across her knees.\\n\\n\\"Everybody in Waerhaven who sees this asks me whether I hunt. Nobody has ever asked me what I shoot.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"What do you shoot?\\"","next":"what_she_shoots"},{"label":"\\"You came south with Vorn.\\"","next":"the_road"},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]}],"what_she_shoots":[{"expression":3,"text":"\\"Nothing,\\" says Yrsa. \\"Four years. Not one thing.\\"\\n\\nShe says it perfectly levelly.\\n\\n\\"There is nothing to shoot on a promontory. No deer, no boar, no wolf, no anything, because there is water on three sides and a wall on the fourth and eighty-five miles of forest past that which nobody goes into.\\"\\n\\n\\"I have got the best bow in this ward and I string it and unstring it about nine times a day and I have not put an arrow into a living thing since I was fourteen years old.\\"\\n\\nShe looks at you.\\n\\n\\"That is the first honest sentence I have said to a stranger in this city and I have no idea why I said it to you.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"When you were fourteen.\\"","next":"the_road"},{"label":"Say nothing.","next":"greeting"}]}],"the_road":[{"expression":3,"text":"\\"Sixty of us,\\" says Yrsa. \\"Eighty-five miles. Fourteen nights, because you do not walk it in twelve unless you are prepared to leave people.\\"\\n\\nShe stops.\\n\\n\\"Forty-one arrived. Everybody in this city knows that number. It is the only thing about us anybody has bothered to learn.\\"\\n\\nShe drinks the bad beer.\\n\\n\\"I was fourteen. I was the one who carried the lamp, because a lamp at the back is worth more than a lamp at the front and I was the smallest and I could keep up.\\"\\n\\n\\"I know what happened on the eleventh night. I have got three sentences about the eleventh night that I say when somebody asks and everybody is very comfortable afterwards.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Say the three sentences.\\"","next":"the_three_sentences"},{"label":"\\"I am not going to ask.\\"","next":"not_asking"},{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]}],"the_three_sentences":[{"expression":5,"text":"She says them. They are good sentences. They are worn smooth, the way a step is worn.\\n\\n\\"It was the eleventh night. We lost people. The Warden did what a Warden has to do.\\"\\n\\nThen she stops, and looks at the door, and does not go on, and the whole end of the table very kindly starts talking about something else, and a man puts another cup in front of her without being asked.\\n\\nThis has clearly happened before. It has clearly happened about once a month for four years.","choices":[{"label":"Wait.","next":"nobody_waits"},{"label":"Let the room take it.","next":"not_asking"}]}],"nobody_waits":[{"expression":6,"text":"You wait.\\n\\nThe table goes on talking about something else. The cup sits there. Yrsa looks at the door for a while, and then at you, and then at the door again.\\n\\n\\"You are not saying anything,\\" she says.\\n\\n\\"Nobody does that. Everybody in this room is extremely good to me. Every single time, somebody says something kind at exactly that point, and I have been very grateful for four years, and I have not got to the end of that sentence once.\\"\\n\\nShe puts the cup down untouched.\\n\\n\\"Do not do it again in here. If you are going to make me finish it, do not make me finish it in the Bough.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"yrsa_wants_to_say_it"},{"xp":80}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"Then somewhere else.\\"","end":true},{"label":"\\"Understood.\\"","end":true}]}],"not_asking":[{"expression":2,"text":"\\"Thank you,\\" says Yrsa, and means it, and the room relaxes around her the way it always does.\\n\\nAnd then, about four seconds later, she looks at you with an expression that has some irritation in it.\\n\\n\\"Everybody says that,\\" she says. \\"You are the eleventh person to say that. It is the correct thing to say. I have got no complaint about it at all.\\"\\n\\nShe picks the bow up.\\n\\n\\"It is only that after four years of the correct thing I have started to wonder what the incorrect thing would sound like.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"yrsa_wants_to_say_it"}],"choices":[{"label":"Back.","next":"greeting"}]}],"the_recruit":[{"expression":4,"text":"\\"Take me with you,\\" says Yrsa.\\n\\nShe says it flatly, having clearly practised it, and then goes on before you can answer because she has practised that too.\\n\\n\\"I am not asking to be rescued. I am asked to be rescued about twice a year by people who mean it beautifully. I have got a bed, work when the Oakum House has it, forty people who look after me and a Warden who would put herself between me and a cart.\\"\\n\\nShe puts her hand flat on the table.\\n\\n\\"What I have not got is one person in this city who did not watch me arrive. Every face out there met me at fourteen on the worst night of my life and has been being careful with me ever since, and I love them, and I cannot breathe.\\"\\n\\n\\"You did not meet me on the road. You met me in a room. That is the whole qualification and it is the only one I have ever wanted.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Come on, then.\\"","effects":[{"recruit":"yrsa"},{"xp":100}],"next":"she_comes"},{"label":"\\"Vorn will not thank me.\\"","next":"about_vorn_first"},{"label":"\\"Not yet.\\"","end":true}]}],"about_vorn_first":[{"expression":3,"text":"\\"She will not,\\" agrees Yrsa. \\"She will say yes in about one second and she will not thank you for a year.\\"\\n\\n\\"Aske Vorn brought nineteen people out of the Wyrdpines and lost nineteen and has spent four years keeping every single one of the forty-one alive in a city that has not got a word for us. If I go, that is one of the forty-one out of her sight.\\"\\n\\nShe pulls her coat straight.\\n\\n\\"And she will say yes. Because she knows exactly what she has been doing to us, and she has not been able to stop, and she has been waiting four years for somebody to take one of us out of the room.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"Come on, then.\\"","effects":[{"recruit":"yrsa"},{"xp":100}],"next":"she_comes"},{"label":"\\"Not yet.\\"","end":true}]}],"she_comes":[{"expression":2,"text":"Yrsa gets up, picks up a pack that has been ready by the wall for a considerable time, and is at the door before you have stood up.\\n\\n\\"I packed it in March,\\" she says, seeing your face. \\"That is not sad. That is a person who has been ready.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Go.","end":true}]}],"camp":[{"conditions":[{"quest":"the_nineteen","status":"completed"}],"expression":2,"text":"Yrsa has the fire built the way she builds it, which is small, hot and behind something, and she is sitting where she can see the dark rather than where she can see the flames.\\n\\n\\"Sit down,\\" she says. \\"I am not going to say anything enormous tonight.\\"","choices":[{"label":"\\"How is it, since?\\"","conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"camp_yrsa_fate"}}],"next":"camp_yrsa_fate"},{"label":"Tell me about your village.","conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"camp_yrsa_village"}}],"next":"camp_yrsa_village"},{"label":"That bow.","conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"camp_yrsa_bow"}}],"next":"camp_yrsa_bow"},{"label":"What was the first winter here like?","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","approval_at_least":30},{"not":{"flag":"camp_yrsa_winter"}}],"next":"camp_yrsa_winter"},{"label":"Stay by the fire.","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","romance_stage":1}],"next":"romance_two"},{"label":"Goodnight.","end":true}]},{"conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","status":"active"}],"text":"Yrsa has the fire built small and hot and behind something, and she is sitting where she can see the dark rather than where she can see the flames, which she does every night and has never once mentioned.\\n\\n\\"Sit,\\" she says. \\"You want to ask me something. You have had the face on since the afternoon.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Tell me about your village.","conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"camp_yrsa_village"}}],"next":"camp_yrsa_village"},{"label":"That bow.","conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"camp_yrsa_bow"}}],"next":"camp_yrsa_bow"},{"label":"Everybody in the Bough is careful with you.","conditions":[{"not":{"flag":"camp_yrsa_kindness"}}],"next":"camp_yrsa_kindness"},{"label":"What is Aske Vorn to you?","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","approval_at_least":15},{"not":{"flag":"camp_yrsa_vorn"}}],"next":"camp_yrsa_vorn"},{"label":"What was the first winter here like?","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","approval_at_least":30},{"not":{"flag":"camp_yrsa_winter"}}],"next":"camp_yrsa_winter"},{"label":"Aske Vorn told me what the mark means.","conditions":[{"flag":"nineteen_told"},{"not":{"flag":"camp_yrsa_the_mark"}}],"next":"camp_yrsa_the_mark"},{"label":"The eleventh night. Finish the sentence.","conditions":[{"flag":"yrsa_wants_to_say_it"},{"not":{"flag":"camp_yrsa_eleventh"}}],"next":"the_eleventh_night"},{"label":"\\"Ask me the thing you have not been asking.\\"","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","approval_at_least":30},{"not":{"companion":"yrsa","romance_stage":1}},{"not":{"companion":"yrsa","romance_locked":true}}],"next":"romance_one"},{"label":"Stay by the fire.","conditions":[{"companion":"yrsa","romance_stage":1}],"next":"romance_two"},{"label":"Goodnight.","end":true}]},{"text":"There is no fire and no camp and nobody to sit at it.","choices":[{"label":"Back.","end":true}]}],"camp_yrsa_village":[{"text":"\\"Hallowfell,\\" says Yrsa. \\"Which is a joke, because there is nothing hallowed about it and it is not in a fell. It is eleven houses in a clearing and a byre and a road that goes two places.\\"\\n\\nShe feeds the fire.\\n\\n\\"Sixty-one people. Nine of them came south. Four of the nine are alive and three of the four are in the Wardenswatch and one of them is me.\\"\\n\\nShe says the numbers the way you say numbers you have said to yourself a great many times in the dark.\\n\\n\\"Nobody in Waerhaven has ever asked me the name of it. Four years. Everybody asks how many came out. Nobody asks out of where.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_yrsa_village"},{"approval":{"yrsa":6}}],"choices":[{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}],"expression":3}],"camp_yrsa_bow":[{"text":"\\"Wyrdpine yew,\\" says Yrsa, \\"cut and backed by a man called Orre who taught every child in eleven houses to shoot because there was nothing else to do between the first snow and the thaw.\\"\\n\\nShe runs a thumb along it.\\n\\n\\"He is not one of the nine. He is not one of the sixty, either. He stood at his own door and told the Warden he was too old for eighty-five miles and would rather see to the stock.\\"\\n\\nShe is quiet.\\n\\n\\"That was the sensible thing and everybody agreed and we walked out of the clearing and he shut the door. I have thought about that door a great deal more than I have thought about the eleventh night, and nobody has ever asked me about it, because a shut door is not a story.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_yrsa_bow"},{"approval":{"yrsa":5}}],"choices":[{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}],"expression":3}],"camp_yrsa_kindness":[{"text":"\\"They are,\\" says Yrsa. \\"They are relentlessly careful with me and they have been for four years and it is the kindest thing anyone has ever done and I have started to hate it, and I would like that on the record as a fault of mine and not of theirs.\\"\\n\\nShe pokes the fire.\\n\\n\\"Here is what it is. Forty people out there watched me arrive at fourteen, at the end of it, carrying a lamp. And every single one of them has decided, separately, without discussing it, that Yrsa Fell must never be made to say anything difficult.\\"\\n\\n\\"So they finish my sentences. They change the subject at exactly the right moment. They put another cup down. It is beautifully done and it is done by people who love me and the result is that I am nineteen years old and I have not completed a sentence about my own life since I was fourteen.\\"\\n\\n\\"You waited. That is all you did. You waited about four seconds longer than anybody else has and I have been thinking about it since.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_yrsa_kindness"},{"approval":{"yrsa":7}}],"choices":[{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}],"expression":6}],"camp_yrsa_vorn":[{"text":"\\"She is the reason I am here,\\" says Yrsa, \\"and I mean that in the plain way and not in the warm way.\\"\\n\\nShe watches the dark.\\n\\n\\"She carried me for the last two miles. I was fourteen and I had walked eighty-three of them and I could not do the last two, and she picked me up and carried me and never once mentioned it and does not know that I remember.\\"\\n\\n\\"And on the eleventh night she said a name three times and wrote a mark and walked on, and I was holding the lamp, and I saw her hand while she wrote it.\\"\\n\\nShe looks up.\\n\\n\\"Both of those are the same woman doing the same job. That is the thing about her that this city cannot get hold of. They want her to be one or the other and she is not being brave about it, she is just doing what a Warden does, and it costs exactly what it costs.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_yrsa_vorn"},{"approval":{"yrsa":6}}],"choices":[{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}],"expression":5}],"camp_yrsa_winter":[{"text":"\\"Warm,\\" says Yrsa.\\n\\nShe laughs at your face.\\n\\n\\"That is the answer and it is the one nobody wants. The first winter here was warm. There was a roof, and it did not snow above the ankle once, and there was bread every single day out of a cart, and I lay in the lazar house with forty other people and I was warm every night for four months.\\"\\n\\nShe feeds the fire.\\n\\n\\"And I did not sleep. Not properly, not once, and neither did anybody else in that room, and after about eight weeks somebody worked out why and said it out loud, and it was a man from a village two valleys from mine and he said: it is that we are safe and they are not.\\"\\n\\n\\"Everybody in that room had left a door shut behind them. And we were warm.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_yrsa_winter"},{"approval":{"yrsa":7}}],"choices":[{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}],"expression":6}],"the_eleventh_night":[{"expression":7,"text":"Yrsa puts the bow down, which she does not do.\\n\\n\\"It was the eleventh night,\\" she says. \\"We lost people. The Warden did what a Warden has to do.\\"\\n\\nAnd then she stops, exactly where she always stops, and the fire pops, and nobody says anything kind.\\n\\nShe waits. You wait.\\n\\n\\"I was carrying the lamp,\\" she says. \\"At the back. That is the job you give the smallest one, because the lamp at the back is what stops the line stretching, and a stretched line is how you lose people in the dark.\\"\\n\\n\\"Ilsa Ordsdaughter stopped walking. She was nineteen. She stopped and turned round and faced back up the way we had come and she would not answer her name.\\"\\n\\n\\"The Warden said it three times. That is the rule, it is in the manual, three times. And then she wrote the mark and we went on.\\"\\n\\nShe looks straight at you.\\n\\n\\"And I was at the back with the lamp. So I was the last one past her. And she was still standing there when the light went off her, and she was facing back the way we had come, and she was perfectly calm, and I have never told anybody the last part.\\"\\n\\n\\"She said thank you. As I went past. Quite clearly.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_yrsa_eleventh"},{"start_quest":"the_nineteen"},{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_nineteen","stage":"she_says_it"}},{"approval":{"yrsa":10}},{"xp":150}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"Thank you for what?\\"","next":"thank_you_for_what"},{"label":"Say nothing at all.","next":"thank_you_for_what"}]}],"thank_you_for_what":[{"expression":8,"text":"\\"I do not know,\\" says Yrsa. \\"That is the whole of it. Four years and I do not know and there is nobody left to ask.\\"\\n\\nShe wraps her arms round her knees, which makes her look exactly fourteen.\\n\\n\\"For the lamp, perhaps. For eleven nights of it. Or for going on, which is what I have decided it was at about three in the morning on about four hundred occasions.\\"\\n\\n\\"Or she was thanking somebody else and I happened to be walking past.\\"\\n\\nShe puts her chin on her arms.\\n\\n\\"That is the sentence. That is the end of it. I have been carrying the end of that sentence for four years and it is not even terrible. It is a girl saying thank you. And every single person who loves me has spent four years making very sure I never got to it.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_nineteen","stage":"the_road_south"}}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"Say it in the chapel. All of it. Out loud.\\"","next":"the_chapel"},{"label":"\\"You have said it now. That is enough.\\"","next":"you_need_not"}]}],"the_chapel":[{"expression":6,"text":"She does it on the Thursday, in the Chapel of the Grey Road, in front of about forty people who have been kind to her for four years.\\n\\nShe says the whole thing. Not the three sentences. The lamp, the line, the rule about saying a name three times, Ilsa Ordsdaughter facing back up the road, and the thank you.\\n\\nNobody finishes it for her. Two people try and are stopped — by Aske Vorn, without a word, with a hand.\\n\\nAfterwards Yrsa is furious for about an hour, at everybody, on no particular grounds, and then she is not.\\n\\n\\"That was awful,\\" she says, later, at the fire. \\"That was completely awful and I would like to do the next one on Tuesday.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_nineteen","stage":"told"}}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"There is a next one?\\"","end":true},{"label":"Say nothing.","end":true}]}],"you_need_not":[{"expression":3,"text":"\\"No,\\" agrees Yrsa. \\"No, you are right.\\"\\n\\nShe picks the bow back up, and something in her face closes very slightly and very politely.\\n\\n\\"That is kind. I mean that, it is genuinely kind, and it is the twelfth time and I can tell you the tone of all twelve.\\"\\n\\nShe strings it, checks it, unstrings it.\\n\\n\\"I shall be easier now. You will notice that. Everybody notices that I am easier afterwards.\\"\\n\\nShe puts it across her knees.\\n\\n\\"Goodnight.\\"","on_enter":[{"quest_stage":{"quest":"the_nineteen","stage":"unsaid"}}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"Goodnight.\\"","end":true}]}],"camp_yrsa_fate":[{"conditions":[{"flag":"yrsa_fate","equals":"told"}],"expression":2,"text":"\\"Better,\\" says Yrsa. \\"Not fixed. Better.\\"\\n\\nShe feeds the fire.\\n\\n\\"I have said her name eleven times this week and nobody has flinched and I have stopped counting whether they flinch, which is the actual improvement.\\"\\n\\nShe looks over.\\n\\n\\"And I have started thinking about the door. Orre''s door. Which I could not do before, because there was a girl standing in a road in front of it.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_yrsa_fate"},{"approval":{"yrsa":6}}],"choices":[{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]},{"expression":3,"text":"\\"Fine,\\" says Yrsa, and it is the well-rehearsed one.\\n\\nThen she stops, and looks at the fire, and does something she has not done.\\n\\n\\"That is not true and you would know if you had not agreed to let me off. Which you did. Which I asked for.\\"\\n\\nShe shrugs.\\n\\n\\"I am all right. I would like it noted that I got here by being let off, and that I am not sure that is the same as getting here.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_yrsa_fate"},{"approval":{"yrsa":6}}],"choices":[{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]}],"romance_one":[{"expression":5,"text":"\\"Right,\\" says Yrsa, and puts the bow down, and sits back on her heels.\\n\\n\\"The thing I have not been asking. Yes. All right.\\"\\n\\nShe looks at the dark rather than at you, which is where she looks when it matters.\\n\\n\\"I am nineteen and every single person who has ever wanted anything from me has wanted to look after me. That is not a complaint about them, it is a description of me — I arrived here at fourteen at the end of eighty-five miles and I have been a thing that gets looked after ever since, and it turns out that is extremely difficult to stop being.\\"\\n\\nShe glances over.\\n\\n\\"You put me in the front of a fight last month. Without discussing it. Without checking afterwards whether I was all right about it.\\"\\n\\n\\"Nobody has done that in four years. I have thought about it every day since and I am not going to pretend I have not.\\"","on_enter":[{"romance":{"companion":"yrsa","stage":1}},{"approval":{"yrsa":5}}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"You are the best shot I have got. That is the whole of it.\\"","next":"romance_one_answer"},{"label":"\\"I will keep doing it.\\"","next":"romance_one_answer"},{"label":"\\"Get some sleep.\\"","next":"camp"}]}],"romance_one_answer":[{"expression":2,"text":"\\"Good,\\" says Yrsa.\\n\\nShe picks the bow back up, which is what she does with her hands, and does not string it.\\n\\n\\"Do not be nice to me about this. I will know, and I have got very fast at spotting it, and it will finish the whole thing inside a week.\\"\\n\\nShe puts another stick on the fire.\\n\\n\\"Sit there. You do not have to say anything. That is the entire offer.\\"","choices":[{"label":"Sit.","end":true}]}],"romance_two":[{"expression":2,"text":"She does not move over, exactly. She stops sitting where she can see the dark, and sits where she can see the fire, and lets you be the one facing out.\\n\\nIt is the largest thing she has ever done and neither of you says a word about it.\\n\\n\\"Wake me at the turn,\\" she says, after a while. \\"Do not let me sleep through. I hate being let sleep through.\\"","on_enter":[{"romance":{"companion":"yrsa","stage":2}}],"choices":[{"label":"\\"At the turn.\\"","end":true}]}],"camp_yrsa_the_mark":[{"expression":6,"text":"Yrsa is quiet for a while.\\n\\n\\"She told you,\\" she says. \\"Out loud. To a stranger.\\"\\n\\nShe puts a stick on the fire.\\n\\n\\"Nobody in the Wardenswatch has ever been told what that mark means. Forty people out there have been looking at a sheet of paper they have never seen, for four years, and being kind to the woman carrying it.\\"\\n\\nShe wraps her arms round her knees.\\n\\n\\"And she said it to you in an afternoon.\\"\\n\\nShe is not angry. It takes you a while to work out what she is, and it is something more like relief.\\n\\n\\"Good,\\" she says at last. \\"Good. That is one of us out from under it. It was always going to have to be her first.\\"","on_enter":[{"set_flag":"camp_yrsa_the_mark"},{"approval":{"yrsa":6}}],"choices":[{"label":"Back to the fire.","next":"camp"}]}]}}',0,0,0,NULL)
